window.WIKI_INDEX=[{"t":"Abasse The Seer","u":"wiki/abasse-the-seer.html","a":["Abasse","The Seer"],"f":"Characters","c":"#c9671b","s":"A seer of the Abunali in Dunis, about sixty-seven years old, whose visions have driven her all her life. She tells people of their fates bluntly, and her erratic manner has most of the village misjudging her. Her twin sister, Shaman Ankti, holds temporary elde","b":"A seer of the Abunali in Dunis, about sixty-seven years old, whose visions have driven her all her life. She tells people of their fates bluntly, and her erratic manner has most of the village misjudging her. Her twin sister, Shaman Ankti, holds temporary eldership over Dunis, and the two don't acknowledge each other unless they have to. Abasse's head is shaven, her eyes are of two colors, and her white ispaa follow no coherent design. She has visions, and tells people of their fates bluntly, whatever it may make them feel. Abasse and Ankti grew up in very strained familial circumstances. Their mother was very depressed due to the loss of their father, and often took the girls to an old temple in Ashla Sanctuary where they would engage in ritualistic meditation sessions, often dedicated to finding answers from the gods or otherworldly spirits. While Ankti grew cold and distant toward their mother, choosing to pursue her ambitions of becoming a kashif, Abasse grew closer. Not emotionally, but rather they shared a common interest in divination, and unlike her mother, Abasse possessed a special sight. Visions that they both presumed to be a gift from the gods, for she could see beyond the tangible. After the passing of their mother, Ankti felt an overwhelming amount of guilt for not being as present in neither her mother's or sister's lives. She attempted to reconnect with Abasse, and in doing so, the two decided that connecting with their spiritual selves was necessary. Though, this wouldn't last long, as a wedge had been driven between them due to their inability to connect emotionally. While it's true that both sisters weren't the emotional type, Abasse was especially distant in comparison. Talking dispassionately about their mother and seeing no point in feeling sorrow. She often went on scatterbrained tangents regarding the afterlife, bluntly telling people about their fates, regardless of how it may make them feel. While Ankti sought to give people guidance through the gods, Abasse often undermined the gods. In recent years, the two don't ever acknowledge each others existence unless they explicitly have to, despite maintaining similar roles and living within the same village of Dunis.","n":""},{"t":"Adonis","u":"wiki/adonis.html","a":["Lord Steward Adonis","Lord Steward"],"f":"Characters","c":"#a8860b","s":"A Lord Steward of about thirty, meticulous in his work and fond of his pleasures, who hopes to retire wealthy.","b":"A Lord Steward of about thirty, meticulous in his work and fond of his pleasures, who hopes to retire wealthy.","n":""},{"t":"Adrien","u":"wiki/adrien.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Aeleina Zuul","u":"wiki/aeleina-zuul.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Lady of Wietmoire, and sister of the High King, Alfor Zuul. Daughter of Aethel Zuul and Catalina Valentyre, she never married, and keeps Wietmoire Manor, where the monarchs gather every year on their way to the Royal Conclave. King Onfroi Talvo, whom she w","b":"The Lady of Wietmoire, and sister of the High King, Alfor Zuul. Daughter of Aethel Zuul and Catalina Valentyre, she never married, and keeps Wietmoire Manor, where the monarchs gather every year on their way to the Royal Conclave. King Onfroi Talvo, whom she was once offered to in marriage, has become her friend. Aeleina has the black hair and blue eyes of her house, as her brother Tybal and her grandfather Anteo did. Her father offered her in marriage to Onfroi Talvo, and was refused. See The Crowning of Onfroi. Aethel gave her Wietmoire Manor, putting his kindest child where the Talvos would have to pass every year. It's the last of his mending still standing. With it she holds the township of Wietmoire, and for it she owes fealty to her kinsman Severin Larrensire, the Prince of Moirach, a sore point between their houses. She never married, and keeps the manor, where the monarchs gather every year for the Royal Conclave. Onfroi sleeps under the roof where his family was killed, and the woman he was offered pours his wine. The two have become friends, carefully. One year, she told him that her father had waited for a letter from him, asking for help against Nirva, and would've sent everything he had. She didn't know what she was telling him.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Aethel Zuul","u":"wiki/aethel-zuul.html","a":["Zuul","High King"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The third High King of the Bicontinental Union, dead by 211 ABU. Second born son of Endelyn Larrensire and the second High King, Anteo Zuul, husband of Catalina Valentyre, and father of Tybal, Aeleina, and the current High King, Alfor Zuul. He sought to undo h","b":"The third High King of the Bicontinental Union, dead by 211 ABU. Second born son of Endelyn Larrensire and the second High King, Anteo Zuul, husband of Catalina Valentyre, and father of Tybal, Aeleina, and the current High King, Alfor Zuul. He sought to undo his father's harm where he could, and made a king of Onfroi Talvo, whose family his father had destroyed. Aethel took after his mother. He had long, flowing, black hair streaked with bronze, which reached the middle of his back, eyes of a deep violet that was closer to red, and a large burn on his left shoulder and neck. He was always thin, and grew tall with age as she had. His features were lean, and sharper than his father's, who was much more broadly built, as the Uprorians are. Many in the realm referred to him as 'pretty' as opposed to handsome, though he was far from unattractive, and was suspected to have several paramours. It was only rumor, based on the way he acted. His way of speaking was softer than his father's, almost flirtatious, and he'd sooner spar with his words than raise his voice. He enjoyed attending feasts and tourneys, fraternizing with nobles across the realms, exchanging tales, and tasting the local wine and, as people told it, the women, wherever he went. He was intelligent, brave, and forgiving, but indecisive. He wanted to earn his father's respect. He took no pleasure in being flattered, but didn't dislike being obeyed. Aethel wasn't born first in line. His elder brother, Archon, died fighting the titans (see The Death of Archon), and the child Archon's wife bore after it was disinherited. As a boy, he'd watched his newborn brother struggle to breathe, and was told not to look (see Endelyn Larrensire). He was in the room when his father killed Gideon and Kaelyn, and did nothing (see The Massacre at Wietmoire). Out of grief, Aethel refused to enter the garden in High Sonham where Endelyn is entombed, and ordered servants to keep it maintained for her memory. When his father choked, only Aethel was in the hall with him, and he did nothing. A young Alfor heard the panicked rushing of servants, and came in to find his father sitting unbothered, sipping his wine as they tried to save Anteo. Aethel watched through the edge of his brow, quietly humming to himself. Tybal was at court in Karlaine, and Aeleina was with her mother at Wietmoire. Anteo had never abdicated, so the High Throne passed to Aethel. The year of his father's death, Cedric Talvo, whose family had been killed on an order people knew was Anteo's (see The Massacre at Wietmoire), came before him in Karlaine. The two had known one another years before. Aethel meant to mend the feud and offered his daughter to Cedric's son, Onfroi. When Cedric kept an older promise instead, Aethel respected him for it, and made Onfroi a king. See The Crowning of Onfroi. Afterward, alone with Cedric, he told him that Gideon hadn't knelt, and that he himself had stood there. After Nirva took Tybal and closed Talvar's bay, he waited for a letter from Onfroi asking for help, and would've sent everything he had. It never came. As High King, Aethel wanted to understand how many had survived his father's purge (see Ten-Year War), and had a jotuun survivor brought to him as a prisoner. Believing the Zuuls to want all jotuun gone, the prisoner feared being already dead, and attacked. It was a cornered being's fear that gave Aethel his scar. The jotuun was executed for it, against Aethel's wishes. The prisoner was killed by an Inquisitor of Zuul's Hand, but not before giving up where the jotuun holdouts were. Aethel reprimanded the Inquisitor, but couldn't deny the results, and acted to find them. He found Ayala, Augmen's daughter, hiding out with seven other jotuun in the ancient ruined city of Qis. She asked why she shouldn't kill him where he stood. \"Because I am not my father,\" he answered. He earned her trust, and over several years they grew much closer. They always met in private, as she'd be recognized at once. The two fell in love, though it could never have been pursued. It ended when Qis was found, through no doing of his, and Ayala came to heavily mistrust and actively hate him. What it cost his wife is told in Catalina Valentyre. She'd confessed to him, and he told no one. He let Ayala hate him rather than his wife, and let Alfor blame Ayala rather than his mother. Alfor had the Hand kill her for it, and Aethel learned of it when only one came back. He'd read the letter that Dendrilla Ariadne had written to his father, and known that he sat on his nephew's throne. He wrote back to her, telling her it was never her fault, and he would also send money. It's how Alexium came to be well off. His reply ended with a request, that she keep the truth from her son. As long as Alexium didn't know, nobody could make him a claimant. Her letter was among his things when he died. He died by Alfor's hand. Alfor visited him as he sat in front of the hearth, staring into the flame, deep in thought, and spoke to him for several minutes, already having decided his father's fate. Aethel was quiet. He was unhappy with his life, and had already made peace with himself. Though, he apologized to Alfor, never having wanted his son's upbringing to be like his own. For as long as he remembered, Alfor had believed his father to be weaker than his grandfather, and he was taken aback. He didn't make it known, but there was a sudden sense of guilt for what he was about to do, and yet, he continued. Finally, he asked his father if he would like some tea, and Aethel accepted. He poured him a cup of Varrow's Leaf tea, and they sat by the hearth, staring at it in silence until Aethel shut his eyes and didn't open them. There was no struggle, and no choking. Aethel succumbed to the same fate as his mother, but unlike her, he died wearing a slight grin. He'd known what was in the cup. He found peace. \"Because I am not my father.\" — to Ayala, in Qis \"Our families' histories have been negatively connected for generations. I intend to mend that.\" — to Cedric Talvo, in Karlaine \"I respect a man of honor more than a man of desperation.\" — to Cedric Talvo, in Karlaine","n":""},{"t":"Afina","u":"wiki/afina.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#c9671b","s":"An orphan raised in Kama by Midori, six years old when she first appears. She thinks of Artemis as her older brother and wants to be like him, though it's she who's asked to keep him out of trouble. Afina speaks her mind, and isn't easily sent away. According ","b":"An orphan raised in Kama by Midori, six years old when she first appears. She thinks of Artemis as her older brother and wants to be like him, though it's she who's asked to keep him out of trouble. Afina speaks her mind, and isn't easily sent away. According to Artemis, she's a decent actor but a terrible liar. She has quite the appetite, and can sniff out a sweet vex roast from down the hall. When Midori was away from the orphanage, it was Afina she asked to watch over Artemis and keep him from stealing, though Afina was the younger of the two. In time, she began to do things on her own, such as slipping away to earn tosis as a surprise for him. She left Kama with Midori, Artemis and Azra, and they settled in a hut in the trees of Khamu. She was ten when she took part in the Kashif Trials, and her group was the first to finish the opening trial. In the hunting trials, she was placed under Marshal Akkennoten, whom she admired. When he had her shoot at a dummy of a tulkai, she struck its heart. She had been aiming for its eye. She grew tired of Artemis watching over her every move, and told him she wasn't a little kid anymore. \"We don't leave each other.\" — to Artemis, during the trials","n":""},{"t":"Aila","u":"wiki/aila.html","a":["Aila Dueval"],"f":"Characters","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"A twenty-year-old of the Dueval Family, and daughter of its last queen, Aissa. With her family fallen, she means to become the new leader of the criminal underground.","b":"A twenty-year-old of the Dueval Family, and daughter of its last queen, Aissa. With her family fallen, she means to become the new leader of the criminal underground.","n":""},{"t":"Aiore","u":"wiki/aiore.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#2f8a55","s":"The late queen of Phylomina, wife of King Alexium Ariadne and mother of Queen Vaenera and Azra. Born in the Anklan Isles and held in high esteem among the Abunali Islanders, she died about a year after her husband, and by 211 ABU her elder daughter rules in th","b":"The late queen of Phylomina, wife of King Alexium Ariadne and mother of Queen Vaenera and Azra. Born in the Anklan Isles and held in high esteem among the Abunali Islanders, she died about a year after her husband, and by 211 ABU her elder daughter rules in their place. Aiore was gentle, yet very headstrong. She was royalty of sorts in the Anklan Isles, held with high esteem among the Abunali Islanders, and desired by many men. She met Kade Asher when he made landfall on Tabla Pise, but had accepted Alexium's proposal and borne a child by the time he'd left his crew. A couple of years later, she returned to Morowai, found Kade caring for her elderly mother, and fell in love with him all over again. Azra was born of the affair. Guilt-ridden, she eventually told Alexium the truth, and chose to leave with Azra rather than give up Kade's whereabouts. Alexium refused to let her go, and sent Azra away. See Vaenera & Azra's Origin. In the years after Malu was renamed, she made a hobby of foraging from the local jungles with Vaenera. On one of those days she reached into a bush, and took hold of something she shouldn't have brought home. See The Fall of Malu. After Morowai was burned, Aiore went looking for Azra and her mother. She did not find Azra, but she found her mother in what little was left of her abode, curled in the fetal position, ash and bone, clutching Azra's toy. She came back with it, gave it to Alexium without a word, and never spoke to him again. She outlived Alexium by about a year, and hers is among the losses that Vaenera blames herself for. It's when Vaenera first began to lean on Alfor Zuul. Vaenera & Azra's Origin","n":""},{"t":"Aissa","u":"wiki/aissa.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The last queen of the Dueval Family, and mother of Aila.","b":"The last queen of the Dueval Family, and mother of Aila.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Akir","u":"wiki/akir.html","a":["Akir the Mighty"],"f":"Characters","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"A quiet and courageous man of about thirty-eight, found at the stables of Pa'iq, who means to protect Tanzu at all costs.","b":"A quiet and courageous man of about thirty-eight, found at the stables of Pa'iq, who means to protect Tanzu at all costs.","n":""},{"t":"Alexium Ariadne","u":"wiki/alexium-ariadne.html","a":["King Alexium Ariadne","King Alexium","Alexium","King"],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The late king of Phylomina, a smuggler captain who drove a dra'iq from the city when it was still called Malu, and was asked by its dying king to take his place. Son of Dendrilla Ariadne, who was cast out by the Zuuls over his birth, husband of Aiore, and fath","b":"The late king of Phylomina, a smuggler captain who drove a dra'iq from the city when it was still called Malu, and was asked by its dying king to take his place. Son of Dendrilla Ariadne, who was cast out by the Zuuls over his birth, husband of Aiore, and father of Queen Vaenera. He died of a fall from his palace into the bay, which people take for his own doing, and Vaenera rules after him. Alexium had reddish brown hair and dark eyes. He'd had to question who his father was his whole life, and it made him exceptionally curious. The second born son of Dendrilla and Prince Archon Zuul, he was born after Archon's death, with reddish brown hair and dark eyes. People did not believe he was Archon's child, and Anteo Zuul disinherited him. He was Archon's son. Alexium was already a successful man, well off because of money that Aethel Zuul sent, when he asked for Aiore's hand on Tabla Pise, and she accepted. He never bothered to get to know her family. He and his crew drove a dra'iq from Malu with their vessels, and the dying King Arabah, his longtime friend, pleaded with him to take his place as ruler. He restored the city, gave it a new name, and kept a secret in his palace for its sake. See The Fall of Malu. He raised Azra as his own until Aiore confessed that Kade Asher was her father. When Aiore chose to leave with her rather than give up Kade, he refused to let his wife go, and cast the child out instead. See Vaenera & Azra's Origin. When he heard that Morowai had burned, he felt exceptionally guilty, believing that he'd sent Azra to her doom. She'd grown up thinking that her own family hated her, especially him. He'd known of his birth in small part, from how his mother reacted whenever there was news of the Zuuls. It was only after Morowai that it was confirmed, when word came from overseas that Dendrilla had died. Her belongings were sent to him, and her family had intentionally stored the proof with the rest of her things: Aethel's reply (see Dendrilla Ariadne). It was a quiet and disappointing revelation, both in himself for casting Azra out, and in being related to such a detestable man as Anteo. His guilt was compounded by the parallels to how Anteo had treated him when he was born. Then he questioned why his mother and her family had hidden the truth from him. If rumor had begun to spread, Anteo, notorious for his violent nature, would have acted on it. Turning Dendrilla out had been, in truth, a small mercy. After Anteo's death, she kept it hidden because Aethel had asked her to. Alexium kept the reply in his storage beside Azra's toy (see Aiore). He died of a fall from his palace into the bay. Having seen him grieve after Morowai, people took it for his own doing. Alfor Zuul was behind it. He'd found Dendrilla's letter, and knew that a man with a better claim than his own sat on a throne, and it was one of Zuul's Hand who saw to the fall. Vaenera succeeded him, and rules Phylomina as of 211 ABU. Vaenera & Azra's Origin","n":""},{"t":"Alfor Zuul","u":"wiki/alfor-zuul.html","a":["Zuul","High King"],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The fourth and current High King of the Bicontinental Union, seventy-eight years old, and the grandson of Anteo Zuul. He took the High Throne after his father, Aethel Zuul, and has since become a recluse in High Sonham, a seat whose location the realms don't k","b":"The fourth and current High King of the Bicontinental Union, seventy-eight years old, and the grandson of Anteo Zuul. He took the High Throne after his father, Aethel Zuul, and has since become a recluse in High Sonham, a seat whose location the realms don't know. He keeps about four courtiers, who travel at his behest to any ruler's court, and hosts the monarchs once a year at the Royal Conclave. He has no heir. He's raised a ward, Kiro, and mentors the young Queen Vaenera of Phylomina. Charming and detached, he plays with the realms from a distance, and lets Talvar suffer Nirva without his aid. Alfor has black hair with patches of grey, and eyes of a faded red. Much like his father, he's thin, and when he was younger he was considered to be handsome as well. Late in life, he's become reclusive and let himself go, and his claws and hair grow unkempt. He kept his hair medium-length for a while, before he let it grow down to his legs. He has two extra fingers on his hand, near concealed beneath his sleeve. They're rumored of, and the rumor is true. He's charming, observant, and strategically playful, with a cruelty that comes easily to a man so detached from empathy. He takes after his father's way of speaking, and enjoys sparring with his words rather than raising his voice. He manipulates from a distance, and doesn't let himself be tied to the outcome. He was young when his grandfather died. He heard the panicked rushing of servants, and entered the hall to find his father sitting unbothered, sipping his wine and quietly humming to himself, as they tried to save Anteo. It was the only time he ever admired his father, and then came Karlaine. He stood beside his father, and watched, when Aethel made a king of Onfroi (see The Crowning of Onfroi). He saw a crown given away for an honest answer, and thought it the weakest thing he'd ever watched a king do. It's where his contempt for Aethel and for Talvar began. It was on a mission to mend their family's feud with the Talvos that Nirva took his brother. Aethel had proposed it, and it was discussed among the household. Alfor rolled his eyes, and views the mission as foolish. He probed his brother on it in private, and refused when Tybal invited him along, feeling as though he wasn't being heard by either his brother or his father (see Tybal Zuul). He hates Ayala. He believes that his father loved her more than his mother, and that their closeness drove Catalina Valentyre to her death. He's often wondered if Ayala bore an illegitimate child. As a prince, under his father's roof, he sent all seven of Zuul's Hand to kill her. Aethel died by Alfor's hand, of a cup of Varrow's Leaf tea (see Aethel Zuul). Alfor had always believed his father to be weaker than his grandfather, until Aethel apologized to him that night. He felt guilt for what he was about to do, and did it. He suspects that his father knew. The grin has never left him, and he's never been able to decide if his father was forgiving him or had won. His elder brother, Tybal, died before their father, so the High Throne passed to Alfor, and with it the powers once granted to Anteo. He holds the Royal Conclave each year at High Sonham, and keeps about four courtiers, who travel at his behest to any ruler's court. King Lanis Kalmere is outwardly loyal to him. Alexium Ariadne sent word to him for the armaments to defend his city from a dra'iq. See The Fall of Malu. King Onfroi Talvo has spent years appealing to him for aid against Nirva. He dismisses the pleas, viewing Talvar's suffering as the consequence of its founder's defiance. He knows that Onfroi has done nothing wrong, and isn't trying to villainize him, but rather to protect his family's elite status by refusing to fraternize with a house he believes to be lesser. He believes that the Zuuls owe the Talvos nothing, and that both families earned their places in the world, right of conquest always having been a widely accepted principle on Artisans. At the Royal Conclave, in answer to one of Onfroi's pleas, he coldly jested that perhaps Nirva swallowing his brother whole was an unfortunate sign from the gods. He seldom visits the garden of his grandmother, Endelyn Larrensire, in High Sonham, but goes there to quiet his mind. He doesn't tend its plants. He's raised and nurtured Kiro throughout almost the entirety of the boy's life, and educated him personally. Kiro refuses to question any order he's given. Alfor had the boy's jotuun guardian, Theara, executed, and spared him to mold him, which Kiro doesn't know. He keeps the secret of The Vessel, and intends to learn everything it holds. Alfor is infertile, and cannot have a legitimate heir. This is why he's taken on a mentor-like relationship with Queen Vaenera, who doesn't know her relation to the Zuul bloodline. He knows of it from a letter that Dendrilla Ariadne wrote to Anteo, which he found among Aethel's things after killing him. He was behind the death of her father, Alexium Ariadne, which left a young queen in need of a mentor.","n":""},{"t":"Alibet","u":"wiki/alibet.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The mother of Ayala, whom she had with Augmen. Nothing's known of her after she left them. She left them both within days of the birth. She didn't want to keep a white-haired child.","b":"The mother of Ayala, whom she had with Augmen. Nothing's known of her after she left them. She left them both within days of the birth. She didn't want to keep a white-haired child.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Alora 'Tanzu' Rhea","u":"wiki/alora-tanzu-rhea.html","a":["Tanzu","Tanzu the Great"],"f":"Characters","c":"#a8860b","s":"A twelve-year-old who works the stables of Pa'iq, goes by Tanzu the Great, and wants to leave home and live free. The name is taken from the Kashif Marshal T'Anzu, a hero to the child since he deterred an occi away from the town. Akir is never far off.","b":"A twelve-year-old who works the stables of Pa'iq, goes by Tanzu the Great, and wants to leave home and live free. The name is taken from the Kashif Marshal T'Anzu, a hero to the child since he deterred an occi away from the town. Akir is never far off.","n":""},{"t":"Alusha the Vengeful","u":"wiki/alusha-the-vengeful.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The woman who waged war on the entire groll population of Abu, known for the Athuran Groll Wastings. She's long gone by 211 ABU, but her message still echoes in the heads of both atlae and groll. After her children were mutilated by members of a groll tribe in","b":"The woman who waged war on the entire groll population of Abu, known for the Athuran Groll Wastings. She's long gone by 211 ABU, but her message still echoes in the heads of both atlae and groll. After her children were mutilated by members of a groll tribe in Sundiri, Alusha rallied Abu to her cause, and within a decade its native grolls were eradicated. When the jotuun had grolls accepted back into Athuran society, she rose once again, baiting groll tribes into staged, public killings to prove what she'd always claimed of them. See Athuran Groll Wastings. Her message remains echoing in the heads of both atlae and groll.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Amae Talvo","u":"wiki/amae-talvo.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A daughter of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo of Talvar, and elder sister of Koto. She's married to a lord in Athura.","b":"A daughter of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo of Talvar, and elder sister of Koto. She's married to a lord in Athura.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Amori","u":"wiki/amori.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A child of the Abunali in Khamu who hopes to become a kashif, and Azra's childhood friend. Amori is gentle, and quick to smooth over a quarrel. Since the Kashif Trials, he bears the mark of Isi: a horizontal stripe on each cheek, and a more detailed line from ","b":"A child of the Abunali in Khamu who hopes to become a kashif, and Azra's childhood friend. Amori is gentle, and quick to smooth over a quarrel. Since the Kashif Trials, he bears the mark of Isi: a horizontal stripe on each cheek, and a more detailed line from his forehead to the bridge of his nose. A child of the Abunali, he befriended Azra during her stay in Khamu. He had no partner he trusted for the trials, so she promised to hunt with him, and the two were grouped with Artemis and Afina. Marshal T'Akoteh knew him by name, much to his delight. Theirs was the first group to finish the opening trial. When the groups were reformed, he and Azra joined another, and he told Artemis and Afina it wasn't goodbye. Elder Azhotep anointed him for his comradery and compassion. \"Guys, this isn't goodbye. We'll see each other again.\" — to Artemis and Afina","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Aniketh","u":"wiki/aniketh.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"A friend of Augmen since childhood, whose family raised Augmen's daughter, Ayala, on their farm. He joined the jotuun alongside Augmen, and died before the Ten-Year War. When he found Augmen caring for a newborn on his own, the two of them brought the child, A","b":"A friend of Augmen since childhood, whose family raised Augmen's daughter, Ayala, on their farm. He joined the jotuun alongside Augmen, and died before the Ten-Year War. When he found Augmen caring for a newborn on his own, the two of them brought the child, Ayala, to his family's large farm. He left for the order with Augmen once they'd spoken with his mother, and they went on several missions as neophytes before they came back. On the night of his expulsion, Augmen came back for the forbidden texts, and Aniketh was keeping watch. He stood in Augmen's way, too selfless to step aside. It was an accident, with a power Augmen had only ever practiced. He was the first person Augmen killed that he'd loved, and after it, there was nothing left for Augmen to go back to.","n":""},{"t":"Ano","u":"wiki/ano.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"An orphan raised in Kama by Midori, older than Artemis and his tormentor. He mocks him as \"Little Oiyas\" with his friends, and claims that Artemis hurts everyone around him. With his friends, he would mock Artemis through his bedroom door, calling him \"Little ","b":"An orphan raised in Kama by Midori, older than Artemis and his tormentor. He mocks him as \"Little Oiyas\" with his friends, and claims that Artemis hurts everyone around him. With his friends, he would mock Artemis through his bedroom door, calling him \"Little Oiyas\" and promising to eat his share of dinner. It ended, at least once, with a door to the toes. He met Artemis again in Khamu, at the Kashif Trials, where he was the oldest of a group under Marshal Akkennoten. The two were made to group together, and he warned Aurel and Arya against him, claiming that Artemis hurts everyone around him. At practice, his arrow took a dummy of a tulkai through the neck, and Akkennoten praised it as swift and clean.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Anteo Zuul","u":"wiki/anteo-zuul.html","a":["Zuul","High King"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The second High King of the Bicontinental Union, and the first Zuul to hold the High Throne. Long dead by 211 ABU, he's remembered as a tyrant, for the Ten-Year War, in which nearly all jotuun were destroyed, and for The Massacre at Wietmoire, which it's an op","b":"The second High King of the Bicontinental Union, and the first Zuul to hold the High Throne. Long dead by 211 ABU, he's remembered as a tyrant, for the Ten-Year War, in which nearly all jotuun were destroyed, and for The Massacre at Wietmoire, which it's an open secret that he was behind. He was the husband of Endelyn Larrensire, and father of Archon and Aethel. The powers he was granted were never relinquished, and his grandson, Alfor Zuul, holds them now. Anteo was pale and broadly built, as the Uprorians are, with the black hair and blue eyes of his house. He wore his hair long. He was cold and unforgiving, with little patience for those who took their time in telling him something, and he bore humiliation poorly. He was as petty as he could realistically be. His failure against Oprax agitated him to no end. Anteo was the son of Tybal Zuul I, who had him late in life and died in battle. He was raised briefly by his grandfather, Aevor Zuul, who had been head of the house when Talvar fell (see The Fall of Talvar), until Aevor himself died of a burst belly. Anteo was voted in after Tarris Kalmere, and was granted emergency powers by the rulers who joined the Union. He heavily abused them, securing succession rights for his children, and influenced those beneath him to call it the Zuul Union. He tried to force Oprax into the Union, and it simply waited him out behind its wall. His predecessor had taken Ōnari and Kabejō Garrison from it, which alone was enough to enrage him. See Anteo's Failure. Anteo held the title of Duke of Tuvar, and no hilltribe had ever brought him a feud. He'd taken it for wildness, until he heard that they'd all been brought to someone: Gideon. It's an open secret that Anteo was behind The Massacre at Wietmoire. He had the Talvos killed in their family home and seized it. King Gideon Talvo walked into High Sonham alone to face him. Anteo offered him his wife's life if he knelt, and was refused. Knowing that Gideon would win a duel, he had his men force him down, and beheaded him. He beat and strangled Kaelyn to death in the same room, and had them both thrown into the ravine. He wasn't the honorable type to bury his dead enemies. Endelyn chose the wives of both their sons, Dendrilla Ariadne for Archon and Catalina Valentyre for Aethel. Anteo wanted a Kalmere for Archon, and never liked either choice, but he didn't have the patience for it to suggest any further candidates. When his eldest was killed by a titan, Anteo prepared for war within the day. He overpowered the titans in the forests between Upror and Morgar, but the jotuun intervened before he could have them slain, and he begrudgingly retreated from an army of them. See The Death of Archon. Archon's wife, Dendrilla Ariadne, gave birth after his death. Anteo took one look at the babe, Alexium, and denied that he was a Zuul. He disinherited the child and kicked Dendrilla out of Wietmoire. She wrote to him after, and he kept what she wrote as proof against her. Archon's death left Endelyn in a depression she never recovered from, and the leaves she ate in her garden in hope of happiness killed her. Anteo pressured her caretakers to save her. He had her entombed beneath the tree that killed her, in a garden he hated for it and loved as she had, and came to visit it frequently. See Endelyn Larrensire. With the ex-jotuun Augmen as his enforcer, Anteo waged a war of extermination on the jotuun, devised by the two of them in secret, from the siege of Ancrage to that of the Temple of Isi. He was deprived of Augmen by the end of it, but reveled in a near-total victory. See Ten-Year War. Anteo never abdicated. Long after Endelyn's death, he choked to death. Servants tried to save him while Aethel sat and sipped his wine, and Alfor came in to see it (see Aethel Zuul). The High Throne passed to Aethel. The High Sovereign was never made to relinquish the powers granted to Anteo, and a Zuul still sits on the High Throne as of 211 ABU. Many stadians lost family in his fruitless war with Oprax, and turned on the Opraxi refugees who came after. \"That is no Zuul.\" — to Dendrilla Ariadne, on seeing her newborn son","n":""},{"t":"Archon Zuul","u":"wiki/archon-zuul.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The prince who would have followed his father, Anteo Zuul, to the High Throne, had he not died fighting the titans. First born son of Anteo and Endelyn Larrensire, and husband of Dendrilla Ariadne, he's remembered for the arrogance that led him from one battle","b":"The prince who would have followed his father, Anteo Zuul, to the High Throne, had he not died fighting the titans. First born son of Anteo and Endelyn Larrensire, and husband of Dendrilla Ariadne, he's remembered for the arrogance that led him from one battle to another, and for the war his death set off. Archon had black hair streaked with bronze, and his father's blue eyes. He grew stubble earlier than most of his family, and many women considered him handsome. Throughout his life, Archon's arrogance and pride led him from one battle to another, winning most and fearing little. His father wanted him to marry a Kalmere, but at the time, the only choice was a girl twelve years younger than him, Oriane Kalmere. Archon married his mother's choice, Dendrilla Ariadne, who stayed at Wietmoire Manor after it was taken from the Talvos (see The Massacre at Wietmoire). Their firstborn son, Haylor, was a stillbirth. He rushed into battle to aid his friends in Stade, and lost one of them, Sabba, to a titan. He sought vengeance on it and killed the wrong one. With Joras, he set out to stop the titans his act had provoked from reaching Stade, and only then learned what fear was. He was killed trying to flee. See The Death of Archon. His wife was pregnant when he died, and people did not believe the child, Alexium, was his. With Alexium disinherited, the succession fell to his brother, Aethel.","n":""},{"t":"Arin","u":"wiki/arin.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#a8860b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Artemis","u":"wiki/artemis.html","a":["Artemis","Arty"],"f":"Characters","c":"#c9671b","s":"A nineteen-year-old orphan of Kama, raised by Midori, who travels with his closest friend, Azlan. His white hair has made people wary of him all his life, and he bears the mark of a kashif. Loud, carefree, and too arrogant for his own good, he means to make th","b":"A nineteen-year-old orphan of Kama, raised by Midori, who travels with his closest friend, Azlan. His white hair has made people wary of him all his life, and he bears the mark of a kashif. Loud, carefree, and too arrogant for his own good, he means to make the most out of life and to become powerful by any means necessary. He was raised in Kama by Midori, alongside Afina and, later, Azra. His white hair set him apart from an early age, and other children called him the \"seed of Oiyas.\" He took part in the Kashif Trials in Khamu, and bears the mark of a kashif. He later fell in with Tibs' gang. Vaenera & Azra's Origin","n":""},{"t":"Arya","u":"wiki/arya.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A girl of the southern Abunali tribes, and the younger sister of Aurel. When she's introduced, the two have come to Khamu to take part in the Kashif Trials. Arya would sooner see a thing for herself than take another's word for it. She knows her gods, which is","b":"A girl of the southern Abunali tribes, and the younger sister of Aurel. When she's introduced, the two have come to Khamu to take part in the Kashif Trials. Arya would sooner see a thing for herself than take another's word for it. She knows her gods, which is more than can be said for her brother. When the groups of the Kashif Trials were reformed, it was Arya who asked Artemis and Afina to join hers, over Ano's objections. She had heard the rumors about Artemis and asked Afina whether they were true. Afterward, she defended him to Ano and Aurel, and stepped between them when they fought. \"Our marshal is in danger, and all you three can do is fight one another?\" — to Artemis, Ano and Aurel","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Augmen","u":"wiki/augmen.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#c9671b","s":"An ex-jotuun who became Anteo Zuul's enforcer, and broke the order's temples for him in the Ten-Year War. Once apprentice to Prior Ad Regalis, and father of Ayala, he died at the war's end. As of 211 ABU his stone spires still stand in Ancrage, and the zealots","b":"An ex-jotuun who became Anteo Zuul's enforcer, and broke the order's temples for him in the Ten-Year War. Once apprentice to Prior Ad Regalis, and father of Ayala, he died at the war's end. As of 211 ABU his stone spires still stand in Ancrage, and the zealots of Neru'Augma mean to bring him back from the dead. Augmen had short, curly, tawny hair, orange eyes lined with kohl, and jagged stripes on both cheeks. He was carefree and cocky, with a short fuse, and cared far too much about how he was seen. Once he got going, his determination was relentless. Augmen and Alibet had a daughter, Ayala, who was born with white hair. Alibet didn't want to keep her, but Augmen loved her regardless. A few days later, he awoke to find that Alibet had run off, leaving the two of them alone with nothing. Aniketh, his friend since childhood, came to visit and found him caring for the child by himself. They decided it was best to take her to Aniketh's family farm, where they felt she'd have a great upbringing. After speaking with Aniketh's mother, the two men joined the jotuun, both in desperate need of purpose. They'd wanted to be jotuun since childhood, and the farm was large, with plenty of people to look after Ayala, so they felt justified in leaving her there. They went on several missions as neophytes, and returned when she was four. Augmen found his daughter socially inept for her age, and quickly learned why (see Ayala). He decided to take her with him, believing that the jotuun could offer her a better upbringing. He struggled the whole way through his training, while she surpassed all of her peers. Before his expulsion, he was the apprentice of Prior Ad Regalis among the jotuun when Endelyn Larrensire accompanied them to the battle with the titans. See The Death of Archon. When Ayala was offered a place at the Temple of Isi, Augmen disapproved, and made it very vocal, despite the warnings of Prior Ad Regalis and the other priors. He was forced to back down. In the years after, he discovered and studied forbidden jotuun texts detailing an ancient form of kinmatter, quintecrakin. It led to his expulsion from the order, and a rift between him and Ayala. He killed Aniketh, his oldest friend, the night he was expelled (see Aniketh). He continued to practice and perfect it in secret, becoming more powerful, but also more corrupt. He became a feared and powerful figure in the underground world of Artisans, using his abilities for personal gain and manipulation. He aided the Union in destroying the jotuun's temples and any who supported them. At Ancrage, he shattered the battlements with massive stone spires, and his battle with the jotuun inside left the city in ruins. He used quintecrakin throughout the war, always unseen. It's how one man broke temples for ten years. He was spending the lives of everyone he'd killed, and nobody lived to say so. He held back from the Temple of Isi for ten years, in exchange for his daughter's safety, and was killed by her hand there at the war's end. See Ten-Year War. The spires he threw are part of Ancrage now, the largest a monument to the families lost. Neru'Augma, a group of zealots, means to bring him back from the dead.","n":""},{"t":"Aurel","u":"wiki/aurel.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A boy of the southern Abunali tribes, older brother of Arya and friend of Ano. When he's introduced, he's taking part in the Kashif Trials with his sister, eager to hunt something big. Aurel is quick to act and slow to notice when he's being led. He forgets hi","b":"A boy of the southern Abunali tribes, older brother of Arya and friend of Ano. When he's introduced, he's taking part in the Kashif Trials with his sister, eager to hunt something big. Aurel is quick to act and slow to notice when he's being led. He forgets his own gods, eats the bait, and would much rather hunt something big. In the Kashif Trials, he was grouped under Marshal Akkennoten with Arya, Ano, Artemis and Afina. At first, he saw no reason that the color of Artemis' hair should make him a bad guy, but Ano's warnings won him over, and he came to blows with Artemis for speaking with his sister. He struggled to keep his bow drawn, and Arya quietly helped him.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Auriel","u":"wiki/auriel.html","a":["Endelyn"],"f":"Characters","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Ayala","u":"wiki/ayala.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A prodigy among the jotuun, and the daughter of Augmen and Alibet, known to people as the demon's spawn. She ended the Ten-Year War by killing her father at the Temple of Isi, and went into hiding with the last of her order. She's dead by 211 ABU. Ayala was of","b":"A prodigy among the jotuun, and the daughter of Augmen and Alibet, known to people as the demon's spawn. She ended the Ten-Year War by killing her father at the Temple of Isi, and went into hiding with the last of her order. She's dead by 211 ABU. Ayala was of Abunali descent, with white hair (see White Hair) and yellow eyes. People know a woman of her description as the demon's spawn. Her mother ran off when she was only days old, and Augmen left her with the family of his friend, Aniketh, when the two of them went to join the jotuun. When she was brought there, the family looked upon her at once with grave concern: \"The hidden ones watch this one closely.\" Everyone on the farm and in town refused to interact with her because they feared Oiyas, and the farm's rapid decline in crops and increasingly frequent raids reinforced it. When she was four, her father took her along, as the jotuun were very open-minded in comparison. Ayala trained as a neophyte under the supervision of several priors, all of whom witnessed her excel through the many trainings and exercises they put her through. She was presented with the opportunity of a lifetime: she'd excel past the rank of paladin, so long as she trained at the Temple of Isi, on Icos. She was hesitant to leave her father behind, but she accepted, against his very vocal disapproval. Augmen's study of quintecrakin had him expelled, and the two of them fell out. As he grew feared, Ayala became a respected and powerful jotuun, who used her abilities for the betterment of others and upheld the principles of the order. The jotuun told her that Augmen had killed Aniketh, but not what for, only that it occurred the night of his expulsion (see Aniketh). She was distraught, and believed that her father must have had a good reason. After much contemplation, and hearing of his further exploits, she withdrew from that notion. For ten years, the war went around the Temple of Isi. When it finally reached her, she killed her father. See Ten-Year War. After the war, she hid from the world in Qis with seven other jotuun, Mirak among them, until Aethel Zuul found them. She was defensive, and asked why she shouldn't kill him where he stood. He told her he wasn't his father. He earned her trust, and they grew much closer over several years, always meeting in private. She fell in love with him, and he with her, though their love could never have been pursued. It ended when Qis was found. Late in their relationship, she heavily mistrusted and actively hated him. She died protecting Mirak, along the eastern cliffs near Medjet. She was training him when the hair on the back of her neck began to stand. Someone was watching. All seven Inquisitors of Zuul's Hand had to dispatch her, and she went down fighting. In the end, she killed all but one, and they had to be replaced over time. Alfor Zuul, still a prince, had sent them.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Azlan","u":"wiki/azlan.html","a":["Azlan","Az"],"f":"Characters","c":"#c9671b","s":"The nineteen-year-old son of a zarbuck rancher from the outskirts of Kama, who travels with his closest friend, Artemis. Earnest and easily worried, he sees himself as a cowardly follower. He means to get out of the slums by hard work and virtue, and one day s","b":"The nineteen-year-old son of a zarbuck rancher from the outskirts of Kama, who travels with his closest friend, Artemis. Earnest and easily worried, he sees himself as a cowardly follower. He means to get out of the slums by hard work and virtue, and one day settle down with someone. Azlan is earnest and friendly to a fault. He rambles when nervous, and apologizes even for apologizing. He grew up on a homestead just outside of Kama, where his family kept zarbuck in stables and wide pastures. Every harvest season, the whole family would gather for a feast, with stories and music, and his grandmother's zarbuck stew. No matter how busy they were, it was the one time of year when everyone made an effort to be together. He met Artemis by running into him with a basket of fruit, near Kama's abandoned orphanage. He called him \"Arty\" at once, apologized for it, hugged him without being asked to, and invited him to the feast. \"A family is like a tree. The branches may grow in different directions, but the roots keep them all together.\" — repeating his grandfather's words to Artemis","n":""},{"t":"Azra","u":"wiki/azra.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A nineteen-year-old who survived the burning of Morowai as a child, was taken in by Midori in Kama, and has since lived among Tibs' gang. Self-sufficient and quick to decide, she means to become a master hunter and seek retribution. When she first came to Kama","b":"A nineteen-year-old who survived the burning of Morowai as a child, was taken in by Midori in Kama, and has since lived among Tibs' gang. Self-sufficient and quick to decide, she means to become a master hunter and seek retribution. When she first came to Kama, her clothes resembled those of the nearby Abunali tribes, though something was off about them, and ornate teal tattoos showed where her skin was bare. She's stronger than she looks, makes friends easily, and is quick to brush her feelings aside. Azra is the daughter of Aiore and Kade Asher, and the younger sister of Queen Vaenera. After Morowai was raided, Tibs brought her to Kama. For the raid itself, see Vaenera & Azra's Origin. Azra met Artemis at Midori's orphanage, having traveled a good deal farther than he had, and offered to help him carry furniture minutes after telling him her home was gone. Having once argued with a sister of her own, she sent him back to make amends with Afina after the two had fought. When Midori left Kama, she took Azra with her, unwilling to leave another child under Tibs' influence. In Khamu, Azra made friends quickly, Amori among them. She led her group as pathfinder in the first of the Kashif Trials, and they were the first to finish. When the groups were reformed, she joined her friends, who were two members short, under Marshal T'Akoteh. Elder Azhotep anointed her with the mark of Atta, for her strength and independence. She later lived among Tibs' gang, in the ruins of an old temple, where he counted her among his favorites. To her, thieving was a way to survive, and the means to one day live in a city, or even a small town. \"We can't change the past. We can only control what we do now.\" — to Artemis, in the gang's hideout Vaenera & Azra's Origin","n":""},{"t":"Bal","u":"wiki/bal.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A man of the Gurtai, and advisor to Khusan, their ruler. He's plotting, and encourages Khusan's ruthless development. He hid Guryeg's death from Oprax as an investment, since a chieftain's son trained by the enemy was worth more to him than a chieftain, and an","b":"A man of the Gurtai, and advisor to Khusan, their ruler. He's plotting, and encourages Khusan's ruthless development. He hid Guryeg's death from Oprax as an investment, since a chieftain's son trained by the enemy was worth more to him than a chieftain, and answered Khusan's letters as his father. He doesn't know that Khusan has worked it out. He thinks the family were all needlessly foolish, but that Blood Magic is a useful tool when it isn't yourself you're sacrificing.","n":""},{"t":"Catalina Valentyre","u":"wiki/catalina-valentyre.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The late wife of the third High King, Aethel Zuul, and mother of Tybal, Aeleina, and the current High King, Alfor Zuul. She was of the Valentyres, merchants with distant Azkassari blood, and was beloved at High Sonham, where she died grieving her eldest son. C","b":"The late wife of the third High King, Aethel Zuul, and mother of Tybal, Aeleina, and the current High King, Alfor Zuul. She was of the Valentyres, merchants with distant Azkassari blood, and was beloved at High Sonham, where she died grieving her eldest son. Catalina had fair skin, brown hair, and eyes of a red so pale that people took them for hazel until the sun was on them. Her son Alfor has them. She was warm, and a worrier. She mothered everyone, servants included, and couldn't rest while anyone was away from home. It made her beloved. She was a merchant's daughter all the same, shrewd and good with figures, and she ran the household's accounts for years. She married Aethel when he was still the second son. Endelyn chose her for him, as she chose Dendrilla Ariadne for Archon. Anteo never liked either choice. Catalina lost her firstborn son to Nirva, with nothing found to bury, and fell into despair. Aethel was away at Qis while she grieved, and she began to ask where he went. It was the accounts that told her. When she learned, she told Zuul's Hand. The holdouts died for it, and it's what she couldn't live with. Already prone to despair, she confessed it to Aethel, and cast herself from a ledge of High Sonham. Alfor blames Ayala, and her closeness to his father, for his mother's death, and never learned what his mother did first.","n":""},{"t":"Cho Abbot","u":"wiki/cho-abbot.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#b3332e","s":"As the personal handmaiden and stylist to Queen Kota and Princess Koto in Trion, she is responsible for their hair, attire, and overall public presentation. Mute since childhood and devoted to service, she's both confidant and caretaker to Koto. Niles Abbot is","b":"As the personal handmaiden and stylist to Queen Kota and Princess Koto in Trion, she is responsible for their hair, attire, and overall public presentation. Mute since childhood and devoted to service, she's both confidant and caretaker to Koto. Niles Abbot is her half-brother. Mute since childhood, Cho communicates through gesture, serving as both her language and her discipline. Unlike her half-brother Niles, whose pride and verbosity embody the excesses of nobility, Cho teaches through action—her silence a study in restraint and purpose. Her craft reflects her values: careful, deliberate, and deeply personal. To Koto, she is both confidant and caretaker; to the rest of the palace, she is an invisible constant whose quiet diligence keeps the royal image intact. Cho’s inability to speak does not stem from weakness but from a vow of service, a remnant of Opraxi vestal tradition where one’s hands and deeds convey devotion more truthfully than words. Though she cannot voice her thoughts, her expression lies in her art—the dresses she tailors, the hairstyles she arranges, and the calm she brings to the storm of courtly life. Cho is trusted with part of the winter grain that Kota sends to Novon in secret. Kota has never had to say aloud what she's asking for.","n":""},{"t":"Commander Jiro","u":"wiki/commander-jiro.html","a":["Jiro","Commander","Jiro Kanakao","Master of Arms"],"f":"Characters","c":"#b3332e","s":"Commander and Master of Arms within the Talvarian Knights, and the younger brother and second of Kazuro Kanakao, their Lord Commander.","b":"Commander and Master of Arms within the Talvarian Knights, and the younger brother and second of Kazuro Kanakao, their Lord Commander.","n":""},{"t":"Cyreth","u":"wiki/cyreth.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Dendrilla Ariadne","u":"wiki/dendrilla-ariadne.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The late wife of Prince Archon Zuul, and mother of Haylor and Alexium. Anteo Zuul disinherited her son and turned her out of Wietmoire, and she's remembered as the disgraced princess whose child no one believed was a Zuul. Endelyn chose her for Archon. Anteo h","b":"The late wife of Prince Archon Zuul, and mother of Haylor and Alexium. Anteo Zuul disinherited her son and turned her out of Wietmoire, and she's remembered as the disgraced princess whose child no one believed was a Zuul. Endelyn chose her for Archon. Anteo had wanted his heir to marry a Kalmere, and never liked the choice. She stayed at Wietmoire Manor after her marriage, once it had been taken from the Talvos (see The Massacre at Wietmoire). Her firstborn, Haylor, was a stillbirth. She was pregnant with Alexium at the time of Archon's death (see The Death of Archon), but people did not believe he was Archon's child. Anteo Zuul took one look at the reddish brown hair and dark eyes of the babe, and coldly told her, \"That is no Zuul,\" before walking away. He disinherited the child and kicked Dendrilla out of Wietmoire. Alexium was Archon's son. Anteo refused to believe it. She wrote Anteo a letter, and sent it to High Sonham. It was a heartfelt apology. She felt that it was her fault for dressing a certain way, making others believe that she might have led others on, but she assured Anteo that she had lain with no man but Archon. A sad reality, that even when she'd done no wrong, she felt responsible for what happened. Anteo read it, took the apology for a confession, and kept the letter as proof that he'd been right. Aethel Zuul read it as well, and had it among his things when he died. Alfor Zuul found it there, and saw the truth in the same words. Aethel had answered her, and sent money besides. As of 211 ABU, Alfor still has her letter, and the answer is among Alexium's belongings in Phylomina. She and her family hid it from her son until she died, at first out of fear of Anteo, and then because Aethel had asked it of her. How he learned is told in Alexium Ariadne. Alexium took her name, and became king of Phylomina.","n":""},{"t":"Dismas","u":"wiki/dismas.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Doros Meredeon","u":"wiki/doros-meredeon.html","a":["Sea Marshal"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A merchant captain, and the Sea Marshal in the King of Orsena's court. He's the Bank of Pearls' choice for the office, which once belonged to the house of Polys, and he guards the Bank's routes before anything else. The office belonged to the house of Polys un","b":"A merchant captain, and the Sea Marshal in the King of Orsena's court. He's the Bank of Pearls' choice for the office, which once belonged to the house of Polys, and he guards the Bank's routes before anything else. The office belonged to the house of Polys until it was sold to him, as the Bank's choice. He hasn't fought at sea. The Bank's routes come first with him, and the island towns come after. There's an unspoken bargain between him and the pirates of the isles. They don't touch the Bank's ships, and his fleet is never where they are. Nobody's ever said it aloud, and it isn't written down.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Egon Talvo","u":"wiki/egon-talvo.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A son of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo of Talvar, dead by 211 ABU.","b":"A son of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo of Talvar, dead by 211 ABU.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Elder A'Te","u":"wiki/elder-ate.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Matriarch of Dunis for many decades. Died peacefully at the start of 211 ABU; Shaman Ankti, a close friend, was given temporary eldership.","b":"Matriarch of Dunis for many decades. Died peacefully at the start of 211 ABU; Shaman Ankti, a close friend, was given temporary eldership.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Elder Amunti","u":"wiki/elder-amunti.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"One of the three elders of the kashif, and the one who presents the opening challenge of the Kashif Trials. He's eccentric and theatrical, but the most cautious of the three. Amunti stands with a slouch, clasping and rubbing his hands together. He has a flair ","b":"One of the three elders of the kashif, and the one who presents the opening challenge of the Kashif Trials. He's eccentric and theatrical, but the most cautious of the three. Amunti stands with a slouch, clasping and rubbing his hands together. He has a flair for the dramatic, and his own words for things. He presented the opening challenge of the Kashif Trials, an obstacle course he described as \"jumpy balance-y swingy puzzles,\" and had to be reminded to warn the children that it wouldn't be as easy as they thought.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Elder Antekano","u":"wiki/elder-antekano.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"One of the three elders of the kashif, and an elder of the people in Khamu, whose children she's charged with keeping safe during the Kashif Trials. She's a friend to Midori, and lets her and her children stay in the village. She let Midori and her children st","b":"One of the three elders of the kashif, and an elder of the people in Khamu, whose children she's charged with keeping safe during the Kashif Trials. She's a friend to Midori, and lets her and her children stay in the village. She let Midori and her children stay in the village, and offered to repay her for the lessons she gave there. Having noticed how interested Artemis was in the kashif, she suggested he enter the Kashif Trials, assuring Midori that the finest of them would be watching over the children. It fell to her to keep the children safe during the trials. By her word, any parent could remove their child from harm's way, though that child would be barred from further trials. She was pleased to see Midori's brood finish first. \"As the Elder of the people, the duty falls onto me to keep your children safe.\" — before the trials","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Elder Azhotep","u":"wiki/elder-azhotep.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"One of the three elders of the kashif and their commander-in-chief, who trained the marshals that serve under him. He presides over the Kashif Trials in Khamu, and holds firmly to their traditions. Azhotep is tall and burly, and his large fur cloak and long, g","b":"One of the three elders of the kashif and their commander-in-chief, who trained the marshals that serve under him. He presides over the Kashif Trials in Khamu, and holds firmly to their traditions. Azhotep is tall and burly, and his large fur cloak and long, greying mane make him all the more imposing. He holds firmly to tradition, but is fair in his judgments. He presided over the Kashif Trials in which Artemis, Azra and Afina took part, and took notice of Artemis the moment he saw him in the crowd. When disqualifications and complications left groups short, he had them reformed so that all might have an equal chance to finish. He anointed those who completed the trials. \"To those who think themselves to be ready, I urge you not to step forward… To those who know they are, please do.\" — to the children of Khamu \"We do not take without giving, nor do we give needlessly.\" — at the anointing","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Elise Polys","u":"wiki/elise-polys.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The wife of Leander Polys, lord of the house of Polys in Lorence, and the younger sister of the pirate Kade Asher, who once rescued her from the men who killed their parents. She was born to an aristocratic family of Arlene. On the way to Lorence, pirates kill","b":"The wife of Leander Polys, lord of the house of Polys in Lorence, and the younger sister of the pirate Kade Asher, who once rescued her from the men who killed their parents. She was born to an aristocratic family of Arlene. On the way to Lorence, pirates killed her parents and took her. Kade and his allies, Joss Harrow among them, later killed those responsible and rescued her, and she married Leander in Lorence. When Leander captured Kade, she begged for the choice he was given, which was the only mercy he'd get. His crew had never touched a ship of the Polys, for her sake. See Capture.","n":""},{"t":"Endelyn Larrensire","u":"wiki/endelyn-larrensire.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The late wife of the second High King, Anteo Zuul, and mother of Archon and Aethel. She was of the Larrensires, who rule in Endlemoire, the city the Zuuls had lived in as nobles. Long dead by 211 ABU, she lies entombed beneath the Varrow's Tree in the garden s","b":"The late wife of the second High King, Anteo Zuul, and mother of Archon and Aethel. She was of the Larrensires, who rule in Endlemoire, the city the Zuuls had lived in as nobles. Long dead by 211 ABU, she lies entombed beneath the Varrow's Tree in the garden she kept at High Sonham, where her grandson, Alfor Zuul, still goes to quiet his mind. Endelyn was tall and pale, with long, wavy, black hair streaked with bronze, and red eyes. She was compassionate and emotional, and wanted nothing more than to live happily with her family. Stress threw her into a deep depression. She chose the wives of both her sons, Dendrilla Ariadne for Archon and Catalina Valentyre for Aethel. Anteo never liked either choice. Endelyn gave birth to a third child at High Sonham, while a dra'iq screamed in the distance. Anteo held her hand throughout, and brightly smiled: \"We've finally done it.\" \"This had better be a girl,\" she answered. \"Then we could finally be at peace.\" The child was a boy with white hair (see White Hair). Anteo's smile faded the moment he unwrapped the cloth. He handed the infant back, gave the midwives a distinct look, and stormed out, and they nodded in compliance. When Endelyn was shown him, she covered her face and waved them away. They lowered the infant into the washtub and left. Aethel, who was ten, approached the washtub and watched the infant struggle to breathe. Endelyn told him not to look, and he ran from the room, crying. When Archon was killed and Anteo prepared for war, she feared her husband would meet a similar fate. She traveled to a jotuun monastery to plead for assistance, only to find they were already planning to intervene, and Prior Ad Regalis told her she could accompany him and his apprentice, Augmen. See The Death of Archon. Anteo granted her a room in High Sonham for a garden, and she planted a Varrow's Tree at its center. She found the dead buried beneath it and told no one, and made it her tradition to kneel before the tree and pray quietly, which she kept to her last day (see High Sonham). She was kneeling there when she heard Kaelyn scream (see The Massacre at Wietmoire). Archon's death left her in a deep depression that nothing seemed to rectify. She spent her few remaining years frequently visiting her garden, where she consumed the tree's leaf. She wanted only to be happy, but no amount of poisonous petals would suffice. Before long, she fell into an eternal slumber, and her servants found her moments later. Her family frequently stood by her bedside in her last remaining days, wishing they'd been there to comfort her. Anteo pressured the caretakers to try and save her, but there was very little they could do, and before long, her soul was Oies' to take. Anteo hated the garden for what it caused, but loved it as Endelyn once had. He had her mummified and entombed underneath the Varrow's Tree, where she remained untouched, and he, too, started to visit the garden frequently. The Larrensires gave the High Throne a queen in her. The Prince of Moirach, Severin Larrensire, is her nephew.","n":""},{"t":"Enya Talvo","u":"wiki/enya-talvo.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A daughter of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo of Talvar, and elder sister of Koto. She's married to a lord in Athura.","b":"A daughter of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo of Talvar, and elder sister of Koto. She's married to a lord in Athura.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Evander Larrensire","u":"wiki/evander-larrensire.html","a":["Evander"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The younger son of Severin, the Prince of Moirach and lord of Endlemoire, about thirty years old. Of the Larrensire, he was sent to court in Karlaine as a boy. He views Thea as a little cousin at most, and genuinely wants to protect her. He's quietly ashamed o","b":"The younger son of Severin, the Prince of Moirach and lord of Endlemoire, about thirty years old. Of the Larrensire, he was sent to court in Karlaine as a boy. He views Thea as a little cousin at most, and genuinely wants to protect her. He's quietly ashamed of his family's pride. Internally, he knows his father wants to marry him off, but he wishes to pursue his own interests, finding no woman that suits his truest desires. In the event his father ever does intend to marry him off, his plan is to swear himself to the Highguard and relocate to High Sonham. He doesn't desire to be a Highguard, but if his father imposes a will he cannot bear, he feels as though the choice is the only one he can make. By law, his father must oblige.","n":""},{"t":"Gartan","u":"wiki/gartan.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The older son of Guryeg and Milun, of the Boryign clan of The Hunto. He died years before 211 ABU, attempting to rescue his brother, Khusan, from Oprax.","b":"The older son of Guryeg and Milun, of the Boryign clan of The Hunto. He died years before 211 ABU, attempting to rescue his brother, Khusan, from Oprax.","n":""},{"t":"General Tsavin","u":"wiki/general-tsavin.html","a":["General","Tsavin"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A late general in the service of the Zuul Union, whose castle, Tsavin's Fortress, still stands in the Anklan Isles.","b":"A late general in the service of the Zuul Union, whose castle, Tsavin's Fortress, still stands in the Anklan Isles.","n":""},{"t":"Guryeg","u":"wiki/guryeg.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Once the chieftain of The Hunto, of the Boryign clan. Husband of Milun, and father of Gartan and Khusan. He died during a hunt, only a few years after Khusan had been offered to Oprax as a prisoner.","b":"Once the chieftain of The Hunto, of the Boryign clan. Husband of Milun, and father of Gartan and Khusan. He died during a hunt, only a few years after Khusan had been offered to Oprax as a prisoner.","n":""},{"t":"Haylor Zuul","u":"wiki/haylor-zuul.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The first born son of Dendrilla Ariadne and Prince Archon Zuul. He was a stillbirth.","b":"The first born son of Dendrilla Ariadne and Prince Archon Zuul. He was a stillbirth.","n":""},{"t":"Joras","u":"wiki/joras.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A friend of Prince Archon Zuul, who was with him when he died. See The Death of Archon. When the titans marched south, it was Joras who opted to prevent them from reaching Stade. Archon agreed, and was killed. See The Death of Archon.","b":"A friend of Prince Archon Zuul, who was with him when he died. See The Death of Archon. When the titans marched south, it was Joras who opted to prevent them from reaching Stade. Archon agreed, and was killed. See The Death of Archon.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Joss Harrow","u":"wiki/joss-harrow.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A pirate captain of the Anklan Isles, once the first mate and closest friend of Kade Asher, and the man who burned Morowai. In 211 ABU, his crew are being hunted down one by one, and he hasn't told them by whom. Joss was one of the pirates who helped Kade kill","b":"A pirate captain of the Anklan Isles, once the first mate and closest friend of Kade Asher, and the man who burned Morowai. In 211 ABU, his crew are being hunted down one by one, and he hasn't told them by whom. Joss was one of the pirates who helped Kade kill those responsible for his parents' deaths, and rescue his sister, Elise. When Kade left for the sake of Aiore, he gave Joss the ship. Joss and the crew never touched a ship of the Polys, for Elise's sake. Then Kade gave up their hideout, and it was her husband, Leander Polys, who came for them. Most of them escaped, and Joss lost the most. He struck back at Morowai, under the sails that Kade would know, and beat him in his final stand. See Vaenera & Azra's Origin. Kade, whom he'd left for the fire, has been killing his crew ever since. Joss could tell who it was from the first of them, by the way it was done. He hasn't told his crew, who don't know whom they're dying for, and he recruits more. By 211 ABU he and his closest mates are the ones Kade wants most.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Kade Asher","u":"wiki/kade-asher.html","a":["Kade"],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"Once an infamous pirate captain, now a hunter of his own kind. He lost everything he loved when his old crew burned Morowai, and in 211 ABU, with next to nothing to his name, he's killing them one by one for the authorities of Orsena. The one he wants most is ","b":"Once an infamous pirate captain, now a hunter of his own kind. He lost everything he loved when his old crew burned Morowai, and in 211 ABU, with next to nothing to his name, he's killing them one by one for the authorities of Orsena. The one he wants most is Joss Harrow, who was his closest friend. Kade was a free spirit who didn't allow himself to be controlled. When he was a pirate, he loved it. Kade's family were aristocratic. They were on their way to Lorence from Arlene, hoping to find more opportunity there, when pirates killed his parents and took his little sister, Elise. He fell into a life of piracy after that. The pirates responsible were later killed by Kade and some allies he'd made along the way, pirates themselves, and Elise was rescued. She married an aristocrat in Lorence, Leander Polys, lord of the house of Polys and Sea Marshal. By that point, Kade couldn't find it in himself to settle down, so he continued being a pirate, now a captain of his own crew. He was in his late twenties. They were a band of vagrants turned pirates, and he became infamous to civilians and pirates alike. Over time he fell out with them, and felt stuck among them. When they made landfall on Tabla Pise, he met Aiore, and put the life behind him for her sake. He gave the ship to his first mate and closest friend, Joss Harrow, one of the pirates who'd helped him rescue Elise. By the time he'd fully left his crew, she had accepted Alexium Ariadne's proposal. He remained on the island, caring for Aiore's elderly mother and supporting the sprouting villages of the community. When Aiore returned, the two had an affair, and Azra was born. Kade was living comfortably in Morowai, helping Aiore's mother and trying to be a better man for her and their sprouting family, when he was located by the authorities. It was the house of Polys, acting on behalf of the crown of Orsena, and the Sea Marshal who captured him was his own brother-in-law. They threatened to throw him in a dungeon and have him rot for the rest of his days for the life he'd lived. The only way they wouldn't was if he gave up his old crew. He could lose his family, or give up his old one. The choice was the only mercy he'd get, and Elise had begged for it. He chose to sell his crew out, and they were hit for it. They'd always known whose wife Elise was, and for her sake they'd never touched a ship of the Polys. Then it was her husband who came for them, and Joss, who'd helped save her, lost the most. The authorities came crashing down on their hideout on one of the islands, but weren't efficient, and most of the crew were able to escape. Once Aiore told Alexium the truth, Kade knew he couldn't stay in Morowai with Azra and her grandmother. He left them, and they never heard from him again. The pirates who later burned Morowai flew a sigil associated with him. They were his old crew. Kade returned once he saw their sails, as nothing good could come from that image. It was as though they knew what would draw him out of hiding. There was no way the authorities could have known of the hideout unless Kade had ratted, so they struck him as payback. Hard. They wanted Kade and his wife, believing Aiore to be living on the island at the time, and not knowing of her place in Phylomina. Azra was simply collateral to them. He made a final stand against Joss, lost, and just barely survived. See Vaenera & Azra's Origin. Kade was rescued by the authorities who'd confronted him in the first place. They'd realized they were playing a pirate's game, and they weren't pirates, so they had him hunt down each member of his old crew for them. He's done it with next to nothing to his name, and has believed all the while that everyone's dead, Azra included. Joss wouldn't see his crew dropping dead and not recruit more, so there are new faces among them, and by 211 ABU Kade has more left to kill than the captain. But his primary target, both personally and by contract, is Joss and his closest mates. Vaenera & Azra's Origin","n":""},{"t":"Kendo Burinkan","u":"wiki/kendo-burinkan.html","a":["Lord Kendo Burinkan","Lord Buttsuck"],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A twenty-one-year-old lord of Ashelan and Opraxi blood, obsessed with protecting the innocent and dishing out authority.","b":"A twenty-one-year-old lord of Ashelan and Opraxi blood, obsessed with protecting the innocent and dishing out authority.","n":""},{"t":"Khusan","u":"wiki/khusan.html","a":["Khusan Boryign"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The chief of The Hunto, and the Ruler of the Gurtai behind the rumored unification of their tribes, whose name the world outside doesn't know. Of the Boryign clan, the younger son of Guryeg and Milun, he was offered to Oprax as a prisoner when he was a boy, an","b":"The chief of The Hunto, and the Ruler of the Gurtai behind the rumored unification of their tribes, whose name the world outside doesn't know. Of the Boryign clan, the younger son of Guryeg and Milun, he was offered to Oprax as a prisoner when he was a boy, and has sharpened the Gurtai into a weapon against it since he came home. Khusan is imposing, and his goal is to unify the Gurtai tribes. Once a treaty hostage, he speaks fluent Foomesh, was trained in the very organization the Gurtai lost to, and returned with the one weapon his ancestors lacked. He grew up in the Emperor's court in Oprax, alongside the girl who would become The Blood Empress. They grew up together, but house arrest is still living as someone's prisoner. Believing he was destined for more, he escaped, fleeing into the Opraxi countryside. For two years he wandered, avoiding recognition and accepting charity from strangers, until he found his way to the other side of the Kintari Mountains. After nearly freezing to death and being hunted by predators, he finally returned to the west, only to find that his father, a chieftain of one of the more powerful tribes, had died years ago, alongside his mother and brother. The Emperor wasn't responsible. Guryeg died during a hunt only a few years after Khusan had been offered as a prisoner. Gartan died attempting to rescue him from Oprax. Milun was taken as a prize by the new chieftain, Nuko, merely a late chieftain's wife turned concubine. She died attempting to perform a blood sacrifice with her unborn child, fathered by Nuko, to bring her family back. Had the ruthless Emperor known Guryeg had already perished, Khusan would've been put to death. Nuko had risen as the new chieftain before his return. Khusan challenged Nuko and won, earning the title of chief, before uniting the rest of the tribes into The Gurtai Horde. Bal, the man whispering in his ear, is plotting, and encourages the young man's ruthless development. It was Bal who hid Guryeg's death from Oprax, which kept Khusan alive. It was an investment: a chieftain's son trained by the enemy was worth more to him than a chieftain. When Khusan wrote home, Bal answered as his father, shaping the boy from a distance and learning everything he confided. Khusan has since worked it out and says nothing, keeping Bal close because the voice that raised him is in there somewhere. Bal thinks his family were all needlessly foolish, but that blood magic is a useful tool when it isn't yourself you're sacrificing. Risen to prominence among the Gurtai, he's sharpened them into a weapon, structured and organized to directly fight Oprax, wanting vengeance against the Emperor. But the Emperor has died, and his daughter has taken his place. She saw how dangerous he is and what he has accomplished, and secured an alliance, citing their fleeting friendship when they were younger. He accepted warily, and she gained access to Blood Magic through him and his courtiers. His closest advisors warn him not to trust her. Privately, he's clear that he doesn't: he knows that his riders, once past Oprax's defenses, could easily take the basin.","n":""},{"t":"King Ahaad","u":"wiki/king-ahaad.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#c9671b","s":"The king of Sulala, capital of Abu, and father of Princess Adja.","b":"The king of Sulala, capital of Abu, and father of Princess Adja.","n":""},{"t":"King Arabah","u":"wiki/king-arabah.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The last king of Malu, before it became Phylomina. A dignified and honest man, and a longtime friend of Alexium Ariadne, who took his place when he died in the attack of 196 ABU. When a dra'iq attacked his city, Arabah knelt before his throne and prayed, and w","b":"The last king of Malu, before it became Phylomina. A dignified and honest man, and a longtime friend of Alexium Ariadne, who took his place when he died in the attack of 196 ABU. When a dra'iq attacked his city, Arabah knelt before his throne and prayed, and was struck in the chest by a stray stone. He lived long enough to ask the names of those who'd driven it off, and to find that their captain was his friend. With his final plea, he asked that Alexium take his place. See The Fall of Malu.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"King Araeus Savvad II","u":"wiki/king-araeus-savvad-ii.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#2f8a55","s":"A king of about fifty-seven who feels cheated of his birthright, and seeks full control over Orsena.","b":"A king of about fifty-seven who feels cheated of his birthright, and seeks full control over Orsena.","n":""},{"t":"King Arvaan","u":"wiki/king-arvaan.html","a":["King Arvaan of Suran"],"f":"Characters","c":"#a8860b","s":"The late king of Suran, husband of Queen Ai and father of Prince Amersi. He was killed by the Keiran Clan while trying to free his city from its war with the Duevals.","b":"The late king of Suran, husband of Queen Ai and father of Prince Amersi. He was killed by the Keiran Clan while trying to free his city from its war with the Duevals.","n":""},{"t":"King Cedric Talvo","u":"wiki/king-cedric-talvo.html","a":["The Illegitimate King"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The founder of Trion and of the Kingdom of Talvar, derided by his contemporaries as 'The Illegitimate King.' Son of King Gideon Talvo and Queen Kaelyn Skyfall, husband of Lady Haileen Ermsburry, and father of King Onfroi Talvo, who wears the crown he was refus","b":"The founder of Trion and of the Kingdom of Talvar, derided by his contemporaries as 'The Illegitimate King.' Son of King Gideon Talvo and Queen Kaelyn Skyfall, husband of Lady Haileen Ermsburry, and father of King Onfroi Talvo, who wears the crown he was refused. Dead by 211 ABU, he's entombed beneath Greystone. With his father and Lord Hiro Kuroshi, Cedric brought Opraxi refugees across the sea to Athura (see Internal Struggle). They came home to find their family killed, and Onfroi alive in the gardens. Cedric pleaded to face Anteo Zuul at his father's side, but was sent away with his son. Haileen, who'd been with her family in Endletonn, was left in Icos. See The Massacre at Wietmoire. Exiled from Icos for treason, Cedric raised Greystone and then Trion with Hiro's help, and promised his son to Hiro's granddaughter. See The Founding of Trion. His reign cemented the city as a haven for immigrants from both Oprax and Ashela. The same year Anteo Zuul died, he returned to Icos to legitimize his realm, where the court of Karlaine whispered of 'the Prince Overseas.' He turned down the daughter of Aethel Zuul for Onfroi, having already promised him to Kota, and left without the crown he'd asked for. His son had it instead. See The Crowning of Onfroi. Aethel then told him, alone, how his parents had died. He was the only Zuul who'd ever told Cedric the truth. Afterward, Cedric took his son to see Haileen, who'd heard of his landfall and didn't expect him to show up. She sailed to Trion with them. Cedric ran the realm from beside the throne, and never once sat in it. In court he knelt to his son like anyone else, and meant it. When Onfroi came of age he stepped back the same day, and that night told him the way to High Sonham. He never told him what Aethel had said, only that his grandfather hadn't knelt. He lived with Haileen for a long while, and lived to see the ships stop when Nirva closed Blackrock Bay. He told Onfroi to ask Aethel Zuul for help, as one honorable man would another, and died still sure it'd come. Onfroi had never asked. Haileen outlived him. He and Haileen are entombed next to each other in the crypt beneath Greystone. Alfor Zuul dismisses Talvar's pleas for aid, viewing its suffering as the consequence of its founder's defiance. \"Forgive me, but is being ruler of eight kingdoms not enough?\" — to Aethel Zuul, in Karlaine","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"King Edgard Talvo","u":"wiki/king-edgard-talvo.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"King Gideon Talvo","u":"wiki/king-gideon-talvo.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The head of the Talvo Royal Family before The Massacre at Wietmoire, husband of Queen Kaelyn Skyfall, and father of King Cedric Talvo. He went to face Anteo Zuul alone, and was killed. In 211 ABU his grandson, Onfroi, rules the Talvar raised by those he'd sent","b":"The head of the Talvo Royal Family before The Massacre at Wietmoire, husband of Queen Kaelyn Skyfall, and father of King Cedric Talvo. He went to face Anteo Zuul alone, and was killed. In 211 ABU his grandson, Onfroi, rules the Talvar raised by those he'd sent away. Gideon was quite tall and strong, with a gruff voice. He was known for his distinctive level-headedness. Gideon was the son of Edmund Talvo, who never saw the west. His grandfather, Aldric Talvo, had seen Talvar as a boy, and lived long enough to tell Gideon about it, the knee included. A lord at most by law, Gideon went west and apologized to the hilltribes as no Talvo had, and they chose him as their king in secret. It took years. He walked alone to each tribe that had been warred on, sat with its dead, and was told every name. At each he said which of it was his family's doing, asked for nothing, and went on to the next. At the end he swore that no Talvo would kneel again, for anything, unless the tribes said so. Tormod Crannoch let him through the moors, and the tribes brought him their feuds at Moirlander's Respite. He settled them so well that the quiet in the west gave him away. See Talvo Royal Family. With Cedric and Lord Hiro Kuroshi, Gideon brought Opraxi refugees across the sea to Athura when they weren't safe anywhere in Icos, so that they might live peacefully in new lands. See Internal Struggle. He was away in Athura when his family was killed, and came home to the aftermath. He ordered Hiro to flee with Cedric and his grandson, believing the rest must be faced alone. He walked into High Sonham unannounced to face Anteo, who offered him Kaelyn's life if he knelt. He began to go down, and she told him not to, holding him to his oath. He stood. Kneeling would have undone everything he'd worked to rebuild, though nobody in that room would understand it. Anteo had him forced to his knees and beheaded him. See The Massacre at Wietmoire. The survivors raised a new Talvar, where his grandson, Onfroi, is king. \"You are no king.\" — to Anteo Zuul, in High Sonham","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"King Lanis Kalmere","u":"wiki/king-lanis-kalmere.html","a":["Lanis","King Lanis"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The king of Stade, sixty-eight years old, husband of Queen Loranne Kalmere, and father of Thea and Reine. He has no son, and refuses to name either daughter his heir, while the houses of his realm quietly choose sides. Son of Mandel Kalmere, grandson of Tarris","b":"The king of Stade, sixty-eight years old, husband of Queen Loranne Kalmere, and father of Thea and Reine. He has no son, and refuses to name either daughter his heir, while the houses of his realm quietly choose sides. Son of Mandel Kalmere, grandson of Tarris Kalmere, and younger brother of Oriane Kalmere, he's outwardly loyal to the High King, Alfor Zuul. Lanis is the picture of a king, broad and fair, with a blond beard going grey, and a quiet voice that doesn't match it. People hear certainty in him that isn't there. He met his first wife, Nephele, at Dremmel, where he came as a new king. The whole hall rose but her. The Oristenes recognize no hierarchy at home. She asked him what he thought of something, and waited until he answered, which nobody had. Nephele, an Ibyan woman and Thea's mother, died shortly after childbirth and naming her daughter. His next marriage, to Loranne, was one of duty rather than love. Though, Lanis grew fond of her, as she seemed to understand his melancholy and enabled him to quickly make peace with himself and move on. He's never made peace with how much of Nephele he sees in Thea. Lanis has no male heirs, and refuses to name an heir until a son is born. Leaning toward either of his daughters is close to treason (see Houses of Stade). He's close to his elder sister, Oriane, who was passed over for him, and tells her what he tells nobody else. She's the only one who knows what he'd say if he had to name one. He's outwardly loyal to the High King, Alfor Zuul.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"King Onfroi Talvo","u":"wiki/king-onfroi-talvo.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The King of Talvar, seventy-four years old, who holds court in Trion beside his wife, Queen Kota Talvo. His realm is failing. Nirva has closed Blackrock Bay for nearly four decades, resentment festers among his people, and the High King, Alfor Zuul, has spent ","b":"The King of Talvar, seventy-four years old, who holds court in Trion beside his wife, Queen Kota Talvo. His realm is failing. Nirva has closed Blackrock Bay for nearly four decades, resentment festers among his people, and the High King, Alfor Zuul, has spent years dismissing his pleas for aid. Of his six children, only the youngest, Koto, is left for him to protect. He's come to suspect that what became of the others was no accident, and is quietly looking for whoever's behind it while he plays the part of a stable ruler. Onfroi has straight, brown, shoulder-length hair and cyan eyes. He's a dutiful and diplomatic ruler, but emotionally distant, and trusts few. He's stricken with guilt over the deaths and disappearances of his children, and must appear stable all the same. The son of King Cedric Talvo and Lady Haileen Ermsburry, Onfroi survived The Massacre at Wietmoire as a small child, hidden by the servants in the gardens, and was raised across the sea, apart from his mother, who was kept in Icos. He was fifteen when he traveled to Karlaine with his father, through a winter that sent him into coughing fits he tried to hide. He entered the hall as the heir of an unrecognized ruler, and Aethel Zuul made him a king. See The Crowning of Onfroi. Cedric then took him to see his mother, whom he hadn't met in a long while, and the three of them sailed to Trion. Until he came of age, Cedric ran the realm from beside his throne, and knelt to him in court like anyone else. The night his father stepped back, he told Onfroi the way to High Sonham. Of what was done there, he only ever told him that his grandfather hadn't knelt. He married Kota, the granddaughter of Lord Hiro Kuroshi. For nearly four decades, Nirva has crippled trade and travel across Blackrock Bay, and Tybal Zuul, whom he'd befriended by letter and invited to Trion, was lost to the creature on the way. It's the first death that Onfroi ever blamed himself for. Cedric told him to ask Aethel for help, and Onfroi never did. He couldn't write to a man for ships when that man's son had died coming to see him. He let his father believe that he had, and Cedric died sure it'd come. By the time Onfroi did write, it was to Alfor Zuul. He's spent years appealing to him for aid, only to be dismissed. At the Royal Conclave, Alfor made a jest of it to Onfroi's face. Onfroi hasn't asked in quite the same way since. Each year, on the way there, he spends a night at Endletonn with the Ermsburrys, because his mother asked it of him before she died. They give him her room, and he's never asked whether they'd really have hanged. At Wietmoire, he's the guest of Alfor's sister, Aeleina Zuul, who keeps the manor, and has become her friend. One year, she told him that her father had waited for a letter, and would've sent everything he had. She didn't know what she was telling him, and he walked in the gardens until morning. Resentment festers among his people. He knows that Kota pays for Novon's winter grain in secret, and has the treasury round the numbers off. Neither has ever said a word about it to the other.","n":""},{"t":"Kiro","u":"wiki/kiro.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#b3332e","s":"The eighteen-year-old ward of the High King, Alfor Zuul, who raised and educated him, and the youngest and most zealous of his Inquisitors. Skilled with a spear and possessed of a crude yet potent elektrokin, he follows every command given to him without quest","b":"The eighteen-year-old ward of the High King, Alfor Zuul, who raised and educated him, and the youngest and most zealous of his Inquisitors. Skilled with a spear and possessed of a crude yet potent elektrokin, he follows every command given to him without question, believing it his duty to repay the generosity shown to him. Fiercely loyal, and driven by a desperate need to prove himself, Kiro strives to fulfill every command given to him diligently and without question, believing it his duty to repay the generosity shown to him. His crude yet potent elektrokin and proficient spearmanship make him formidable, though he lacks the experience and ferocity of his peers, and has significant room to grow. Raised and nurtured by the High King throughout almost the entirety of his life, he has pledged himself fully towards repaying his perceived debt. Due to this, he has uncontested faith in the High King and refuses to question any orders given to him, no matter how grim they may be. As an infant, Kiro was entrusted to Theara, one of the last surviving jotuun. His mother was an opraxi refugee in Upror, who couldn't keep a child there. She's never known whose house he grew up in. In Theara's care, he began learning elektrokin, which she'd discovered in her seclusion. Their quiet life came to an abrupt end when Alfor, having discovered her whereabouts, sought her out. She'd used her lightning to protect the boy, and the rumor of it spread. Discreetly accompanied by his Inquisitors, he confronted her on her loyalties, and upon confirming her unwillingness to cooperate, ordered her execution. Before departing, he stumbled upon the boy hidden within the temple. One among his Inquisitors prepared to kill the child, but Alfor spared him, intending to mold him into a powerful and compliant instrument of his will. Unaware of the truth, Kiro was raised within Zuul's Hand, where he underwent unrelenting training in combat, and was conditioned to obey without hesitation. Eager to prove himself worthy of the High King's trust and time, he's become a skilled assassin, and is the youngest and most zealous of the Inquisitors. Educated personally by the High King and mentored by Kiyana in wielding a spear, he has a comprehensive education and proficiency with his spear. On top of this, he has some grasp of elektrokin, courtesy of his deceased Jotu'un master. He can't use it reliably, and barely produces a spark on his own. To channel his elektrokin into a directed bolt, he uses a device that Alfor gifted him after taking him in and watching him train with the other Inquisitors. It's a stun baton, out of place because it's not of this world. Nobody knows its purpose or name, but clicking a button on it produces a shock. It was found in a crashed spaceship in the far north (see The Vessel). None of the people of Artisans can explain it, and it's pretty much a secret between Alfor and his immediate confidants. His goal is to shape Kiro into a weapon, not too dissimilar from what Augmen was to Anteo.","n":""},{"t":"Kiyana","u":"wiki/kiyana.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Lady Emi Kuroshi","u":"wiki/lady-emi-kuroshi.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The wife of Lord Hiro Kuroshi, who brought the Opraxi refugees to Novon, mother of Lord Akrio Kuroshi, and grandmother of Queen Kota Talvo.","b":"The wife of Lord Hiro Kuroshi, who brought the Opraxi refugees to Novon, mother of Lord Akrio Kuroshi, and grandmother of Queen Kota Talvo.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Lady Haileen Ermsburry","u":"wiki/lady-haileen-ermsburry.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The late wife of King Cedric Talvo and mother of King Onfroi Talvo, of the Ermsburrys of Endletonn. She was kept in Icos, apart from them both, until Anteo Zuul died, and lived out her days in Trion. By 211 ABU she lies beside Cedric in the crypt beneath Greys","b":"The late wife of King Cedric Talvo and mother of King Onfroi Talvo, of the Ermsburrys of Endletonn. She was kept in Icos, apart from them both, until Anteo Zuul died, and lived out her days in Trion. By 211 ABU she lies beside Cedric in the crypt beneath Greystone. Haileen was with her family in Endletonn on the night of The Massacre at Wietmoire. Anteo Zuul's order reached them before Cedric could. She was left in Icos, and kept with the Ermsburrys until Anteo was pronounced dead. They told her the truth. If she left, her whole family would hang for it. Nobody locked the gate, and she could have walked out any night. She stayed for them. After Onfroi was named King of Talvar, Cedric took him to see her. She'd heard of Cedric making landfall, and didn't expect him to show up. She enjoyed meeting her son for the first time in a long while, and sailed to Trion with them. She lived with Cedric for a long while. Before she died, she asked Onfroi to visit the Ermsburrys, and he's stopped there every year since. She died after Cedric, and was entombed next to him in the crypt beneath Greystone.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Lady Sakuri Kuroshi","u":"wiki/lady-sakuri-kuroshi.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The wife of Lord Akrio Kuroshi of Novon, and mother of Queen Kota Talvo.","b":"The wife of Lord Akrio Kuroshi of Novon, and mother of Queen Kota Talvo.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Leander Polys","u":"wiki/leander-polys.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The lord of the house of Polys, of Lorence, and once Sea Marshal in the King of Orsena's court, an office he's lost to Doros Meredeon. He's the husband of Elise Polys, and hates her brother, Kade Asher, whom he captured and now has to watch succeed where his f","b":"The lord of the house of Polys, of Lorence, and once Sea Marshal in the King of Orsena's court, an office he's lost to Doros Meredeon. He's the husband of Elise Polys, and hates her brother, Kade Asher, whom he captured and now has to watch succeed where his fleet couldn't. Leander hates Kade for what he is. He believes him to be a good-for-nothing killer, despite the fact that Kade saved Elise, without which Leander would never have married her at all. As Sea Marshal, he captured Kade and raided his old crew's hideout, and most of them escaped. See Capture. After Morowai burned, Kade was set to hunting his old crew down. Leander couldn't take them out with a fleet, and Kade, with next to nothing to his name, is dispatching them one by one. In a way, it's showing Leander up. He's since lost his office to Doros Meredeon.","n":""},{"t":"Lord Akrio Kuroshi","u":"wiki/lord-akrio-kuroshi.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A lord of the Kuroshi, thanked in Novon for the grain he gives out each winter. He's the son of Lord Hiro Kuroshi and Lady Emi Kuroshi, husband of Lady Sakuri Kuroshi, and father of Queen Kota Talvo. He gives out part of the winter grain that Kota sends to Nov","b":"A lord of the Kuroshi, thanked in Novon for the grain he gives out each winter. He's the son of Lord Hiro Kuroshi and Lady Emi Kuroshi, husband of Lady Sakuri Kuroshi, and father of Queen Kota Talvo. He gives out part of the winter grain that Kota sends to Novon in secret as his own, and takes the thanks.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Lord Commander Kazuro","u":"wiki/lord-commander-kazuro.html","a":["Lord Commander","Kazuro","Kazuro Kanakao"],"f":"Characters","c":"#b3332e","s":"Lord Commander of the Talvarian Knights, who serve the crown of Talvar, and the older brother of Jiro Kanakao.","b":"Lord Commander of the Talvarian Knights, who serve the crown of Talvar, and the older brother of Jiro Kanakao.","n":""},{"t":"Lord Hiro Kuroshi","u":"wiki/lord-hiro-kuroshi.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#b3332e","s":"The Opraxi lord who fled Oprax during its internal struggle, brought its refugees across the sea, and founded Novon. He helped King Cedric Talvo raise Trion, and his granddaughter, Kota, is queen of Talvar. He was the husband of Lady Emi Kuroshi, and father of","b":"The Opraxi lord who fled Oprax during its internal struggle, brought its refugees across the sea, and founded Novon. He helped King Cedric Talvo raise Trion, and his granddaughter, Kota, is queen of Talvar. He was the husband of Lady Emi Kuroshi, and father of Lord Akrio Kuroshi. The advisor to the old Tennō gave Hiro's name to clan Honda, which had an old feud against his house. He succeeded in saving his family, and refused ever to return. Opraxi weren't safe anywhere in Icos. With Gideon and King Cedric Talvo, Hiro brought refugees across the sea to Athura, so that they might live peacefully in new lands. They were there when the Talvos were killed, and came home to the dead. On Gideon's order, Hiro fled to Athura with Cedric and Onfroi. See The Massacre at Wietmoire. Hiro brought the refugees who built Novon and Greystone, and they lived under his protection and lordship. He asked only one thing of Cedric, that his granddaughter, Kota, be married to Cedric's son. See The Founding of Trion. He stood beside the boy in Karlaine, and said nothing when Aethel Zuul called his family lowborn. See The Crowning of Onfroi. His granddaughter, Kota, married King Onfroi Talvo and became queen of Talvar.","n":""},{"t":"Mandel Kalmere","u":"wiki/mandel-kalmere.html","a":["Mandel"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The late king of Stade, son of Tarris Kalmere and father of King Lanis Kalmere, who rules in 211 ABU. His elder child, Oriane, was passed over for her brother, and began the cadet line at Stade. Mandel carried himself with an eagerness that others found diffic","b":"The late king of Stade, son of Tarris Kalmere and father of King Lanis Kalmere, who rules in 211 ABU. His elder child, Oriane, was passed over for her brother, and began the cadet line at Stade. Mandel carried himself with an eagerness that others found difficult to respect, acutely conscious of the man standing above him. He wasn't always so. It began once the Zuuls ruled the High Sovereignty as a dynasty. He stood among his own courtiers in Karlaine while Aethel Zuul sat upon his throne and judged the Talvos. See The Crowning of Onfroi. A courtier of the High Crown came to Stade without sending word. Mandel rode out to meet them, so as not to seem late, and fell. Lanis became king after him.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marrec Kalmere","u":"wiki/marrec-kalmere.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The grandson of Oriane Kalmere, of the cadet line of the Kalmeres at Stade. His line comes after the king's daughters, Thea and Reine. Oriane has never once put him forward. Houses have been writing to him for years, and he doesn't know it. She reads the lette","b":"The grandson of Oriane Kalmere, of the cadet line of the Kalmeres at Stade. His line comes after the king's daughters, Thea and Reine. Oriane has never once put him forward. Houses have been writing to him for years, and he doesn't know it. She reads the letters first, and burns them.","n":""},{"t":"Marshal Ahmenkare","u":"wiki/marshal-ahmenkare.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal Ahtepa","u":"wiki/marshal-ahtepa.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal Akambim","u":"wiki/marshal-akambim.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal Akkennoten","u":"wiki/marshal-akkennoten.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"One of the twelve marshals of the kashif, and so admired that the children of Khamu go out of their way to meet him. He takes a group of them through the hunting trials of the Kashif Trials. Akkennoten watches and listens before he speaks. He sets traps rather","b":"One of the twelve marshals of the kashif, and so admired that the children of Khamu go out of their way to meet him. He takes a group of them through the hunting trials of the Kashif Trials. Akkennoten watches and listens before he speaks. He sets traps rather than rush into a hunt, and holds patience to be among the most important skills a kashif can have. He led Artemis, Afina, Ano, Aurel and Arya through the hunting trials. He had them climb to camp by their claws, and tested each of them with the bow before he'd take them into the jungle, telling them what their shot would have meant on a real hunt. He reminded them that companionship would be weighed alongside their kills, and that the highest score alone wouldn't make them fit to be kashif. \"Hunting isn't just about killing the biggest thing. If that's what you want, then being a kashif isn't for you.\" — to Aurel","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal Amayeta","u":"wiki/marshal-amayeta.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal Annonnoa","u":"wiki/marshal-annonnoa.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal Ashtaku","u":"wiki/marshal-ashtaku.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal Atalamun-ra","u":"wiki/marshal-atalamun-ra.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal Azparenhep","u":"wiki/marshal-azparenhep.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal T'Ahvos","u":"wiki/marshal-tahvos.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal T'Akoteh","u":"wiki/marshal-takoteh.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"One of the twelve marshals of the kashif, who makes a point of knowing as many of the Abunali as he can. Warm with the children he trains, he wishes his duties left him time for a family of his own. T'Akoteh is warm with the children he trains. He once woke a ","b":"One of the twelve marshals of the kashif, who makes a point of knowing as many of the Abunali as he can. Warm with the children he trains, he wishes his duties left him time for a family of his own. T'Akoteh is warm with the children he trains. He once woke a hut of them by tickling the youngest, and laughed at their shock. In the Kashif Trials, he led Artemis, Azra, Afina and Amori, settling the quarrel over who would lead by putting Azra in front and Artemis behind to look after the youngest. He warned them that being last gets you nowhere fast, then cut loose logs to send barreling at them from the trees. When the groups were reformed, he took charge of Azra, Amori and their friends, and Elder Azhotep was impressed with them at the anointing. \"A Kashif must serve and protect the land and people of the Abunali. Knowing as many people as possible is just a part of the title.\" — to Amori","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marshal T'Anzu","u":"wiki/marshal-tanzu.html","a":["T'Anzu","Kashif Marshal T'Anzu"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Midori","u":"wiki/midori.html","a":["Mother Midori","Yaya"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The keeper of an orphanage in Kama, known to the children she raises as \"Yaya.\" Artemis, Afina and Azra are among them. When she's introduced, the orphanage has fallen on hard times, and she's struggling to provide for those who depend on her. Midori hums to h","b":"The keeper of an orphanage in Kama, known to the children she raises as \"Yaya.\" Artemis, Afina and Azra are among them. When she's introduced, the orphanage has fallen on hard times, and she's struggling to provide for those who depend on her. Midori hums to herself as she works. She's gentle with children and stern when she has to be, and once faced down an angry baker with nothing but a washboard. She was treated differently herself at a young age, and believes mistakes are lessons best learned early. Her orphanage stood near Kama's lake, far from the market road, beneath a hand-crafted sign in pumesh that read \"Little Helpers,\" and below it, \"Mother Midori's Orphanage.\" She would gather the children for nights of storytelling. The older ones earned money around town, and she wrote letters to the new king for aid. Though she didn't trust Tibs, she relied on him to look after the children and bring them food. Once Kama fell under Talvar, the mayor could no longer fund the orphanage. Midori quarreled with Tibs over the errands he had been sending her children on, turned down his offer of money, and left Kama with Artemis, Afina and Azra. Elder Antekano let them stay in Khamu, where Midori taught the villagers and asked for nothing in return. Her lessons drew on a map of the known world, written in hyenesh, which her family had passed down and revised for generations. At Antekano's urging, she let the children enter the Kashif Trials, so long as they looked after one another. \"You raise your hand to him again, and I'll show you what a true problem looks like!\" — to the baker of Kama \"I don't expect you to up and decide to become a king, but I want you to do something that makes you feel like you belong.\" — to Artemis, in Khamu","n":""},{"t":"Milun","u":"wiki/milun.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The late wife of Guryeg, chieftain of The Hunto, and mother of Gartan and Khusan. After Guryeg's death, she was taken as a prize by Nuko, the new chieftain of The Hunto, merely a late chieftain's wife turned concubine. She died attempting to perform a blood sa","b":"The late wife of Guryeg, chieftain of The Hunto, and mother of Gartan and Khusan. After Guryeg's death, she was taken as a prize by Nuko, the new chieftain of The Hunto, merely a late chieftain's wife turned concubine. She died attempting to perform a blood sacrifice with her unborn child, fathered by Nuko, to bring her family back. See Blood Magic.","n":""},{"t":"Mirak-Yen'iq Sipa","u":"wiki/mirak-yeniq-sipa.html","a":["Mirak","Old Hermit","Master"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"An old hermit of Mandor Canyon, in the deserts of Bali, about eighty-nine years old, who keeps a shop of herbs, elixirs and oddities in a cave there. He's one of the last of the jotuun, which he keeps an absolute secret, and he hopes to bring the order back, b","b":"An old hermit of Mandor Canyon, in the deserts of Bali, about eighty-nine years old, who keeps a shop of herbs, elixirs and oddities in a cave there. He's one of the last of the jotuun, which he keeps an absolute secret, and he hopes to bring the order back, but has no one to pass his knowledge on to. Mirak is native to Athura. He was in the order by the time the Ten-Year War began, at the Temple of Khada, far from home, and couldn't have known what became of his family. During the war, he left the temple without leave to find them, and it fell while he was gone. He found the house empty, and came back to a sealed granite door. Those who'd stayed to protect their home were behind it. He never learned what became of his family. By the war's end he'd lost everything, and he became the secret apprentice of Ayala. He was one of the seven jotuun who hid with her in Qis, and was there when Aethel Zuul found them. Ayala died protecting him near Medjet (see Ayala). It's the reason he's alive at all, and why he won't waste it. He remains in Mandor Canyon, but relocates every so often. He keeps his past and involvement with the jotuun an absolute secret, as Alfor Zuul carries on his grandfather's legacy.","n":""},{"t":"Mortis Barrentyne","u":"wiki/mortis-barrentyne.html","a":["Mortis"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A lord of the Barrentyne, an Azkassari noble house of Norvania, father of Loranne, and grandfather of Reine. He took a calculated risk in marrying Loranne to King Lanis Kalmere, and means to seat his blood on the Stadian throne.","b":"A lord of the Barrentyne, an Azkassari noble house of Norvania, father of Loranne, and grandfather of Reine. He took a calculated risk in marrying Loranne to King Lanis Kalmere, and means to seat his blood on the Stadian throne.","n":""},{"t":"Nephele","u":"wiki/nephele.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The late first wife of King Lanis Kalmere, of the Oristene, and mother of Thea. She died shortly after childbirth and naming her daughter, who has her looks. When Lanis came to Dremmel as a new king, the whole hall rose but her. Her house recognizes no hierarc","b":"The late first wife of King Lanis Kalmere, of the Oristene, and mother of Thea. She died shortly after childbirth and naming her daughter, who has her looks. When Lanis came to Dremmel as a new king, the whole hall rose but her. Her house recognizes no hierarchy at home. She asked him what he thought of something, and waited until he answered, which nobody had.","n":""},{"t":"Niles Abbot","u":"wiki/niles-abbot.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A spoiled and prideful servant of Trion Palace, about forty-three years old, earning his keep in the hope of one day becoming a lord. Cho Abbot, the queen's handmaiden, is his half-sister.","b":"A spoiled and prideful servant of Trion Palace, about forty-three years old, earning his keep in the hope of one day becoming a lord. Cho Abbot, the queen's handmaiden, is his half-sister.","n":""},{"t":"Nirvaad","u":"wiki/nirvaad.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"An old farmer, about seventy-six years old, who works his farm on the bank of the Mes-Naila River with his wife. His income is constantly threatened, and he's a debt to repay.","b":"An old farmer, about seventy-six years old, who works his farm on the bank of the Mes-Naila River with his wife. His income is constantly threatened, and he's a debt to repay.","n":""},{"t":"Nuko","u":"wiki/nuko.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The chieftain of The Hunto after Guryeg, until he was defeated by Khusan, who challenged him for the title of chief. He took Guryeg's wife, Milun, as a prize and concubine, and fathered her unborn child.","b":"The chieftain of The Hunto after Guryeg, until he was defeated by Khusan, who challenged him for the title of chief. He took Guryeg's wife, Milun, as a prize and concubine, and fathered her unborn child.","n":""},{"t":"Oriane Kalmere","u":"wiki/oriane-kalmere.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The elder sister of King Lanis Kalmere, about eighty-six years old, who was passed over for him. The daughter of Mandel Kalmere, she holds the old castle of Stade, where she began the cadet line, and is the one person Lanis can't order to say what she thinks. ","b":"The elder sister of King Lanis Kalmere, about eighty-six years old, who was passed over for him. The daughter of Mandel Kalmere, she holds the old castle of Stade, where she began the cadet line, and is the one person Lanis can't order to say what she thinks. Anteo Zuul wanted a Kalmere for his heir, Archon. Oriane was the only choice, and she was twelve years younger than him. Archon married Dendrilla Ariadne instead. She was an only child until she was about eighteen, when Lanis was born, and she was passed over for him. As of 211 ABU, she still holds the castle, and is the one person Lanis can't order to say what she thinks. Her line comes after his daughters, Thea and Reine. She has a grandson, Marrec, and has never once put him forward. Houses have been writing to him for years, and she reads the letters first, and burns them. She and Lanis are close. He tells her what he tells nobody else, and she's the only one who knows what he'd say if he had to name an heir. See Houses of Stade.","n":""},{"t":"Prince Amersi","u":"wiki/prince-amersi.html","a":["Prince Amersi of Suran","The Son of Suran","The Fallen Prince"],"f":"Characters","c":"#a8860b","s":"The twenty-one-year-old prince of Suran, called the Fallen Prince. The Keiran Clan killed his father, King Arvaan, took his city, and captured him alongside his mother, Queen Ai, whom he means to protect while he reclaims what they've lost.","b":"The twenty-one-year-old prince of Suran, called the Fallen Prince. The Keiran Clan killed his father, King Arvaan, took his city, and captured him alongside his mother, Queen Ai, whom he means to protect while he reclaims what they've lost.","n":""},{"t":"Prince Rand Talvo","u":"wiki/prince-rand-talvo.html","a":["Rand"],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The eldest son of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo of Talvar, who vanished while accompanying a shipment to Crage.","b":"The eldest son of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo of Talvar, who vanished while accompanying a shipment to Crage.","n":""},{"t":"Prince Theron Damaris","u":"wiki/prince-theron-damaris.html","a":["Theron Damaris","Theron"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Prince of Ibya, of the house of Damaris, seated at Pavilia. He's one of the three princes of Stade, sworn directly to its king. With the king refusing to name an heir, he leans toward Thea.","b":"The Prince of Ibya, of the house of Damaris, seated at Pavilia. He's one of the three princes of Stade, sworn directly to its king. With the king refusing to name an heir, he leans toward Thea.","n":""},{"t":"Princess Adja","u":"wiki/princess-adja.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The princess of Sulala, and daughter of King Ahaad.","b":"The princess of Sulala, and daughter of King Ahaad.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Princess Koto Talvo","u":"wiki/princess-koto-talvo.html","a":["Princess","Koto","Little Talvo"],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The nineteen-year-old princess of Talvar, and the youngest child of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo. With her siblings dead, missing, or married away, she's the last of them at court in Trion, and speaks for the capital as its emissary. Brave and short-","b":"The nineteen-year-old princess of Talvar, and the youngest child of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo. With her siblings dead, missing, or married away, she's the last of them at court in Trion, and speaks for the capital as its emissary. Brave and short-fused, she wants control over her own life. As a child, she was carried through Kama in a carriage escorted by Talvarian Knights, not long after the town came under Talvar. Her hair was worn in a traditional opraxi style, and the townsfolk had never seen anyone like her. She returned about three years later, and spoke to the crowd herself. She's been an emissary of her house since she was six, first as a figurehead, and since she was twelve with leave to speak on the capital's behalf. She writes to her sisters every season, though their last reply came four years ago.","n":""},{"t":"Princess Reine Kalmere","u":"wiki/princess-reine-kalmere.html","a":["Reine"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The younger daughter of King Lanis Kalmere of Stade, by Queen Loranne Kalmere, and half-sister of Thea. Of Azkassari descent through her mother, she has Norvania and the queen in her favor, while her father refuses to name an heir. Reine has her mother's looks","b":"The younger daughter of King Lanis Kalmere of Stade, by Queen Loranne Kalmere, and half-sister of Thea. Of Azkassari descent through her mother, she has Norvania and the queen in her favor, while her father refuses to name an heir. Reine has her mother's looks: pale, with black hair streaked with bronze, and red eyes. She and Thea look nothing alike. Reine was taught from a young age to be more empathetic than the more traditional members of her mother's family. Her father refuses to name an heir. Reine has Norvania and the queen in her favor, with the Barrentynes leaning toward her, and the Larrensires at a price. See Houses of Stade.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Princess Thea Kalmere","u":"wiki/princess-thea-kalmere.html","a":["Thea"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The sixteen-year-old elder daughter of King Lanis Kalmere of Stade, and half-sister of Reine. Her mother, Nephele, an Ibyan woman and his first wife, died shortly after childbirth and naming her. Thea has age and the Ibyan coast in her favor, while her father ","b":"The sixteen-year-old elder daughter of King Lanis Kalmere of Stade, and half-sister of Reine. Her mother, Nephele, an Ibyan woman and his first wife, died shortly after childbirth and naming her. Thea has age and the Ibyan coast in her favor, while her father refuses to name an heir. Thea has her mother's looks: an olive complexion, wavy dark brown hair, and brown eyes. Lanis has never made peace with how much of Nephele he sees in her. Thea accuses Reine of \"believing she's better than everyone else,\" being jealous herself. Her father refuses to name an heir. Thea has age and the Ibyan coast in her favor, with the Vaskers and her mother's kin, the Oristenes, leaning toward her. She's never been sure if the Oristenes love her or the claim. See Houses of Stade.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Prior Ad Regalis","u":"wiki/prior-ad-regalis.html","a":["Ad Regalis","Ad","Regalis"],"f":"Characters","c":"#c9671b","s":"A prior of the jotuun, and the master of Augmen, who died in the last siege of the Ten-Year War. Ad Regalis wore his tawny hair in a ponytail, and kept a short beard. He was precise and diplomatic, loyal to the grand uthisha, and understanding with those who c","b":"A prior of the jotuun, and the master of Augmen, who died in the last siege of the Ten-Year War. Ad Regalis wore his tawny hair in a ponytail, and kept a short beard. He was precise and diplomatic, loyal to the grand uthisha, and understanding with those who came to him for help. When Endelyn Larrensire came to plead for assistance, the jotuun were already planning to intervene, and he told her she could accompany him and Augmen. The jotuun saved individuals on both sides of the battle. See The Death of Archon. He warned Augmen against speaking out when his daughter, Ayala, was offered a place on Icos, and Augmen backed down. He was killed by Augmen at the Temple of Isi. See Ten-Year War. \"You should be proud, Augmen. Your daughter has been presented with an opportunity some of us will never even get the chance to have. This is for the best.\" — to Augmen","n":""},{"t":"Prior Angkor","u":"wiki/prior-angkor.html","a":["Prior","Angkor"],"f":"Characters","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Prior Barth","u":"wiki/prior-barth.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Prior Taka Nosa Reth","u":"wiki/prior-taka-nosa-reth.html","a":["Prior","Taka","Taka Nosa Reth"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Queen Ai","u":"wiki/queen-ai.html","a":["Queen Ai of Suran"],"f":"Characters","c":"#a8860b","s":"The widowed queen of Suran, about forty-two years old, captured with her son, Prince Amersi, when the Keiran Clan killed King Arvaan and took the city. She hopes to reclaim her family's place in it.","b":"The widowed queen of Suran, about forty-two years old, captured with her son, Prince Amersi, when the Keiran Clan killed King Arvaan and took the city. She hopes to reclaim her family's place in it.","n":""},{"t":"Queen Kaelyn Skyfall","u":"wiki/queen-kaelyn-skyfall.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The wife of King Gideon Talvo and mother of King Cedric Talvo, killed alongside her husband after The Massacre at Wietmoire. In 211 ABU her grandson, Onfroi, is king of Talvar. She was taken from Wietmoire Manor on the night her family was killed, while her hu","b":"The wife of King Gideon Talvo and mother of King Cedric Talvo, killed alongside her husband after The Massacre at Wietmoire. In 211 ABU her grandson, Onfroi, is king of Talvar. She was taken from Wietmoire Manor on the night her family was killed, while her husband and son were away, and was brought before Gideon at High Sonham. Anteo Zuul offered to let her live if he knelt, and she told him not to. He'd sworn to the hilltribes that no Talvo would kneel again unless they said so, and she made sure he remembered, even at the cost of her life. She was made to watch Gideon die, and Anteo then beat and strangled her in the very same room. See The Massacre at Wietmoire.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Queen Kota Talvo","u":"wiki/queen-kota-talvo.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#b3332e","s":"The queen of the Kingdom of Talvar, seventy-three years old, and wife of King Onfroi Talvo. Born a Kuroshi, she's the daughter of Lord Akrio Kuroshi and Lady Sakuri Kuroshi, and granddaughter of Lord Hiro Kuroshi, who fled Oprax with his family. She keeps orde","b":"The queen of the Kingdom of Talvar, seventy-three years old, and wife of King Onfroi Talvo. Born a Kuroshi, she's the daughter of Lord Akrio Kuroshi and Lady Sakuri Kuroshi, and granddaughter of Lord Hiro Kuroshi, who fled Oprax with his family. She keeps order in Trion and provides for Koto, the last of her children left to her, while her grandfather's people in Novon have come to hate her. Kota has long, black, flowing hair and grey eyes. She's intelligent and lighthearted, yet authoritarian when it comes to order in Trion. When it's absolutely necessary to speak up, she fails to. Her hair and attire are in the care of her handmaiden, Cho Abbot. She was betrothed to Onfroi as a child, at her grandfather's request. Cedric turned down the High King's own daughter to keep his word. See The Crowning of Onfroi. The year the taxes rose, the people of Novon, her grandfather's refugees, came to the gate of Trion and called for her by her family's name, not the king's. She stood at a window and couldn't make herself go down. The guards cleared the gate, and they've never called for her since. She still knows what's right, even when she can't bring herself to confront Onfroi to make the lives of those under the crown easier. What she can't say, she sends. It's split between Cho, her father, and a merchant of Trion who's paid not to wonder, as more than one name looks less suspicious than one bulk expenditure on a ledger. Her father gives his part out as his own, and takes the thanks. Novon doesn't know that she pays for its winter grain, and hates her all the same. Onfroi found it years ago, and has the treasury round the numbers off. Neither has ever said a word about it to the other.","n":""},{"t":"Queen Loranne Kalmere","u":"wiki/queen-loranne-kalmere.html","a":["Loranne"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The queen of Stade, second wife of King Lanis Kalmere, and mother of Reine. Her father, Mortis Barrentyne, married her to the king to seat his blood on the throne, though she shares little of her family's pride. Loranne is of Azkassari descent, and Azkassari i","b":"The queen of Stade, second wife of King Lanis Kalmere, and mother of Reine. Her father, Mortis Barrentyne, married her to the king to seat his blood on the throne, though she shares little of her family's pride. Loranne is of Azkassari descent, and Azkassari in appearance, but unlike what her family would expect, she appreciates others' differences. Her marriage to Lanis was one of duty rather than love, her father, Mortis Barrentyne, taking a calculated risk to seat his blood on the Stadian throne. Though, Lanis grew fond of her, as she seemed to understand his melancholy.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Queen Milera Savvad","u":"wiki/queen-milera-savvad.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Queen Vaenera","u":"wiki/queen-vaenera.html","a":["Queen","Queen Vaenera Aelia Ariadne","Vaenera"],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The young queen of Phylomina, about twenty-one years old, and the daughter of Aiore and Alexium Ariadne, both of whom she's lost. She rules under the guidance of her mentor, the High King, Alfor Zuul, while the city's upper class try to sway her. Though her si","b":"The young queen of Phylomina, about twenty-one years old, and the daughter of Aiore and Alexium Ariadne, both of whom she's lost. She rules under the guidance of her mentor, the High King, Alfor Zuul, while the city's upper class try to sway her. Though her sister, Azra, was believed to have died when Morowai burned, rumors have led her to suspect otherwise. Vaenera has long, curly, medium blonde hair and green eyes. She's upright, rational, and well-spoken, with a streak of the perfectionist. Many of the city's upper class know she's still quite young and assume she's gullible, and some try to sway her judgment with bribes. She resents pirates and scoundrels alike, and becomes very emotional when her lost sister is brought up. She came to the city as a very young daughter of its new king. When her mother found an infant dra'iq in the jungle, it was Vaenera, no older than seven, who asked to keep it. See The Fall of Malu. She and Azra grew up together in Morowai before moving to Phylomina, and grew closer still during the visits Azra was allowed after Alexium sent her away. When Morowai was burned, Vaenera and her family assumed Azra was dead. Rumors have since led her to suspect otherwise, and she always insists on knowing whether there's a mole on the woman's chin. See Vaenera & Azra's Origin. Her father died of a fall from the palace, and she blames herself for not seeing the signs of a grief that didn't kill him (see Alexium Ariadne). When her mother died, about a year after, she first began to lean on Alfor Zuul. He's her mentor, and cannot have a legitimate heir. She doesn't know that he was behind her father's death. Vaenera doesn't know that she's of Zuul blood. The truth is in her father's storage: the reply that Aethel Zuul wrote to Dendrilla Ariadne. Alfor has the letter it answers. Vaenera & Azra's Origin","n":""},{"t":"Rost Talvo","u":"wiki/rost-talvo.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The youngest son of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo of Talvar. Sweet but reckless, he longed for battle so much that he fled from home in the dead of night, and he's been missing since.","b":"The youngest son of King Onfroi Talvo and Queen Kota Talvo of Talvar. Sweet but reckless, he longed for battle so much that he fled from home in the dead of night, and he's been missing since.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Ruler of the Gurtai","u":"wiki/ruler-of-the-gurtai.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The mysterious ruler behind the rumored unification of the Gurtai tribes. Their identity is unknown across Artisans. The ruler is Khusan, whose note holds the full story.","b":"The mysterious ruler behind the rumored unification of the Gurtai tribes. Their identity is unknown across Artisans. The ruler is Khusan, whose note holds the full story.","n":""},{"t":"S'Afar","u":"wiki/safar.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Leader of Neru'Augma and practitioner of quintecrakin.","b":"Leader of Neru'Augma and practitioner of quintecrakin.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Sabba","u":"wiki/sabba.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A friend of Prince Archon Zuul, whose death to a titan drove the prince to the vengeance that killed him. See The Death of Archon. In the cold north with Archon, Sabba ambushed an encampment of Morgari and titans, using plastite to collapse the cave they were ","b":"A friend of Prince Archon Zuul, whose death to a titan drove the prince to the vengeance that killed him. See The Death of Archon. In the cold north with Archon, Sabba ambushed an encampment of Morgari and titans, using plastite to collapse the cave they were holding out in. The titans rose from the debris, and Sabba was caught before the two could flee. Archon's vengeance for it led to his own death. See The Death of Archon.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Severin Larrensire","u":"wiki/severin-larrensire.html","a":["Lord Severin Larrensire","Prince Severin Larrensire"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Prince of Moirach and lord of Endlemoire, of the Larrensire, and father of Evander, whom he means to marry off. He's the nephew of Endelyn Larrensire, who was High Queen, and so kin to the High King, Alfor Zuul.","b":"The Prince of Moirach and lord of Endlemoire, of the Larrensire, and father of Evander, whom he means to marry off. He's the nephew of Endelyn Larrensire, who was High Queen, and so kin to the High King, Alfor Zuul.","n":""},{"t":"Shaman Ankti","u":"wiki/shaman-ankti.html","a":["Shaman","Ankti"],"f":"Characters","c":"#c9671b","s":"A shaman of the Abunali in Dunis, about sixty-seven years old, who was given temporary eldership over the village when Elder A'Te died at the start of 211 ABU. Once a kashif, she seeks to give people guidance through the gods. She's the twin sister of Abasse T","b":"A shaman of the Abunali in Dunis, about sixty-seven years old, who was given temporary eldership over the village when Elder A'Te died at the start of 211 ABU. Once a kashif, she seeks to give people guidance through the gods. She's the twin sister of Abasse The Seer, whom she doesn't acknowledge unless she has to. Ankti has long, shaggy, bronze hair, and her kashif markings have faded beside her shaman tattoos. She's dignified and honest, if petty, and ignores personal connections for fear of losing her spiritual one. She seeks to give people guidance through the gods. She grew cold and distant toward her mother, choosing to pursue her ambitions of becoming a kashif. After her mother's passing, she felt an overwhelming guilt, and attempted to reconnect with Abasse. It didn't last, and the two don't acknowledge each other unless they have to. See Abasse The Seer. She was the closest friend of Elder A'Te, Dunis's matriarch. When A'Te died at the start of 211 ABU, Ankti was given temporary eldership until a successor could be anointed.","n":""},{"t":"Tarris Kalmere","u":"wiki/tarris-kalmere.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The first High King of the Bicontinental Union, and the king of Stade who took Ōnari and Kabejō Garrison from Oprax and the west from the Talvos. Long dead by 211 ABU, he was the father of Mandel Kalmere, and grandfather of King Lanis Kalmere, who rules Stade ","b":"The first High King of the Bicontinental Union, and the king of Stade who took Ōnari and Kabejō Garrison from Oprax and the west from the Talvos. Long dead by 211 ABU, he was the father of Mandel Kalmere, and grandfather of King Lanis Kalmere, who rules Stade now. As a young king, Tarris declared the Talvos guilty of treason in the wake of a dra'iq, and spared them when the last king in the west, Conrad Talvo, knelt. By the fall of their crown, the west became his by law. To do it, he burned the charter that had released them from his house's service. See The Fall of Talvar. During his rule, Stade took Ōnari and Kabejō Garrison from Oprax, which was unable to strategically supply either. It was a victory his successor would never match. See Anteo's Failure. The title of High Sovereign was created with him. The Union he first led forbade the predatory seizure of weakened territory. Anteo Zuul was voted in after him.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Blood Empress","u":"wiki/the-blood-empress.html","a":["Blood Empress"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The empress of Oprax, known as 'the Blood Empress' all across Artisans. Within Oprax, to speak the name results in death. She's rumored to practice Blood Magic, a practice forbidden for as long as Oprax has existed. If the rumor is true, it's believed the empr","b":"The empress of Oprax, known as 'the Blood Empress' all across Artisans. Within Oprax, to speak the name results in death. She's rumored to practice Blood Magic, a practice forbidden for as long as Oprax has existed. If the rumor is true, it's believed the empress might have secured a pact with the Gurtai, a potentially dangerous revelation for the stability of Stade. She's the daughter of the late Emperor, and the granddaughter of the rebel daimyo who became Tennō (see Internal Struggle). The rumors are true. She grew up alongside Khusan, the hostage who became the Ruler of the Gurtai. Seeing how dangerous he is and what he has accomplished, she secured an alliance by citing their fleeting friendship when they were younger, and gained access to blood magic through him and his courtiers.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Theara the Jotuun","u":"wiki/theara-the-jotuun.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"One of the last surviving jotuun of her day, and the first guardian of Kiro, whom she raised in hiding. She's dead by 211 ABU. Hidden away in a temple, Theara discovered a nascent form of kinmatter, elektrokin. Kiro was entrusted to her as an infant by his mot","b":"One of the last surviving jotuun of her day, and the first guardian of Kiro, whom she raised in hiding. She's dead by 211 ABU. Hidden away in a temple, Theara discovered a nascent form of kinmatter, elektrokin. Kiro was entrusted to her as an infant by his mother, an opraxi refugee in Upror who'd heard of someone kind in the ruins. He began learning elektrokin in Theara's care. She used her lightning once, to protect Kiro. It may not even have been seen, but lightning is loud, and Inquisitors are always on the prowl. People talk, and rumors spread. Alfor discovered her whereabouts and sought her out, discreetly accompanied by his Inquisitors. He confronted her on her loyalties, and upon confirming her unwillingness to cooperate, ordered her execution. See Kiro.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Tibs","u":"wiki/tibs.html","a":["Mr. Tibs","Uncle Tibs"],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A thief, about sixty-two years old, who has the children of Midori's orphanage call him \"Uncle Tibs.\" He leads a gang made up largely of those same children, grown older, and means to establish a crime syndicate and become wealthy. Tibs is jovial in company, a","b":"A thief, about sixty-two years old, who has the children of Midori's orphanage call him \"Uncle Tibs.\" He leads a gang made up largely of those same children, grown older, and means to establish a crime syndicate and become wealthy. Tibs is jovial in company, and drops it once he's alone with the person he wants something from. He flatters, deflects, and answers questions with questions. He came and went from Kama, bringing the orphans meals too fine to have been prepared locally, and Midori left them in his care despite her distrust. He persuaded Artemis to work jobs for him out of town by telling him it would help her, and asked only that he not tell her. The two traveled to Sukara, Kilan, Shur'iq, Pa'iq and Wapiti. He rescued Azra when Morowai was raided, and brought her to Kama. See Vaenera & Azra's Origin. When the orphanage fell on hard times, he returned after four months away, offering money. Midori had learned of the errands he sent her children on, and left Kama without taking it. He took in Artemis and Azra after the Kashif Trials, telling the elders of the Abunali that he had a place in Trion. His gang made its home in the ruins of an old temple, where he called the two his favorite prodigies. \"I'm willing to make this a family business, Midori!\" — as she left Kama Vaenera & Azra's Origin","n":""},{"t":"Tybal Zuul","u":"wiki/tybal-zuul.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The first born son of Catalina Valentyre and the third High King, Aethel Zuul, and brother of Aeleina and Alfor. He was the heir to the High Throne until his ship was lost to Nirva, nearly four decades before 211 ABU, on its way to mend his family's feud with ","b":"The first born son of Catalina Valentyre and the third High King, Aethel Zuul, and brother of Aeleina and Alfor. He was the heir to the High Throne until his ship was lost to Nirva, nearly four decades before 211 ABU, on its way to mend his family's feud with the Talvos. Tybal was broader than his brother and father, with short, black hair, blue eyes, and a sharp jawline. In his mid-teens, he began to grow stubble earlier than the rest of his family, similar to Archon. And also like Archon, many women considered him handsome. Before he sailed, Alfor met with him in private, trying to probe what he truly thought of the feud, their father, and Anteo. Tybal told him the truth. Anteo was a tyrant who bent laws to get what he wanted, and Aethel was at least trying to play by them. Afterward, he smiled, and invited Alfor to come along. Alfor refused. Tybal became friends by letter with King Onfroi Talvo. Aethel proposed that he visit, it was discussed among the household, and Onfroi welcomed his arrival, inviting him to come and see what Aethel's mercy had built. He sailed for Trion as his father's envoy, to help mend the feud with the Talvos. His ship was among the first that Nirva took, and nothing was found. He died before both his parents, so the High Throne passed to Alfor.","n":""},{"t":"V'Ain Dueval","u":"wiki/vain-dueval.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The founder and first King of the The Dueval Family.","b":"The founder and first King of the The Dueval Family.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Yanna","u":"wiki/yanna.html","a":[],"f":"Characters","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Acolyte of Neru'Augma; practices Jeokin, Hidrokin, and Mnemokin.","b":"Acolyte of Neru'Augma; practices Jeokin, Hidrokin, and Mnemokin.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Abunali","u":"wiki/abunali.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Placeholder Description Also known as the Tan'we Abunali. Also known as the Abunali Islanders. TBD","b":"Placeholder Description Also known as the Tan'we Abunali. Also known as the Abunali Islanders. TBD","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Athuran Sultanate","u":"wiki/athuran-sultanate.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Divided before 159 BBU. See Athuran Groll Wastings.","b":"Divided before 159 BBU. See Athuran Groll Wastings.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Barrentyne","u":"wiki/barrentyne.html","a":["House Barrentyne"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"An Azkassari noble house, seated at Norvania. The Barrentynes want Reine crowned and grateful, but she was raised to be neither proud nor usable. They would absolutely hate to see her marry a Larrensire, which would be stealing their victory from them, but the","b":"An Azkassari noble house, seated at Norvania. The Barrentynes want Reine crowned and grateful, but she was raised to be neither proud nor usable. They would absolutely hate to see her marry a Larrensire, which would be stealing their victory from them, but the need for men would give them very little choice. See Houses of Stade. Mortis Barrentyne Loranne, by birth","n":""},{"t":"Boryign","u":"wiki/boryign.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A clan of The Hunto tribe of the Gurtai. Guryeg Milun Gartan Khusan","b":"A clan of The Hunto tribe of the Gurtai. Guryeg Milun Gartan Khusan","n":""},{"t":"Crannoch","u":"wiki/crannoch.html","a":["House Crannoch"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Bogwatch. Of Moirish blood: short, stubborn, and older here than anyone. Their people lived on these moors before Endlemoire or Linnenmoire, and they keep the only safe paths through the bog. The Crannochs are a sour bunch who","b":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Bogwatch. Of Moirish blood: short, stubborn, and older here than anyone. Their people lived on these moors before Endlemoire or Linnenmoire, and they keep the only safe paths through the bog. The Crannochs are a sour bunch who lack the sophistication and etiquette of the more refined houses, but their importance to the realm should not be understated. Bogwatch and Moirlander's Respite are key holdings to keeping the western tribes of Upror away from the more civilized kingdoms in the east, and the same goes vice versa. They receive any guests who wish to speak with them at the Respite, which they raised. The Crannochs want to be left alone, but armies that walk the moors have to answer to those who hold and know them, and every army crossing from west to east needs a guide. What's asked of them is circumstantial. It depends on who sits at Bogwatch, who their visitors are, and the reason for staying, among other factors. Generally, they're welcoming people, but there have been Crannochs in the past who've leveraged their importance beyond what is necessary, either for financial or other personal reasons. They don't kiss the ground that kings walk on. They and the Talvos had made peace with the hilltribes by the time Talvar was founded, hence why the hilltribes allowed the city to be built quietly (see The Fall of Talvar). As of 211 ABU, an old man, Colum Crannoch, sits at Bogwatch, and he's welcoming to certain guests: those who were friends to the Talvos. His father, Tormod Crannoch, let Gideon Talvo west through the moors to apologize to the hilltribes. When the tribes began bringing Gideon their feuds in secret, they brought them to the Respite, where west and east could both sit down without anyone asking why. The old man was a boy then, carrying bowls, and is the last person alive in the east who saw Gideon judge. Onfroi has never met him, and nobody has told him there's a man alive who heard his grandfather speak. Colum hasn't gone to him either. A Crannoch doesn't go to kings, so the door is open and it's Onfroi's to walk through. Colum believes him to be \"as stuck up as the Stadian nobles he kisses up to.\" He's partly right. Onfroi does court the Stadian lords, but because his crown was granted rather than chosen, and he needs every friend he can get. Colum mistakes need for pride. His father never relayed any of it to the Kalmeres, who found out from the quiet in the west (see Talvo Royal Family). See Houses of Stade. Colum Crannoch, lord of Bogwatch as of 211 ABU, welcoming to friends of the Talvos Tormod Crannoch, his father, who let Gideon through the moors Torcall Crannoch, his grandfather","n":""},{"t":"Damaris","u":"wiki/damaris.html","a":["House Damaris","The Damaris"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Pavilia in the Ibyan Isles, and the Princes of Ibya, sworn directly to the king (see Titles and Ranks). They've held the title since Ibya reformed its governance, when the greatest city-state holder, at Pavilia, was made Princ","b":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Pavilia in the Ibyan Isles, and the Princes of Ibya, sworn directly to the king (see Titles and Ranks). They've held the title since Ibya reformed its governance, when the greatest city-state holder, at Pavilia, was made Prince over the rest (see Ibya). They lean toward Thea. See Houses of Stade. Prince Theron Damaris, Prince of Ibya","n":""},{"t":"Ermsburry","u":"wiki/ermsburry.html","a":["House Ermsburry","The Ermsburrys"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A lesser noble house of Stade, holding Endletonn under the Larrensires of Endlemoire. King Onfroi Talvo is their kin through his mother, Lady Haileen Ermsburry, who was kept with them after the massacre at Wietmoire Manor. They'd have hanged if she left, and s","b":"A lesser noble house of Stade, holding Endletonn under the Larrensires of Endlemoire. King Onfroi Talvo is their kin through his mother, Lady Haileen Ermsburry, who was kept with them after the massacre at Wietmoire Manor. They'd have hanged if she left, and she stayed. Onfroi spends a night with them every year, on the way to the Royal Conclave, in her room. See Houses of Stade.","n":""},{"t":"Gurtai","u":"wiki/gurtai.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Gurtai are a nomadic people of southwestern Icos, much older than the bloodlines of the modern stadian and opraxi, and far removed from the icosi that later came to the continent. They're a robust atlae, and as stubborn as they are old. The Gurtai once liv","b":"The Gurtai are a nomadic people of southwestern Icos, much older than the bloodlines of the modern stadian and opraxi, and far removed from the icosi that later came to the continent. They're a robust atlae, and as stubborn as they are old. The Gurtai once lived along the south of the continent, especially in the fertile basin where modern Oprax resides. There they warred with the proto-Opraxi. Though the more robust of the two, the Gurtai weren't as organized, nor as experienced in fighting the diverse enemies the proto-Opraxi had grown accustomed to. Over a couple of centuries their numbers dwindled, as tribes either migrated west or burned. See The War for the Basin. The lands to the southwest weren't newly populated by this. The Gurtai had ventured that way for generations during seasonal migrations, but now they were forced to remain. It wasn't all war and chaos. Some interbreeding occurred, whether through peace treaties or prisoners of war, and some Opraxi carry Gurtai blood. For the long stretch between then and now, the tribes remained independent. Yet whispers pass through many ears to higher courts, speaking of a great unification of the tribes: a coalition so great that it could threaten the stability of Oprax and Stade alike, should they choose aggression as their first act. It's unknown who their mysterious ruler is, but they must be quite an imposing person to force order among a people as stubborn as the Gurtai. Blood Magic, forbidden in Oprax for as long as it has existed, is freely practiced among Gurtai shamans. It's rumored that The Blood Empress practices it as well. If so, it's believed she might have secured a pact with the Gurtai. The Hunto The Tughai The Shume When they form a coalition, they become The Gurtai Horde. Khag Ugyar (Neutral ground) Tenbet (The Hunto) Zam Gurtuz (The Tughai) Orlag (The Shume)","n":""},{"t":"Hallorn","u":"wiki/hallorn.html","a":["House Hallorn"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Mory, where it holds the road between Karlaine and Morgar. Of Ibyan blood. Loyal to the king to a fault, the Hallorns pray for a prince in public and want things to stay as they are. Their whole position depends on a child who","b":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Mory, where it holds the road between Karlaine and Morgar. Of Ibyan blood. Loyal to the king to a fault, the Hallorns pray for a prince in public and want things to stay as they are. Their whole position depends on a child who doesn't exist. See Houses of Stade.","n":""},{"t":"Highguard","u":"wiki/highguard.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A sworn guard, based in High Sonham, where those who join must relocate. Highguard swear an oath of taking no wife and bearing no child, and are forbidden from speaking to anyone, being aggressively formal. They escort monarchs to High Sonham for the Royal Con","b":"A sworn guard, based in High Sonham, where those who join must relocate. Highguard swear an oath of taking no wife and bearing no child, and are forbidden from speaking to anyone, being aggressively formal. They escort monarchs to High Sonham for the Royal Conclave. By law, a father must oblige a son who swears himself to the Highguard.","n":""},{"t":"Honda","u":"wiki/honda.html","a":["Clan Honda"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#b3332e","s":"A clan of Oprax, a lesser one at the time of its internal struggle, with an old feud against the house of Lord Hiro Kuroshi. When the old Tennō's advisor gave them Hiro's name, they came for his family. At the new Tennō's coronation, it was a man of clan Honda","b":"A clan of Oprax, a lesser one at the time of its internal struggle, with an old feud against the house of Lord Hiro Kuroshi. When the old Tennō's advisor gave them Hiro's name, they came for his family. At the new Tennō's coronation, it was a man of clan Honda who stepped forward and handed him the sword that cut the advisor down. The Honda were given nothing for it. It wasn't only loyalty. The advisor was begging, and the next thing he said might have been whose names he'd given to whom. The man of clan Honda was closing a mouth.","n":""},{"t":"Hunter's Guild","u":"wiki/hunters-guild.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Hunter's Guild is an organized pest-control guild whose origins can be dated back all the way back to the start of the Gilded Era. all big game hunting has many outposts stationed in major cities across the continent where the more long distance hunts can ","b":"The Hunter's Guild is an organized pest-control guild whose origins can be dated back all the way back to the start of the Gilded Era. all big game hunting has many outposts stationed in major cities across the continent where the more long distance hunts can be cashed in, given the hunter conducting it still has the warrant for their hunt. Killing just any random beast and trying to cash it in won't work, because then you'd have a bunch of hunters killing animals for a quick payday. This even extends to colossal beasts like Nirva, killing her will see no reward as there's no bounty for her. With the process of creating a bounty, the lodge keepers have to agree with the ethical and practical nature of the warrant. Business comes to a grinding halt when it comes to Icos however, as the hunter's guild has very little foothold on the continent due to the regulations on mercenary work set by the powers that be. Not only that, but the beasts of Icos tend to be limited to dra'iq, and the few that aren't remain confined to the furthest corners of lands where nobody dares settle. All of these factors combined mean that there's no headquarters for the Hunter's Guild anywhere Icos other than the guild outposts, which can be found in every major city across both Icos and Athura. Business operates as follows. Concerned individuals come in, talk with one of the lodge keepers about their 'pest' related issues, and hope that an experienced enough hunter takes on their request within a timely manner. Due to how simple the business operates, there are many new hunters coming in every day accepting novice bounties left and right, so virtually no problems stay unanswered for long. Hunter's Lodge","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Jotu'un","u":"wiki/jotuun.html","a":["Jotuun"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Jotu'un, or simply jotuun, are a religious order that dates back as far as the start of recorded history, of which, is as rich and detailed as both Icos and Athura combined. The jotuun were eradicated by Anteo Zuul in the Ten-Year War, and some of their kn","b":"The Jotu'un, or simply jotuun, are a religious order that dates back as far as the start of recorded history, of which, is as rich and detailed as both Icos and Athura combined. The jotuun were eradicated by Anteo Zuul in the Ten-Year War, and some of their knowledge and traditions went with them. They still kept written pieces of literature in their temples, and this knowledge was hoarded after their eradication. The entry-level rank for newly inducted members. They are in the process of learning kinmatter at the various temples, isolated from society so as to not taint their view of the world around them while in such an impressionable position. A higher rank where members undergo an apprenticeship with a jotuun prior. Occasionally, paladins educate neophytes at the temples on everything they've learned during their training. The highest rank most average jotuun will attain. Priors are experienced in kinmatter and are responsible for carrying out missions with their apprentices to help civilians or solve environmental issues. A rank for jotuun who have chosen to stay out of political affairs, but still protect the balance of nature and help civilians wherever possible. They may be unaffiliated with any particular temple, but still possess a deep knowledge of kinmatter and use it to serve the greater good. The master rank, attained by jotuun who have exceeded average feats and talents. Uthisha have mastery over kinmatter and take on the most demanding missions. A very rare rank that is only attainable by a select few. Grand Uthisha possess great wisdom and are often called upon for guidance. They also conduct conferences with one another and decree what the entire jotuun order does. I Thou will detach oneself from a life of vanity and otherwise dark desires. II Thou will be tolerant and accepting regardless of creed, race, or religion. III Thou shall always offer your service to those in need. IV Thou shall not envy. V Thou shall not judge in place of the gods. VI Thou shall not kill unjustly. VII Thou shall always act in favor of balance, remembering that all life is interconnected. VIII Thou shall seek knowledge and wisdom, always striving to expand your understanding of kinmatter and the world around you. IX Thou shall remain modest and respect the will of the gods.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Kalmere Royal Family","u":"wiki/kalmere-royal-family.html","a":["House Kalmere","The Kalmeres"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The royal family of Stade, which stood among the founding houses of the Bicontinental Union. They're the dynasty of Karlaine and Princes of Karlem, of mixed Ibyan and Moirish blood rather than Azkassari (see Titles and Ranks). Tarris Kalmere, grandfather of La","b":"The royal family of Stade, which stood among the founding houses of the Bicontinental Union. They're the dynasty of Karlaine and Princes of Karlem, of mixed Ibyan and Moirish blood rather than Azkassari (see Titles and Ranks). Tarris Kalmere, grandfather of Lanis Mandel Kalmere, father of Lanis Oriane Kalmere, Mandel's daughter, passed over for her younger brother, who kept the old castle of Stade and began the cadet line there. Anteo Zuul wanted her for his heir, Archon, when she was a child. Her line comes after Lanis's daughters. Marrec Kalmere, grandson of Oriane King Lanis Kalmere, current ruler Nephele, late first wife of Lanis Queen Loranne Kalmere, second wife of Lanis Princess Thea Kalmere, elder daughter of Lanis, by Nephele Princess Reine Kalmere, younger daughter of Lanis, by Loranne","n":""},{"t":"Kashif","u":"wiki/kashif.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Placeholder Description. Elder Antekano Elder Azhotep Elder Amunti Marshal Akkennoten Marshal T'Akoteh Marshal Ahmenkare Marshal Ashtaku Marshal Azparenhep Marshal T'Ahvos Marshal Atalamun-ra Marshal Akambim Marshal T'Anzu Marshal Amayeta Marshal Annonnoa Mars","b":"Placeholder Description. Elder Antekano Elder Azhotep Elder Amunti Marshal Akkennoten Marshal T'Akoteh Marshal Ahmenkare Marshal Ashtaku Marshal Azparenhep Marshal T'Ahvos Marshal Atalamun-ra Marshal Akambim Marshal T'Anzu Marshal Amayeta Marshal Annonnoa Marshal Ahtepa","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Keiran Clan","u":"wiki/keiran-clan.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"An organized and highly elite clan based in Bali. Often times the aggressor in any confrontation they find themselves in, the Keiran Warriors are feared immensely and their range goes even as far as Icos, although strained in numbers. After the brutal massacre","b":"An organized and highly elite clan based in Bali. Often times the aggressor in any confrontation they find themselves in, the Keiran Warriors are feared immensely and their range goes even as far as Icos, although strained in numbers. After the brutal massacre of the Dueval clan, Keiran members brandish pristine stainless gold weapons and armor with ornaments made from the most expensive of animal pelts. The outstanding victory gifted them the entire province of Bali and most namely their new capital, Suran.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Larrensire","u":"wiki/larrensire.html","a":["House Larrensire","The Larrensires"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"An Azkassari noble house, the rulers of Endlemoire and Princes of Moirach, sworn directly to the king of Stade. Their principality encompasses Endletonn, Lortonn, Norvania, Bogwatch, Moirlander's Respite and Wietmoire (see Titles and Ranks). They gave the High","b":"An Azkassari noble house, the rulers of Endlemoire and Princes of Moirach, sworn directly to the king of Stade. Their principality encompasses Endletonn, Lortonn, Norvania, Bogwatch, Moirlander's Respite and Wietmoire (see Titles and Ranks). They gave the High Throne a queen in Endelyn Larrensire. The Ermsburrys hold Endletonn under them. The Zuuls owe them fealty for Wietmoire, which they hold by conquest, a sore point between the two houses. The blood of Azkassar runs thick, even if they don't agree politically with the Barrentynes. They're more than inclined to offer Reine their help, if she agrees to marry a Larrensire. See Houses of Stade. Endelyn Larrensire Severin Larrensire, Prince of Moirach, lord of Endlemoire and nephew of Endelyn Evander Larrensire, younger son of Severin","n":""},{"t":"Merchant Guild","u":"wiki/merchant-guild.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Aided Karlaine in the transport of the oil taken from Jaraq.","b":"Aided Karlaine in the transport of the oil taken from Jaraq.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Merrowick","u":"wiki/merrowick.html","a":["House Merrowick"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Stillwater Watch. Of Ibyan blood, posted to watch the western hilltribes of Upror, and married into them for generations. Before The Massacre at Wietmoire, the Merrowicks reported to Karlaine, as good news, that there hadn't b","b":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Stillwater Watch. Of Ibyan blood, posted to watch the western hilltribes of Upror, and married into them for generations. Before The Massacre at Wietmoire, the Merrowicks reported to Karlaine, as good news, that there hadn't been one feud in the west for years. Somebody there asked who was settling them. They've lived ever since with having ended it by being pleased. The Merrowicks want the law to admit that the hilltribes aren't the king's. They'd hear of The Gurtai Horde first, and may not be believed, or may not tell. See Houses of Stade.","n":""},{"t":"Neru'Augma","u":"wiki/neruaugma.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Named after the Neruta, a dra'iq-like mythical creature that can rebirth after death; Neru'Augma is a group of religious zealots who study the old Jotu'un religion for their primary mission of bringing Augmen back from the dead to usher in a new era of peace a","b":"Named after the Neruta, a dra'iq-like mythical creature that can rebirth after death; Neru'Augma is a group of religious zealots who study the old Jotu'un religion for their primary mission of bringing Augmen back from the dead to usher in a new era of peace and prosperity. Notable Members: S'Afar (Master; practices Jeokin, Pirokin, Hidrokin, Quintecrakin) Yanna (Acolyte; practices Jeokin, Hidrokin, Mnemokin)","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Oristene","u":"wiki/oristene.html","a":["House Oristene"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Dremmel, on the road to Acrenon. Of pure Ibyan blood, and the family of Nephele. Their old custom recognized no hierarchy, so their lord is still called 'first speaker' at home. The Oristenes want Nephele's daughter acknowledg","b":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Dremmel, on the road to Acrenon. Of pure Ibyan blood, and the family of Nephele. Their old custom recognized no hierarchy, so their lord is still called 'first speaker' at home. The Oristenes want Nephele's daughter acknowledged. Thea has never been sure if they love her or the claim. See Houses of Stade.","n":""},{"t":"Pellard","u":"wiki/pellard.html","a":["House Pellard"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Lortonn, the last castle before Ōnari, and the first to burn when Oprax raids. Of mixed blood, some of it Opraxi. Half the household speaks Foomesh. The Pellards want soldiers, from anyone. If Karlaine won't send them, The Blo","b":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Lortonn, the last castle before Ōnari, and the first to burn when Oprax raids. Of mixed blood, some of it Opraxi. Half the household speaks Foomesh. The Pellards want soldiers, from anyone. If Karlaine won't send them, The Blood Empress could offer peace instead. See Houses of Stade.","n":""},{"t":"Polys","u":"wiki/polys.html","a":["House Polys","The Polys"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"One of the powerful noble houses of the Anklan Isles, from Lorence. They're a family of shipwrights, who were paid in titles when the crown couldn't pay in pearls. The office of Sea Marshal in the King of Orsena's court was once theirs. The king owed the Bank ","b":"One of the powerful noble houses of the Anklan Isles, from Lorence. They're a family of shipwrights, who were paid in titles when the crown couldn't pay in pearls. The office of Sea Marshal in the King of Orsena's court was once theirs. The king owed the Bank of Pearls, and the office was sold to a man the Bank chose, Doros Meredeon, a merchant captain who's never fought at sea. He guards the Bank's routes first, and the island towns are left to themselves. The burning of Morowai only made their loss worse. They still build the ships another man commands. Their lord and Sea Marshal, Leander Polys, married Elise, the younger sister of Kade Asher. He captured Kade on the crown's behalf, and raided his old crew's hideout on his word. Most of the crew escaped. For Elise's sake, they'd never touched a ship of the Polys. After Morowai, Kade was set to hunting them down.","n":""},{"t":"Research Commission","u":"wiki/research-commission.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Alfor Zuul's commission of researchers, through whom he means to reveal the secrets of The Vessel.","b":"Alfor Zuul's commission of researchers, through whom he means to reveal the secrets of The Vessel.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Talvarian Knights","u":"wiki/talvarian-knights.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Description Pending. Lord Commander Kazuro Commander Jiro (Master of Arms) Armed Forces (Army) Unnamed Captain (City Guard) Unnamed Commander (Naval Forces)","b":"Description Pending. Lord Commander Kazuro Commander Jiro (Master of Arms) Armed Forces (Army) Unnamed Captain (City Guard) Unnamed Commander (Naval Forces)","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Talvo Royal Family","u":"wiki/talvo-royal-family.html","a":["House Talvo","The Talvos"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"The royal family of the Kingdom of Talvar, and once kings in the far west of Icos. The Talvos began as vassals of the Kalmeres, sent to a frontier nobody else wanted, with a title and no one to rule. For generations they cut roads, kept granaries open through ","b":"The royal family of the Kingdom of Talvar, and once kings in the far west of Icos. The Talvos began as vassals of the Kalmeres, sent to a frontier nobody else wanted, with a title and no one to rule. For generations they cut roads, kept granaries open through winter, and settled feuds between the hilltribes without taking a side. They buried their own dead beside the tribes'. The hilltribes kneel to no one, and have no word for king. They have feuds, and now and then a person whose judgment even the losing side obeys. When the last tribe brings that person its feud, they're what the east would call a king. It can't be asked for or inherited, and it ends when the feuds stop coming. In this way a Talvo became king of Tuvar, in the west of Upror. Each Talvo after had to prove worthy again, and some didn't. Their seat was Talvar, a city-state and the only powerhouse on that side of the continent. The Talvos and the Crannochs had made peace with the hilltribes by the time Oswulf Talvo founded it, and the tribes allowed it to be built quietly, with no conflict. A colossal dra'iq destroyed Talvar, and before the Talvos could recover, Tarris Kalmere declared them guilty of treason, for raising High Sonham in hiding. The last king in the west, Conrad Talvo, bowed to save his family, and his crown was annulled. Afterward, the Kalmeres and Zuuls warred with the hilltribes to keep them weak. See The Fall of Talvar. The Talvos later built Wietmoire Manor, where much of the family was massacred (see The Massacre at Wietmoire). Anteo Zuul seized it afterward. Gideon, Cedric, and Lord Hiro Kuroshi were away in Athura at the time, bringing Opraxi refugees across the sea (see Internal Struggle). Gideon and Hiro were also storing part of the family's wealth there, which Cedric didn't know of until Hiro showed him (see The Founding of Trion). By law Gideon was a lord at most. The hilltribes chose him as king in secret, the first Talvo they'd chosen in well over a century. He went west himself and apologized as no Talvo had: his forebear knelt without asking them, and shouldn't have. It took years: he walked alone to each tribe that had been warred on, sat with its dead, was told every name, and said which of it was his family's doing. At the end he swore that no Talvo would kneel again, unless the tribes said so. Tormod Crannoch let him through the moors. The apology got him heard, and then the tribes began bringing him their feuds, in secret, at Moirlander's Respite. The Crannochs never relayed it to the Kalmeres. They found out from the quiet. The Merrowicks reported, as good news, that there hadn't been one feud in the west for years, and at Karlaine, somebody asked who was settling them. Anteo noticed on his own. He held the title of Duke of Tuvar, and no hilltribe had ever brought him a feud. He'd taken it for wildness, until he heard they'd all been brought to someone. His crown is why he's called king, and why Anteo couldn't let the family live. Gideon stayed to face Anteo alone. He sent the others away and went to High Sonham, which his family had built. He walked in unannounced, and refused to kneel. Anteo killed him and Kaelyn there (see The Massacre at Wietmoire). Since then, monarchs have been escorted there so that none can retrace the way. Gideon had told Cedric how to find High Sonham, and Cedric passed it on to Onfroi, who knows it still. The survivors raised a new Talvar in Athura (see The Founding of Trion), and Cedric returned to Icos to legitimize it. The same year Anteo Zuul died and Aethel Zuul took his place, Aethel granted kingship to Cedric's fifteen-year-old son, Onfroi (see The Crowning of Onfroi). King Gideon Talvo, killed by Anteo Zuul Queen Kaelyn Skyfall, wife of Gideon, killed by Anteo King Cedric Talvo, known as 'The Illegitimate King', son of Gideon and Kaelyn Lady Haileen Ermsburry, wife of Cedric, left and kept in Icos until Onfroi was named king. She and Cedric are entombed beneath Greystone. King Onfroi Talvo, current ruler, son of Cedric and Haileen Queen Kota Talvo, wife of Onfroi, born a Kuroshi Children of Onfroi and Kota: Prince Rand Talvo, deceased Enya Talvo, married to a lord in Athura Egon Talvo, deceased Amae Talvo, married to a lord in Athura Rost Talvo, missing Princess Koto Talvo Lord Hiro Kuroshi and Lady Emi Kuroshi Lord Akrio Kuroshi, son of Hiro and Emi, and Lady Sakuri Kuroshi, the parents of Kota","n":""},{"t":"The Dueval Clan","u":"wiki/the-dueval-clan.html","a":["The Crimson Shroud","The Dueval Family","The Duevals"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Crimson Shroud, known to most as 'the shroud', is an infamous cabal based in Bali. Known for their deep-rooted connections to the various historical royal families of Athura, they have amassed immense wealth through unsavory deals. The cabal is a consortiu","b":"The Crimson Shroud, known to most as 'the shroud', is an infamous cabal based in Bali. Known for their deep-rooted connections to the various historical royal families of Athura, they have amassed immense wealth through unsavory deals. The cabal is a consortium of begrudged noble bastards who aim to target the dynasties from which they descend. There are several clans of note who hold great power within the Crimson Shroud, but the Duevals are the foremost, being the founding dynasty. Athurans don't culturally carry surnames, so the name Dueval comes from V'Ain, the founder, who took the name of his ship and coined the surname, passing it onto respected crewmates and kin. This clan name has since become synonymous with the dynasty. A unique tradition within the Crimson Shroud is the dying of their hair. Within the Dueval family, a member's hair grows redder with each ritualistic dunking, initially intended to symbolize the blood on their hands. For the Duevals, the red hair is a mark of respect and authority, granted based on accomplishing feats for the family. Their connections, wealth, and ruthless enforcement has made the Crimson Shroud the most feared and respected syndicate in all of Athura. In spite of their illegitimacy, public executions were commonplace for those who repeatedly disrespected the families, including the seemingly untouchable rulers of Athura if any so happen to act reckless and speak ill of the shroud regardless of their diluted royal blood. The leading family of the shroud donned the titles of Icosian nobility as an insult to their Athuran customs. Though, since the bicontinental union, Sultans became Kings. The insult, much like the titles they were forged against, vanished with time. The Duevals note the color crimson as their defining standard. The 'Crimson King' or 'Crimson Queen' holds supreme authority, ruling with an iron grip. Many years after its founding, the Duevals came to control most of Athura's sovereignty through coercion, blackmail, and strategic alliances. They even ensured that any fallout from their illicit activities fell onto the royal families they manipulated, preserving the Duevals’ anonymity and protecting their interests. The Crimson Shroud maintained supreme power from the shadows. The Crimson Shroud specializes in a variety of enterprises such as smuggling, with a network that spans both continents, slave redistribution, ensuring no slaves potential goes wasted, and black market auctions, where rare and forgotten items are sold to the highest bidder. To protect their interests and keep other syndicates at bay, the Duevals offer hired muscle to maintain order in settlements that Athuran rulers wish to keep clean. These operations are meticulously managed, ensuring a steady flow of wealth and influence. Despite their formidable power, the reign of the Crimson Shroud inevitably came to an end during The Battle of Suran at the hands of the Keiran Clan. This marked the end of their dominance, shaking the foundations of the underworld they worked so hard to keep in order. The fall of the Crimson Shroud left a power vacuum in the underworld, allowing new crime families and organizations to form. V'Ain Dueval Aissa Aila","n":""},{"t":"The Gurtai Horde","u":"wiki/the-gurtai-horde.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The coalition formed when the tribes of the Gurtai unite: The Hunto, The Tughai and The Shume. It's sworn at Khag Ugyar, which becomes its seat.","b":"The coalition formed when the tribes of the Gurtai unite: The Hunto, The Tughai and The Shume. It's sworn at Khag Ugyar, which becomes its seat.","n":""},{"t":"The Hunto","u":"wiki/the-hunto.html","a":["Hunto"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"One of the more powerful tribes of the Gurtai, and the tribe of the Boryign. Riders with the largest herds. Their heart is Tenbet.","b":"One of the more powerful tribes of the Gurtai, and the tribe of the Boryign. Riders with the largest herds. Their heart is Tenbet.","n":""},{"t":"The Orsena Civic Guard","u":"wiki/the-orsena-civic-guard.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Orsena Civic Guard is the primary law enforcement agency within the city of Orsena, renowned for its well-organized structure and highly disciplined personnel. Tasked with maintaining public order, safeguarding the city's citizens, and enforcing municipal ","b":"The Orsena Civic Guard is the primary law enforcement agency within the city of Orsena, renowned for its well-organized structure and highly disciplined personnel. Tasked with maintaining public order, safeguarding the city's citizens, and enforcing municipal law, the Civic Guard is a respected institution. However, despite their skill in handling civilian matters, they are notably inexperienced when it comes to military conflict, as their primary focus has always been urban security rather than battlefield engagement. Each regiment is led by a Regimental Commander, chosen for their exceptional leadership and tactical prowess. These commanders report directly to the Lord Commander, the supreme authority of the Civic Guard, who is appointed by the ruling sovereignty of Orsena. The Lord Commander coordinates the activities of the entire guard, ensuring that the regiments work in harmony to protect the city. The Civic Guard is divided into four specialized regiments, each responsible for different sectors of the city. The regiments are distinguished by both function and district, ensuring that the city is protected in its entirety. This regiment is primarily responsible for the heart of Orsena, including the sovereign’s palace, government buildings, and merchant districts. They are the most prestigious of the four regiments, entrusted with the protection of the city’s elite. Tasked with overseeing the towering, thin spires and higher districts of Orsena, this regiment is known for its expertise in urban navigation and rooftop surveillance. They employ a range of advanced tactics to monitor criminal activity from above, giving them a unique vantage point. Operating in the shadows, the Blades are responsible for covert operations and intelligence gathering. While not assassins, they are skilled in espionage and stealth, investigating potential threats before they escalate. This regiment deals with organized crime, smuggling, and subversive elements within the city. The primary law enforcement regiment within the Orsena Civic Guard, tasked with handling all manner of civil disputes and maintaining public order throughout the city. Whether it be responding to petty crimes, mediating neighborhood conflicts, or dealing with more serious offenses such as theft and assault, the Iron Wardens are the most frequently encountered regiment by the citizens of Orsena. Trained to defuse situations peacefully whenever possible, but well-equipped and capable of using force if necessary. Due to their generalist nature, the Iron Wardens can be found patrolling the streets, investigating incidents, and responding to emergency calls. Their versatility makes them the backbone of the Civic Guard.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Shume","u":"wiki/the-shume.html","a":["Shume"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A tribe of the Gurtai. People of the coast, and the only sailors among the tribes. Their heart is Orlag.","b":"A tribe of the Gurtai. People of the coast, and the only sailors among the tribes. Their heart is Orlag.","n":""},{"t":"The Tughai","u":"wiki/the-tughai.html","a":["Tughai"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A tribe of the Gurtai. Guards of the mountain road. Their heart is Zam Gurtuz.","b":"A tribe of the Gurtai. Guards of the mountain road. Their heart is Zam Gurtuz.","n":""},{"t":"Valentyre","u":"wiki/valentyre.html","a":["The Valentyres"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A family of merchants with blood ties to the Azkassari houses. Their blood has been diluted, as they did not establish an estate in Linnenmoire with the other Azkassari nobles who fled the calamity (see The Fall of Azkassar). The other houses married among the","b":"A family of merchants with blood ties to the Azkassari houses. Their blood has been diluted, as they did not establish an estate in Linnenmoire with the other Azkassari nobles who fled the calamity (see The Fall of Azkassar). The other houses married among their own kin for generations, and the Valentyres wouldn't. They gave the High Throne a queen in Catalina Valentyre.","n":""},{"t":"Vasker","u":"wiki/vasker.html","a":["House Vasker"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Raust, between Karlaine and Arlene, and grown rich on ships. Of Ibyan blood. The Vaskers want the High Throne elected again, as the founders of the Bicontinental Union meant, and a Kalmere to stand for it. They argue that birt","b":"A noble house of Stade, seated at Raust, between Karlaine and Arlene, and grown rich on ships. Of Ibyan blood. The Vaskers want the High Throne elected again, as the founders of the Bicontinental Union meant, and a Kalmere to stand for it. They argue that birth shouldn't decide a throne while backing Thea for being born first. See Houses of Stade.","n":""},{"t":"Zuul Royal Family","u":"wiki/zuul-royal-family.html","a":["House Zuul","The Zuuls"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The family that has held the High Throne of the Bicontinental Union since Anteo Zuul. They hold High Sonham and Wietmoire Manor. They're Uprorian by blood. The Zuuls were nobles who lived in Endlemoire, the city of the Larrensires, in Stade. They rose to the K","b":"The family that has held the High Throne of the Bicontinental Union since Anteo Zuul. They hold High Sonham and Wietmoire Manor. They're Uprorian by blood. The Zuuls were nobles who lived in Endlemoire, the city of the Larrensires, in Stade. They rose to the Kalmeres' court as a reputable courtier's family, and seized titles when they had an opportunity, in steps: Lord when they came to court, Lord Paramount through a convenient death, and Duke of Tuvar when Talvar fell (see The Fall of Talvar). Aevor Zuul was head of the house then, and found High Sonham. They still hold Tuvar as of 211 ABU, as Archdukes, sworn directly to the king of Stade (see Titles and Ranks). They also hold the township of Wietmoire, which Anteo took from the Talvos with the manor, by right of conquest. For Wietmoire they owe fealty to the Larrensires, the Princes of Moirach, a sore point between the two houses. They were rivals to the Talvos. The Zuuls have pale skin, and a strong seed for black hair and blue eyes. Endelyn, being of Azkassari descent, was also pale, with red eyes and black hair streaked with bronze. Her children with Anteo had a mixture of their features. Aevor Zuul, head of the house when Talvar fell, grandfather of Anteo. He died of a burst belly. Tybal Zuul I, son of Aevor, who had Anteo late in life and died in battle Anteo Zuul, second High King Endelyn Larrensire, wife of Anteo Archon Zuul, elder son of Anteo and Endelyn Aethel Zuul, younger son of Anteo and Endelyn, third High King Catalina Valentyre, wife of Aethel Tybal Zuul, firstborn son of Aethel and Catalina, died before his father Aeleina Zuul, daughter of Aethel and Catalina Alfor Zuul, son of Aethel and Catalina, current High King Dendrilla Ariadne, Archon's wife Haylor Zuul, firstborn son of Archon and Dendrilla, a stillbirth Alexium Ariadne, Dendrilla's second son, born after Archon's death, and husband of Aiore. He was disinherited by Anteo, and people did not believe he was Archon's child. He was, so by primogeniture he came before Aethel. Queen Vaenera, daughter of Alexium and Aiore, and of the elder line","n":""},{"t":"Zuul's Hand","u":"wiki/zuuls-hand.html","a":["The Hand of Zuul","The Pleiad"],"f":"Lore/Factions","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A secret order sworn to the Zuul dynasty, founded to achieve their political ambitions through intrigue. There are seven members at all times, handpicked for their loyalty and extraordinary talents. Members serve for life and are marked with a tattoo on their ","b":"A secret order sworn to the Zuul dynasty, founded to achieve their political ambitions through intrigue. There are seven members at all times, handpicked for their loyalty and extraordinary talents. Members serve for life and are marked with a tattoo on their body; desertion means death. A member is called an Inquisitor. They act as assassins and enforcers of the highest order, and are terribly efficient. They might travel within Zuul's entourage, but much like the Highguard, they do not speak unless ordered to by a Zuul, or in private amongst themselves. All seven were needed to dispatch Ayala, and she killed all but one of them. Their number had to be made up over time, and the one who lived trained the rest. Everything the present seven know was taught by someone who'd watched six of the best die to one woman. Adrien Arin Auriel Cyreth Dismas Kiro Kiyana","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Baqui","u":"wiki/baqui.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Massive amphibians, with a bulky body and a long, paddle-like tail. They have smooth, slimy skin that comes in shades of brown or black, helping them blend into the murky waters of their swampy environment. Their eyes are eerily tiny, and they have a wide, too","b":"Massive amphibians, with a bulky body and a long, paddle-like tail. They have smooth, slimy skin that comes in shades of brown or black, helping them blend into the murky waters of their swampy environment. Their eyes are eerily tiny, and they have a wide, toothy maw. Baqui are territorial creatures that lie beneath the surface of the swampy waters. They feed on a variety of prey, including fish, small mammals, and birds. Their venomous tongue allows it to strike quickly and take down prey with ease, assisted by the large barb at the tip of the tongue. Baqui are a major threat to the fishermen and fauna of South Athura and it often damages fishing nets and scares away other wildlife. This has made baqui a massive target to the Talvarian natives and Hunters Guild alike, which ultimately lead to overhunting. This creatures notoriety hasn't helped to gain it any sympathy from the Abunali either, and with the declining population, its extinction is looking more and more likely.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Brightworm","u":"wiki/brightworm.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A bioluminescent worm species native to the southern forests of both Icos and Athura. The brightworms produce glowing silk, used primarily for building their webbing nests, where they lay their luminescent eggs. These nests glow indefinitely, especially since ","b":"A bioluminescent worm species native to the southern forests of both Icos and Athura. The brightworms produce glowing silk, used primarily for building their webbing nests, where they lay their luminescent eggs. These nests glow indefinitely, especially since brightworms spend a significant portion of their lives within them. Though primarily herbivorous, consuming glowing brightpods within the jungles, they are also known to resort to cannibalism in times of scarcity, which can allow a secluded population to thrive a fair bit longer than most other species of worm.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Bush Vex","u":"wiki/bush-vex.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Despite its prickly appearance, the bush vex is covered in a soft, dense fur with a long, bushy tail. Its fur has a mixture of different shades of brown for the overcoat, with a cream-colored undercoat. It has small, beady eyes and long, sharp, curved claws us","b":"Despite its prickly appearance, the bush vex is covered in a soft, dense fur with a long, bushy tail. Its fur has a mixture of different shades of brown for the overcoat, with a cream-colored undercoat. It has small, beady eyes and long, sharp, curved claws used for climbing and intimidating any would-be predators or intruders in its territory. It's native to the southern half of Athura, and can be found in dense underbrush indiscriminate of the biome. They're omnivorous, eating a mixture of fruits, nuts, seeds, insects, and small rodents. As mentioned, Bush Vex is an extremely territorial creature, marking its territory with scent glands located on its wrists, and living within these territories in small family groups, where they'll each take turns patrolling the perimeter of their marked territory. If confronted with a threat, they'll stand on their hind legs and hold their large paws up against the threat while they emit loud barking vocalizations which is actually surprisingly effective at warding them off. This being said, it's known to be quite vocal, making a variety of chirps, squeaks, and growls to communicate with its family group. Despite its aggressive behavior, Bush Vex is also known to be a playful and affectionate creature, frequently grooming each other and engaging in social play.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Dew Vex","u":"wiki/dew-vex.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Small, furry canine insectivores with a slender body. They have large, round eyes and small antennae-like tufts of fur that sprout from their foreheads. Their fur coloration varies depending on the season, from white, pastel green, to beige. Their fur allows t","b":"Small, furry canine insectivores with a slender body. They have large, round eyes and small antennae-like tufts of fur that sprout from their foreheads. Their fur coloration varies depending on the season, from white, pastel green, to beige. Their fur allows them to spread dew and seeds throughout the forest floor via the hooked filaments on their fur. This makes the texture of their fur almost feel like Velcro, only softer. The dew vex are crepuscular creatures that roam the forest at twilight, spreading moisture and seeds as they go. During the evening and nights, they curl up and sleep in burrows or among the roots of trees. They are generally harmless and have a positive impact on their ecosystem, being a good food source for other small creatures and maintaining the diversity of the forest floor due to their dew and seeds.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Flesh Pecca","u":"wiki/flesh-pecca.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Avian scavengers with a beaten and worn appearance, bearing a large, hooked, serrated beak for tearing at flesh. Despite their spindly appearance, they're fairly muscular, putting up quite a fight if ever they were grabbed. Flesh Pecca are ruthless, swarming i","b":"Avian scavengers with a beaten and worn appearance, bearing a large, hooked, serrated beak for tearing at flesh. Despite their spindly appearance, they're fairly muscular, putting up quite a fight if ever they were grabbed. Flesh Pecca are ruthless, swarming injured or dead animals all over the arid regions of Athura. They are opportunistic feeders and are known to carry many various types of diseases, making them a threat to other creatures and to people. Most settlements, no matter how well guarded, steer clear of any peccas flying overhead. Archers will go out of their way to shoot any down on sight due to their high aggression and infection rate.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Garugala","u":"wiki/garugala.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A massive insect that can grow up to 6 feet long. Workers tend to stay close to the hive, defending the eggs, food, and queen, while the drones actively go out in search of food. The garugala is a widely-known threat across all regions of Athura, and are feare","b":"A massive insect that can grow up to 6 feet long. Workers tend to stay close to the hive, defending the eggs, food, and queen, while the drones actively go out in search of food. The garugala is a widely-known threat across all regions of Athura, and are feared by many. Their hives are often avoided by other creatures in the area. Workers are brown or gray in color and have long, spindly legs that allow them to move quickly and efficiently. Their antennae are highly sensitive and they emit a deep buzz when they sense danger, alerting the rest of the hive to the threat. Drones are what the garugala are most known for, being much larger and having a massive sack of acidic juices that emit a bright glow after they have fed. Once they return to the hive after feeding, they secrete their juices into craters dug by the workers, where the larvae will feed and grow. They are also a source of valuable resources for those who are brave enough to hunt garugala drones, or dare to venture into the hives and harvest the acidic juices.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Khovis","u":"wiki/khovis.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Khovis, or khovi in plural, are a species of large, flightless avians endemic to South Athura, with the highest concentration found in the peninsula Ashla Sanctuary. Khovi are a sight to behold, standing tall at the intimidating height of eight and a half feet","b":"Khovis, or khovi in plural, are a species of large, flightless avians endemic to South Athura, with the highest concentration found in the peninsula Ashla Sanctuary. Khovi are a sight to behold, standing tall at the intimidating height of eight and a half feet tall with an imposing frame that's robust and muscular, built for endurance and strength rather than speed. The most striking feature of the khovis is the trio of horns adorning its head. These horns twist and curve as the avian matures and used primarily in intraspecific competition. Khovi are also fiercely protective of their nesting grounds and will not hesitate to chase and ram any intruders with a force that often times can be lethal. Khovi live in tight-knit family units, exhibiting a level of social structure rarely seen in avian species. These units work together to defend their territory, raise their young, and forage for food that they place in piles near their nests. Khovi rarely emit sounds, however their most common sound is their call. It's a deep, resonant sound, reminiscent of a horn being blown. This low, bass-enriched howl can carry for miles, penetrating the dense jungle foliage with ease. It's often used to communicate with lost family members and other family units.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Labua","u":"wiki/labua.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A distant relative of the Obo, but considerably smaller in size. Labua are typically found in more humid environments than their larger cousin, often found near bodies of water or in areas with high humidity levels. Their black coat is adorned with distinctive","b":"A distant relative of the Obo, but considerably smaller in size. Labua are typically found in more humid environments than their larger cousin, often found near bodies of water or in areas with high humidity levels. Their black coat is adorned with distinctive white stripes that help them blend into their surroundings. One of the most distinctive features of the labua is its elongated head, which houses its exceptionally long prehensile tongue, which it uses to forage and snag insects. Its head is adorned with tusks that protrude both from its cranium and its mouth, making it a formidable opponent in self-defense. Despite their small size, labua are known for their tenacity and can be surprisingly ferocious when provoked.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"'Sand Snatcher'","u":"wiki/sand-snatcher.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A vicious bird of prey that inhabits the White Sea. Despite being a large, fearsome predator, very little is known about this bird due to its elusive nature. It has a reputation for being shrouded in mystery, cloaking itself in severe sandstorms and misty rain","b":"A vicious bird of prey that inhabits the White Sea. Despite being a large, fearsome predator, very little is known about this bird due to its elusive nature. It has a reputation for being shrouded in mystery, cloaking itself in severe sandstorms and misty rains that make it difficult to spot. This has led to the Sand Snatcher becoming the stuff of legends, not even receiving a proper name. Being a formidable hunter, it uses its massive wings to generate strong gusts of wind that can whip sand into a frenzy. This makes it nearly impossible for its prey to escape, as the bird can use the sand to obscure its movements and blind its targets. At the same time, it uses its razor-sharp talons to snatch and slash at its prey.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Dra'iq","u":"wiki/draiq.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Dra'iq are draconic organisms too The most common type of dra'iq. Always carnivorous in diet with ferocious temperamental tendencies. The heat of their breath varies from specimen to specimen. Flame is created by two large glands called 'piro glands' in the lo","b":"Dra'iq are draconic organisms too The most common type of dra'iq. Always carnivorous in diet with ferocious temperamental tendencies. The heat of their breath varies from specimen to specimen. Flame is created by two large glands called 'piro glands' in the lower jaw that project oil when contracted. The heat of the breath is enough to ignite the oil and depending how large of a breath they exhale, can reach up to 208 meters in the largest individuals. Although at such a distance, most of the oil is burned up so the flames are less likely to stick onto you. A rare type. Omnivorous in diet, they're usually content to stay in forests rather than hunting for animals outside of their habitat. They don't grow very large, usually capping out at about 40 feet in length. They're exceptional flyers, having a wingspan usually doubling that of dra'iq in their same size range. Their breath isn't kept in their lungs but rather in tube-like sacks spanning the length of their necks. They inhale air, trapping in in these sacks until they need to use it. Mind you they're still able to breath and emit sounds. Whenever they eventually release the air, it's in short barks, or bursts of air that emit gusts of wind of up to 233 mph capable of sending most creatures flying. Fairly common. Carnivorous in diet. These dra'iq usually have the toughest skin out of the entire order, being able to deflect ballista bolts and shake off intense pirokin attacks. They don't like to fly as often as the other types since they can defend themselves best on land. Utilizing large solid objects like boulders or trees against attackers. Uncommon. Carnivorous, Herbivorous, Omnivorous, Piscivorous. Thick scaled skin similar in texture to chainmail. Most commonly unable to harness the power of any element. However there have been select cases of apteral dra'iq using each except for fire.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Ithuum","u":"wiki/ithuum.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"TBD","b":"TBD","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Jaraq","u":"wiki/jaraq.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A young Dra'iq that once inhabited the long stretch of marshes in the lowlands of Icos. Terrorizing the countryside, Jaraq caused most settlements of the southwest to band together and ward the beast away. A foolish plan to enact against any dra'iq, but Jaraq ","b":"A young Dra'iq that once inhabited the long stretch of marshes in the lowlands of Icos. Terrorizing the countryside, Jaraq caused most settlements of the southwest to band together and ward the beast away. A foolish plan to enact against any dra'iq, but Jaraq appeared to be a youngling compared to other known dra'iq of the era. Therefore his power and ferocity were likely to be manageable for the mobs of afflicted Icosians. Despite this line of thinking, numerous attempts at slaying the beast ended in countless lives being lost, and the atlae finally called for the assistance of the titans of the north. After a week of no update, the titans returned to Karlaine with Jaraq's body to confirm his death. Having harvested a great many of resources from Jaraq, Karlaine was made the wealthiest, and most powerful city in Stade if not all of Icos. With the oil from the dra'iq's piro glands now in Karlaine's possession, they could forge even the toughest ore with ease. Jaraq's skeletal remains now sit in the Karlaine castle as a trophy, and his likeness is now used on their flags.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Luscena","u":"wiki/luscena.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Lyrik","u":"wiki/lyrik.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A frightening combination of size, power, and cunning; Lyrik stands at the top of the planet's food chain. Like many other Dra'iq, he can expel searing flames from his mouth. Remarkably, these scarlet flames splinter off towards any organisms within the vicini","b":"A frightening combination of size, power, and cunning; Lyrik stands at the top of the planet's food chain. Like many other Dra'iq, he can expel searing flames from his mouth. Remarkably, these scarlet flames splinter off towards any organisms within the vicinity. Beyond this, he possesses the ability to emit immense heat from his body in times of vulnerability. This process cauterizes any wounds he may have sustained while in combat. On top of this, Lyrik boasts atypical intellect and the unprecedented ability to speak, though this speech is largely unintelligible. With no apparent competition, his reach could undoubtedly encompass the entire planet. That said, he seldom leaves the comfort of his comparatively small territory located somewhere on Avalar.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Mandrid","u":"wiki/mandrid.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"An apteral dra'iq that was captured and confined in the caverns beneath the Bank of Pearls. Though lacking flight and aptitude for any element, Mandrid compensates with its deafening howls and robust physique. Its metabolism is of unique interest, though not t","b":"An apteral dra'iq that was captured and confined in the caverns beneath the Bank of Pearls. Though lacking flight and aptitude for any element, Mandrid compensates with its deafening howls and robust physique. Its metabolism is of unique interest, though not to its captors, as it is exceptionally streamlined and enables it to survive without sustenance for prolonged periods of time.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Mephisto","u":"wiki/mephisto.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Timroquet's final child, and the first to be slain. Mephisto was a raging terra dra'iq that terrorized the westernmost regions of Athura. Known for using her powerful jaws to chuck boulders at settlements and causing sinkholes that swallowed temples whole, Mep","b":"Timroquet's final child, and the first to be slain. Mephisto was a raging terra dra'iq that terrorized the westernmost regions of Athura. Known for using her powerful jaws to chuck boulders at settlements and causing sinkholes that swallowed temples whole, Mephisto demonstrated to all of Athura why all dra'iq are a threat, even if they don't spew fire or fly above the clouds. In 186 ABU, Mephisto was slain by Mirak who decided to take action against the dra'iq as he saw her laying waste to the villages that once existed where Pa'iq rests. Using a combination of aerokin and jeokin, he attempted to force her to submit. But being a dra'iq her temper only grew, and in turn so did her strength, and she eventually escaped his grasp and tore through what little was left of the settlements she destroyed. Unable to keep the beast under control, Mirak had decided that she had to be put down, and so he used his kinmatter to finally kill her. Her remains rest along the Banta Gorge portion of Mandor Canyon, and her skull now hangs in the throne room of Yidji Palace in Sulala.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Nirva","u":"wiki/nirva.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A relatively new and extremely territorial sea Dra'iq that took a more serpentine physiology, Nirva lives in the warm waters of Abunala Sea. She lies dormant for months, sometimes even years, until something disturbs her frighteningly light slumber. Specifical","b":"A relatively new and extremely territorial sea Dra'iq that took a more serpentine physiology, Nirva lives in the warm waters of Abunala Sea. She lies dormant for months, sometimes even years, until something disturbs her frighteningly light slumber. Specifically, a ship sailing on the water's above. With relentless ferocity, she attacks by launching out of the water and wrapping her serpentine body around whatever she catches, constricting it and wrapping her deathly maw around it. Nirva used to roam onto the shores of both Icos and Athura some time ago, which allowed ships to pass through her sea undisturbed. That is, until Rurok swooped down sunk its talons into her, lifting Nirva into the air and carrying her above the seas. She was only able to escape its grip after spraying its face with lakes of water, spooking the creature. Rurok released its grasp and continued off, leaving Nirva plummeting into the sea. Since then, Nirva has never been seen leaving the water, although several plans have been hatched to lure Nirva out and find a method of leading Rurok back to the sea to once again fight and potentially kill Nirva so that naval trade in the sea may resume. Among the first ships she took was that of Tybal Zuul, the High King's eldest son, on his way to Trion.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Quegai","u":"wiki/quegai.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Quegai the Lonesome is an apteral dra'iq that spends his time living peacefully in the Atlan Mountains where wandering travelers are an uncommon occurrence. Those who've seen Quegai note that he is able to stand and walk bipedally like an Atlae, and even sings","b":"Quegai the Lonesome is an apteral dra'iq that spends his time living peacefully in the Atlan Mountains where wandering travelers are an uncommon occurrence. Those who've seen Quegai note that he is able to stand and walk bipedally like an Atlae, and even sings hymns to himself when he's unbothered. Those who he has seen have suffered from amnesia when trying to recall the moment and strings of intense bad luck.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Scylla","u":"wiki/scylla.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A mature female Dra'iq that inhabits the Sommerset Isles. Very little is known about Scylla other than her immense size and blood thinning howls, but thanks to the defeat of Jaraq we now know the insides of these animals are very 'alien', thus making them even","b":"A mature female Dra'iq that inhabits the Sommerset Isles. Very little is known about Scylla other than her immense size and blood thinning howls, but thanks to the defeat of Jaraq we now know the insides of these animals are very 'alien', thus making them even more valuable than the largest animals outside of their order. Scylla hasn't been documented attacking any settlements, and the ringo tribes that inhabit the southern islands say this beast is benevolent, going out of her way to avoid attacking ringo flat-boats travelling off-island, while decimating any atlae ships that just so happen to pass by. Due to the strict boundary Scylla has outlined for many atlae travelers, very few have managed to fully explore any landmass that lay south of Icos and Athura. Needless to say, slaying the beast has become one of the most expensive bounties in all of the lands if any were foolish enough to try.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Tiare","u":"wiki/tiare.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary/Dra'iq","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A quadrupedal dra'iq that has a proclivity for the skies. Known to be particularly docile towards species endemic to its habitat, but has a fiercely intolerant disposition towards foreign organisms. It uses powerful gusts of wind produced by its wings and air ","b":"A quadrupedal dra'iq that has a proclivity for the skies. Known to be particularly docile towards species endemic to its habitat, but has a fiercely intolerant disposition towards foreign organisms. It uses powerful gusts of wind produced by its wings and air sacs to attack intruders and prey alike. Despite its capability, the animal rarely ever hunts, preferring to consume the luscious fruits and berries in its habitat instead.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Neruta","u":"wiki/neruta.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A dra'iq-like mythical creature that can rebirth after death; the namesake of Neru'Augma.","b":"A dra'iq-like mythical creature that can rebirth after death; the namesake of Neru'Augma.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Rurok","u":"wiki/rurok.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna/Legendary","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A massive avian that inhabits the easternmost regions of Artisans, some even suspect it roosts in lands beyond Icos and Athura. Unlike most creatures across Artisans, the rurok is a single individual who's managed to survive for several millennia. Taking the a","b":"A massive avian that inhabits the easternmost regions of Artisans, some even suspect it roosts in lands beyond Icos and Athura. Unlike most creatures across Artisans, the rurok is a single individual who's managed to survive for several millennia. Taking the appearance of a bird of prey, the rurok is dark in coloration, having grey plumage around its collar, with dark, almost blackened skin. Its eyes are a piercing white, and its beak and talons are as sharp as a creature as small as a vex. Its wingspan is uncertain, with many eye-witness accounts supporting the claim that it spans from Sukara to Sulala, tip to tip. Whenever it is seen, it's usually soaring across the distant sky, and its rarely been seen doing anything but. Although on occasion, it has been sighted hunting a number of large fauna, including the likes of Dra'iq who happen to catch its attention. A notable dra'iq who managed to survive an attempted hunt from the rurok is Nirva.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Lokoon","u":"wiki/lokoon.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The lokoon has bright, iridescent feathers that can range from emerald green to fuchsia pink. This makes them highly prized for their vibrant feathers, which are used in various items for fashion and decoration. Its small body is agile and nimble, making it a ","b":"The lokoon has bright, iridescent feathers that can range from emerald green to fuchsia pink. This makes them highly prized for their vibrant feathers, which are used in various items for fashion and decoration. Its small body is agile and nimble, making it a difficult target for predators. Despite its small size, it has a fierce spirit and is not afraid to stand up for itself. Its wings are used mainly for balance and decoration rather than for flying, which it won't even do if cornered and facing inevitable death. Lokoon are highly sought after for their unique flavor and the fact that they are a rarity in the food industry. Although, many people can suffer from severe illness and even death from consuming poorly prepared lokoon meat. As a result, its meat is banned in many areas. Despite the risks, many people are willing to pay top dollar for a taste of this rare and exotic creature.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Narwa","u":"wiki/narwa.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Large mammal, with a sturdy build and tough hide. They have distinctive tusks that vary in number but are all dotted across its very large face. They're primarily used for protection and foraging for foods such as digging up roots, uprooting shrubbery, and tea","b":"Large mammal, with a sturdy build and tough hide. They have distinctive tusks that vary in number but are all dotted across its very large face. They're primarily used for protection and foraging for foods such as digging up roots, uprooting shrubbery, and tearing off the bark of trees. The color of their skin ranges from light grey to a mud brown. They inhabit the savannas of Athura, and have few natural predators due to their formidable tusks and imposing stature. They're exceptionally loyal and social creatures, often seen in small herds comprised of their immediate family, but many young male narwa leave these family units to roam the savannas and deserts alone, where they'll look for a mate from another herd to run off with, eventually forming their own herd. Narwa are domesticated by many villages for their dung, which they use for their crops, tusks, and hide. Despite this, within Abunali culture, it is only slaughtered when absolutely necessary, and some people consider it an extension of their family due to its extreme loyalty.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Obo","u":"wiki/obo.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A moderately sized mammal native to Athura known for its unruly attitude, which is displayed through mock charges at would-be predators. Despite this, it is still commonly kept as livestock by the few regions, likely due to its hearty nature. One of the most s","b":"A moderately sized mammal native to Athura known for its unruly attitude, which is displayed through mock charges at would-be predators. Despite this, it is still commonly kept as livestock by the few regions, likely due to its hearty nature. One of the most striking features of the obo is its massive head, which is adorned with tusks that protrude out from the sides of its mouth. Their mouths are capable of opening wide and snapping exceptionally fast, making them a formidable weapon against potential predators. In addition to this, obo has a reputation for occasionally eating the corpses of the animals they happen to kill in self-defense, a behavior that can be both gruesome and fascinating to observe.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Occi","u":"wiki/occi.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A massive, heavily-armored crustacean predator with a long and bulky body, covered in a hard exoskeleton that protects it from the harsh conditions of the desert. Its forearms are large and powerful, ending in sharp, crushing claws that it uses to dig through ","b":"A massive, heavily-armored crustacean predator with a long and bulky body, covered in a hard exoskeleton that protects it from the harsh conditions of the desert. Its forearms are large and powerful, ending in sharp, crushing claws that it uses to dig through the sand and stone. Its compound eyes are set far apart, giving it an almost 360-degree view of its surroundings. Occi lives beneath the sands and stone of the White Sea, where it burrows deep into the ground to avoid the intense heat and predators. Its strong exoskeleton and powerful claws allow it to dig through the dense sediment and move around freely. It's a voracious predator, hunting and eating anything it can catch in the desert. Its powerful jaws and crushing claws allow it to take down large prey, including other apex predators. Occi are solitary creatures, only coming to the surface to hunt or avoid danger. It is an ambush predator, lying in wait beneath the sand and then bursting out to grab its prey. Its compound eyes give it an advantage in low-light conditions, allowing it to see in the dark burrows it creates. The natives of the desert greatly fear the occi and run at the first sign of one in the region. The hunters hunting for the guild are quite often requested to hunt occi, but most refuse due to the unpredictability of the hunt, it could either go exceptionally well, or exceptionally bad. Due to the rarity of the hunts, the asking prices are usually highest, and hunters new to the region typically underestimate the challenge the occi presents, causing them to lose their life. Occi tail plates are often crafted into fashionable armor sets, which are actually more practical for combat use than most metals due to its lighter weight and dense, leathery composition.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Pipi","u":"wiki/pipi.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Small, furry mammals that inhabit the jungles of Abu. pipi are excellent hunters and are active throughout the day, preying on rodents and birds within its environment. They're known for their agility and ability to fly, often found waiting in trees in search ","b":"Small, furry mammals that inhabit the jungles of Abu. pipi are excellent hunters and are active throughout the day, preying on rodents and birds within its environment. They're known for their agility and ability to fly, often found waiting in trees in search of prey. Its fur is a mellow yellow with dark brown stripes, allowing it to blend in perfectly in its jungle environment. One of the most distinctive features of the pipi is a gland located on the tip of its tail. This gland releases a strong, spicy substance that can be used as a defense mechanism against predators. The substance can be extremely irritating if it gets in your eyes or mouth, but if used in moderation, it adds a unique flavor to food.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Quarri","u":"wiki/quarri.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Massive, shelled reptiles that inhabit the oceans around Icos and Athura. They're omnivores, known to feed primarily on the god-kelp that grows in shallow waters. Its shell is their most distinctive feature, providing both protection from predators and buoyanc","b":"Massive, shelled reptiles that inhabit the oceans around Icos and Athura. They're omnivores, known to feed primarily on the god-kelp that grows in shallow waters. Its shell is their most distinctive feature, providing both protection from predators and buoyancy in the water. The shell is covered in a thick layer of algae and dotted with other marine life, creating a natural camouflage whenever resting on the ocean floor. Despite its size and strength, quarri are also known to be quite gentle, only attacking when it feels threatened or its personal space is invaded during mating season. In the past, quarri were feared by sailors and fishermen, with tales of men being swallowed whole by these massive creatures. However, these stories were later discovered to be a result of misidentification and aggressive mating habits.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Saatra","u":"wiki/saatra.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A fearsome and highly territorial predator, known for its dangerous bite. Living in small packs, these reptilian creatures roam the arid savannas and deserts in search of food and territory. With their strong jaws and razor-sharp teeth, saatra are capable hunt","b":"A fearsome and highly territorial predator, known for its dangerous bite. Living in small packs, these reptilian creatures roam the arid savannas and deserts in search of food and territory. With their strong jaws and razor-sharp teeth, saatra are capable hunters, able to take down even the toughest prey. The septic bite of a saatra is particularly fearsome, spreading dangerous foreign bacteria that causes a searing pain at the impact zone in anything it bites. There is a subspecies known as the veiled saatra that inhabit the dense mountainous jungles of Matau, they have a defensive mechanism in the form of quills along their backs, necks, and tails. These quills can be erected as a warning or to deter potential predators, and can be ejected with incredible force as a last-resort defense. The quills can cause serious injury or even death if a vital area is penetrated.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Sea Home","u":"wiki/sea-home.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A giant clam species that has had a significant impact on the economy of the city of Orsena. Found in the shallow oceans around Tabla Pise and Northwest Athura, these clams are truly massive, with shells large enough to house entire small towns. What sets the ","b":"A giant clam species that has had a significant impact on the economy of the city of Orsena. Found in the shallow oceans around Tabla Pise and Northwest Athura, these clams are truly massive, with shells large enough to house entire small towns. What sets the Sea Home apart from other clam species is the presence of caverns within its massive shells, filled with valuable pearls. These pearls, prized for their beauty and rarity, have been instrumental in establishing Orsena's wealth and prosperity. In fact, the Bank of Pearls is named after these valuable gems, reflecting their importance to the local economy. Fishing for these clams and harvesting their pearls has become a major industry in the region, providing jobs and income for many people. However, over-fishing and habitat destruction have put the future of the Sea Home in jeopardy, and efforts are being made to conserve and protect this species and its valuable ecosystem. Despite this, the Sea Home remains a symbol of wealth and prosperity, and its impact on the local economy and culture is unlikely to be forgotten anytime soon. The Seas The White Abyss Azka Sea Abunala Sea","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Tirvaan","u":"wiki/tirvaan.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A massive, ferocious beast, with a hulking, muscular body and sharp, deadly claws and teeth. Its fur is matted and unkempt, with a dark brown, almost black coloration. Its piercing eyes are bright green around the pupil and yellow around the sclera. Making its","b":"A massive, ferocious beast, with a hulking, muscular body and sharp, deadly claws and teeth. Its fur is matted and unkempt, with a dark brown, almost black coloration. Its piercing eyes are bright green around the pupil and yellow around the sclera. Making its den in caverns and old ruins in the wet jungles and forests of Southern Athura, many people known not to roam too far in the wilderness as you risk stumbling upon a tirvaan. It is a fierce predator, hunting anything that crosses its path and fiercely defending its territory. They're known to be unpredictable and dangerous, striking fear into the hearts of all who hear its deep rumbling roar. It sounds almost like thunder, making it travel further in the dense jungles it roams.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Tjarka","u":"wiki/tjarka.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Large, bipedal rodents with an immediately recognizable appearance. Some notable characteristics are a long, pointed snout and large, expressive ears. The numerous domesticated subspecies come in a variety of different shapes, colors, sizes, and characteristic","b":"Large, bipedal rodents with an immediately recognizable appearance. Some notable characteristics are a long, pointed snout and large, expressive ears. The numerous domesticated subspecies come in a variety of different shapes, colors, sizes, and characteristics. Tjarka are often kept by people to work on their farms, pushing contraptions to grind grains and till soil, as well as being used for their fur. Tjarka are known for their waste-not eating habits, eating whatever happens to be in front of their mouth when hungry. Although their favorite snacks consist of not-so-small mammals and insects. Tjarka are native to the harsher environments of Artisans, such as deserts, tundra's, and cliffsides. They're resilient creatures, able to survive in tough conditions. Many domesticated subspecies are used for various things, with different adaptations for different terrains. Some brief examples are: Mes'iq Tjarka: Wide splayed feet and full fur coverage, best for speeding across the dunes of Athura. Mes'un Tjarka: Skinny feet, almost hoof-life, best for speeding across any surface. Looks the most familiar to the average wild tjarka. Turaaj Tjarka: Used for racing, which is a mixture of both previous types with short, sleek fur for a more streamlined appearance.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Trampa","u":"wiki/trampa.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A massive arachnid that can grow up to 8 feet in length and weigh over 100 pounds. It's covered in a thick layer of black or brown hair and has long, solid legs. Its metal mandibles are large and razor-sharp, capable of tearing apart even the toughest of mater","b":"A massive arachnid that can grow up to 8 feet in length and weigh over 100 pounds. It's covered in a thick layer of black or brown hair and has long, solid legs. Its metal mandibles are large and razor-sharp, capable of tearing apart even the toughest of materials. They are highly territorial and will fiercely defend their hives against any intruders. They have a powerful venom that can immobilize their prey, allowing them to drag it back to their lair to feed on. They are also known to be highly intelligent, able to work together to defend their hives and hunt for food. Trampa are a major problem if their hive is located near a settlement. If their population grows too large, they will venture further in search of food and can cause widespread destruction. They are known to attack livestock, crops, and even people, and it is not uncommon for settlements to band together to eradicate a particularly large or aggressive hive. Despite their fearsome reputation, some people seek out trampa for their valuable venom, which is used in various medicines and hunting gear.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Tulkai","u":"wiki/tulkai.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Massive elephantine herbivores native to the deserts, savannas, swamps, and jungles of Athura. They have tough, leathery skin to protect against the heat and thorns of their environments, and their tusks are long and curved, primarily used to reach tough veget","b":"Massive elephantine herbivores native to the deserts, savannas, swamps, and jungles of Athura. They have tough, leathery skin to protect against the heat and thorns of their environments, and their tusks are long and curved, primarily used to reach tough vegetation. Tulkai are social creatures and are often seen in herds. They spend much of their day grazing on shrubs and bushes, and are well-known for their ability to find water sources in their environments. They can travel long distances across the hot sand or dense vegetation without getting tired, this makes them highly sought after as mounts, as well as their ability to carry an excess amount of weight. However the Abunali Sand Nomads of the desert are highly defensive of these creatures, and attack anyone who dares get close to their herd. This aggression stems from the poaching done to tulkai for their meat and tusks.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Wydjet","u":"wiki/wydjet.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Semi-aquatic apex predator whose range spans throughout all of Athura. Their lifestyle is very laid back as they wait for food to come to them and only move from a water source to chase after whatever happened to get away. While on land it's capable of committ","b":"Semi-aquatic apex predator whose range spans throughout all of Athura. Their lifestyle is very laid back as they wait for food to come to them and only move from a water source to chase after whatever happened to get away. While on land it's capable of committing to extremely quick yet short bursts of speed, but that's not in its detriment at all since these predators have immense stamina. They can go months without water as their large tails and necks store all of the moisture they'll need. So land pursuits can persist for however long they need, and since they're largely water predators they're far more capable in said element.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Yeeko","u":"wiki/yeeko.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A unique bird with a quirky appearance. It has a round, feathery body with long, thin legs that allow it to quickly hop from place to place. Its beak is wide and curved, perfectly suited for digging through the ground in search of insects and rodents. Its feat","b":"A unique bird with a quirky appearance. It has a round, feathery body with long, thin legs that allow it to quickly hop from place to place. Its beak is wide and curved, perfectly suited for digging through the ground in search of insects and rodents. Its feathers are brightly colored and iridescent, ranging from vibrant greens to sunny yellows. Every morning, the yeeko will sing their soft lullabies, which are known to have a calming effect to those who hear them. However, at night, they produce sharp, piercing zings that many find annoying or even startling. To compliment their silly appearance, they display a strangely playful behavior, seeming to enjoy playing pranks on people. These birds have a reputation for being thieves, often sneaking into shops and stealing goods with open displays. They are particularly fond of shiny trinkets and will often hoard them in their nests. Some people view them as pests and try to keep them away from their homes, while others see them as charming creatures that they even keep as pets.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Yippa","u":"wiki/yippa.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Giant, woolly rodents that are native to the marshy ecosystems of Icos. They have webbed hands and feet, which make them excellent swimmers. Their long, spade-shaped tails are used to propel themselves through the water, and they are also strong enough to supp","b":"Giant, woolly rodents that are native to the marshy ecosystems of Icos. They have webbed hands and feet, which make them excellent swimmers. Their long, spade-shaped tails are used to propel themselves through the water, and they are also strong enough to support the weight of a wagon. Yippa are covered in thick, shaggy fur that helps to insulate them from the cold and wet environment of the marsh. Their fur is typically a mottled brown or gray color, which helps to camouflage them in the underbrush. In terms of size, yippa are roughly the size of a small house. Despite being closely related to tjarka, yippa are primarily quadruped, and have a sturdy build with a broad head and a pronounced, rounded snout. Their eyes are covered in a layer of mucus, making their eyesight quite bad despite the large size of their eyeball; this is to prevent the blood-sucking insects from sucking the blood from their eyeballs and to protect from debris that may be floating in the waters, which, in their natural habitat, is quite common due to their sloppy lifestyle. In spite of their somewhat ungainly appearance, yippa are surprisingly intelligent and easy to train, much like their fleet-footed cousins; this makes them perfect pets for the people of Ōnari and a no brainer when choosing a mount for wetland environments.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Yris","u":"wiki/yris.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Yris are revered for their mysterious behavior and rarity. They are shy and elusive creatures that are difficult to observe. They're highly prized for their beauty, with unique patterns and colors that stay the same year round. They have a special relationship","b":"Yris are revered for their mysterious behavior and rarity. They are shy and elusive creatures that are difficult to observe. They're highly prized for their beauty, with unique patterns and colors that stay the same year round. They have a special relationship with the Abunali and natives of Middle Athura, who believe that they carry the spirits of their ancestors. The Abunali give offerings to the yris in hopes of gaining their favor and protection. Despite being sacred, the yris are still hunted by poachers who seek to profit from their valuable pelts, making them a vulnerable species.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Ytara","u":"wiki/ytara.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A striking and colorful bird species that is native to Athura. It is known for its vibrant plumage and flashy tail feathers, which it uses to whip the low-hanging fruit out of the trees. Though, when not around tree fruit, berries dotted around the jungle shru","b":"A striking and colorful bird species that is native to Athura. It is known for its vibrant plumage and flashy tail feathers, which it uses to whip the low-hanging fruit out of the trees. Though, when not around tree fruit, berries dotted around the jungle shrubbery do just fine. Ytara feathers are prized by royalty and other members of the upper class, who keep these birds as symbols of their wealth and status. The tail feathers come in a variety of colors, with some individuals exhibiting truly stunning markings. The most sought after being hues of gold, silver, and coral. Despite its popularity among royalty and traders, ytara are a protected species, with regulations in place that prevent overhunting and capture of these magnificent birds. Those who seek to keep a ytara as a pet must obtain the proper permits and take special care to provide the bird with a safe and comfortable environment.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Zarbuck","u":"wiki/zarbuck.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Fauna","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Zarbuck are fleet-footed herbivores with an ungulate-like appearance, having long legs and a slender body allowing them to run at great speeds. They're often observed grazing on grasses and shrubs in the forests and savannas of both Icos and Athura. They can o","b":"Zarbuck are fleet-footed herbivores with an ungulate-like appearance, having long legs and a slender body allowing them to run at great speeds. They're often observed grazing on grasses and shrubs in the forests and savannas of both Icos and Athura. They can often be found in herds because of their social nature. Their fur comes in a few shades of colors such as beige, gold, and brown. However domesticated zarbuck have a much wider range of colors such as white, black, orange and even red. On the topic of domesticated zarbuck, there are a wide array of subspecies that were bred selectively by breeders for various purposes.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Hapjuk Tree","u":"wiki/hapjuk-tree.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Flora","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Isp","u":"wiki/isp.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Flora","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Marriot","u":"wiki/marriot.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Flora","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A melon that grows out of the ground.","b":"A melon that grows out of the ground.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Nectar Pods","u":"wiki/nectar-pods.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Flora","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Also called 'Cezolis' Mane'. Where nectar (the currency & flavor additive) comes from. Nectar being easily renewable is what gives it less value than tosi's.","b":"Also called 'Cezolis' Mane'. Where nectar (the currency & flavor additive) comes from. Nectar being easily renewable is what gives it less value than tosi's.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Ucheeberry Vines","u":"wiki/ucheeberry-vines.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Flora","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Varrow's Tree","u":"wiki/varrows-tree.html","a":["Varrow's Leaf"],"f":"Lore/Flora","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A tree with white, flaky bark and thin, blue, near-translucent leaves. The one in Endelyn Larrensire's garden in High Sonham shimmers in various colors at noon, when the sunlight shines through the painted glass and onto the tree. It's poisonous, though there'","b":"A tree with white, flaky bark and thin, blue, near-translucent leaves. The one in Endelyn Larrensire's garden in High Sonham shimmers in various colors at noon, when the sunlight shines through the painted glass and onto the tree. It's poisonous, though there's no struggle and no choking when one consumes its leaf. Endelyn ate of it, wanting only to be happy, and died. She's entombed beneath it.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Yawa Tree","u":"wiki/yawa-tree.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Flora","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A tree that produces the yawa fruit.","b":"A tree that produces the yawa fruit.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Ytara Tree","u":"wiki/ytara-tree.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Flora","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Massive evergreen trees native to the southeastern fertile belt of Athura, or more specifically the Tan'we. Produces fruit that hang from vines, of which are whipped out of the trees by ytara.","b":"Massive evergreen trees native to the southeastern fertile belt of Athura, or more specifically the Tan'we. Produces fruit that hang from vines, of which are whipped out of the trees by ytara.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Blood Magic","u":"wiki/blood-magic.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A practice forbidden for as long as Oprax has existed, but freely practiced among Gurtai shamans. The Blood Empress is rumored to practice it.","b":"A practice forbidden for as long as Oprax has existed, but freely practiced among Gurtai shamans. The Blood Empress is rumored to practice it.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Kashif Trials","u":"wiki/kashif-trials.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A sacred tradition and rite of passage, staged for many generations in Khamu, where the young become brave warriors and protectors of the natural world.","b":"A sacred tradition and rite of passage, staged for many generations in Khamu, where the young become brave warriors and protectors of the natural world.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Languages","u":"wiki/languages.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The languages of Artisans as of 211 ABU. Hyenesh is the most dominant, while the older tongues live on among the peoples who kept them. The traditional language of the Atlae. Originates far beyond recorded history. As of 211 ABU, it remains in use by the Abuna","b":"The languages of Artisans as of 211 ABU. Hyenesh is the most dominant, while the older tongues live on among the peoples who kept them. The traditional language of the Atlae. Originates far beyond recorded history. As of 211 ABU, it remains in use by the Abunali and a small fraction of Athura. An old tongue commonly used by the atlae of Icos. Dates back to when Icos was first being colonized by atlae. It remains in use by the many Icosian villages. A relatively modern language. Merges elements of both Pumesh and Dholesh while introducing newer elements to simplify speech and spelling. It's the current most dominant language in all of Artisans. The ancestral language spoken by the Opraxi. Despite being considered a dated language by most scholars, it remains the dominant language spoken as of present within the Oprax region, as any other language is not tolerated within its borders. The traditional language of the Ringo. It's closely linked to both Upper and Lower Et'lesh, and ringo who speak it are the only ones who can understand grolls speaking Upper Et'lesh. An evolved version of the traditional language of the grolls that infuses elements of Pumesh. Though, still largely its own separate language. Since the eradication of the jotuun, nobody can truly communicate with most grolls, and those who speak Upper Et'lesh don't want to go anywhere near one due to their nasty reputation. The traditional language of the grolls. Originates far beyond recorded history. It's the more dominant and basal form of Et'lesh, still spoken by older groll populations. No one else alive can understand a single word of it.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Naluwai","u":"wiki/naluwai.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures","c":"#5c66f5","s":"An annual Abunali event held at the lake south of Kama, celebrating the aquatic side of the natural world.","b":"An annual Abunali event held at the lake south of Kama, celebrating the aquatic side of the natural world.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Bicontinental Union","u":"wiki/bicontinental-union.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Politics","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A binding pact between the realms of both continents, which Oprax refused to join, ensuring military assistance during dra’iq events and forbidding the predatory seizure of weakened territory. The Kalmeres stood among its founding houses, working alongside Ath","b":"A binding pact between the realms of both continents, which Oprax refused to join, ensuring military assistance during dra’iq events and forbidding the predatory seizure of weakened territory. The Kalmeres stood among its founding houses, working alongside Athuran rulers. This alliance rewrote political norms across two continents and became significant enough to define time itself: history was now measured in BBU and ABU. When the Bicontinental Union was formed, its founders understood a harsh truth. You cannot coordinate two continents by committee alone. So they created The Royal Conclave, wherein an elected, temporary arbiter whose authority superseded kings only in matters of dra’iq response, international law within the Union, and sanctions against rule-breaking nations. The High Sovereign was never meant to rule. They were meant to organize, judge, and stabilize. The founders were explicit in ensuring that the role wasn't hereditary, tied to any specific kingdom, and passed only by election when a term naturally ended. The title was created with Tarris Kalmere, the first High King. Anteo Zuul was voted in after him and granted emergency powers by the rulers who joined the Union, which he heavily abused, securing succession rights for his children. His eldest, Archon, died in one of the major conflicts (see The Death of Archon), and Anteo never abdicated, so upon his death the High Throne passed to his youngest, Aethel, and then to Alfor. The High Sovereign was never made to relinquish those powers. Even with them, Anteo tried and failed to force Oprax into the Union. See Anteo's Failure. The High Sovereign has courtiers, though not as many as a traditional ruler would. Alfor has about four, who can travel at his behest to any ruler's court, where they are held as esteemed guests. They are representatives of the High Crown. Initially proposed as the Bicontinental Union, the name the year notations (BBU and ABU) still use. Once Anteo ascended to High Sovereign, he influenced those beneath him to refer to it as the Zuul Union, especially after his children were born and set to inherit it. As of 211 ABU, people refer to it as the Union or the Zuul Union in everyday speech, as a Zuul still sits on the High Throne.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Houses of Stade","u":"wiki/houses-of-stade.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Politics","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The noble houses of Stade as of 211 ABU. With King Lanis Kalmere refusing to name an heir, leaning toward either of his daughters is close to treason. It shows in marriages, seating, and who visits whom. Castle House Blood Leans Stade Kalmere, cadet line Ibyan","b":"The noble houses of Stade as of 211 ABU. With King Lanis Kalmere refusing to name an heir, leaning toward either of his daughters is close to treason. It shows in marriages, seating, and who visits whom. Castle House Blood Leans Stade Kalmere, cadet line Ibyan and Moirish The king's word, and he gives none Pavilia Damaris, Princes of Ibya Thea Mory Hallorn Ibyan The son not yet born Raust Vasker Ibyan Thea Dremmel Oristene Pure Ibyan; Nephele's kin Thea Norvania Barrentyne Azkassari Reine Endlemoire Larrensire Azkassari Reine, at a price Bogwatch Crannoch Moirish Neither Lortonn Pellard Mixed, some Opraxi Whoever sends soldiers Stillwater Watch Merrowick Ibyan, long married west Undecided There are lesser houses beneath these, such as the Ermsburrys. The Kalmeres are Princes of Karlem, the Damaris of Pavilia Princes of Ibya (Prince Theron Damaris), and the Larrensires Princes of Moirach, whose principality takes in Norvania, Bogwatch and Lortonn. The Zuuls are Archdukes of Tuvar. See Titles and Ranks. Thea has age and the Ibyan coast. Reine has Norvania and the queen. Neither has a majority.","n":""},{"t":"Royal Conclave","u":"wiki/royal-conclave.html","a":["The Royal Conclave"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Politics","c":"#5c66f5","s":"An event of paramount importance within the Bicontinental Union, held each year. It serves as a forum for all monarchs part of the Union to present the issues affecting their realms to the High King and petition for relief, and it's how petitions reach the Hig","b":"An event of paramount importance within the Bicontinental Union, held each year. It serves as a forum for all monarchs part of the Union to present the issues affecting their realms to the High King and petition for relief, and it's how petitions reach the High Throne. It's held at High Sonham. The monarchs gather at Wietmoire, and are led the last of the way by the Highguard so that none of them can retrace it. These meetings can last weeks, and take months of travel time for certain rulers. Tensions are always high, as everyone feels as though their word must be heard, or be content with the long silence as they await the next year's conclave.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Titles & Ranks","u":"wiki/titles-ranks.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Politics","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The standard used by the Union, at the Royal Conclave and in its dealings. Each realm keeps its own ladder beneath its crown (see below). High King / High Queen King / Queen Duke / Dutchess Lord Paramount / Lady Paramount Lord / Lady Stade: King; Archduke of T","b":"The standard used by the Union, at the Royal Conclave and in its dealings. Each realm keeps its own ladder beneath its crown (see below). High King / High Queen King / Queen Duke / Dutchess Lord Paramount / Lady Paramount Lord / Lady Stade: King; Archduke of Tuvar, Princes of Moirach, Karlem and Ibya, one Duke; Counts; Lord-Elector as an office. Morgar: Grand Prince, Duke, Lord. Azkassar was the first civilization to establish feudalism, and \"king\" was a title heard only there. Ibya was governed by strategoi, each over a nome, who sat together in a senate. See The Peoples of Old Icos.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Da'et","u":"wiki/daet.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The sunken realm, kept by Oies. See Faiths.","b":"The sunken realm, kept by Oies. See Faiths.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Faiths","u":"wiki/faiths.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The two great faiths of Artisans: the Faith of the Hidden Ones, dominant in Athura, and the Order of the Ancients. The Faith of the Hidden Ones is the dominant Athuran religion centered on the conviction that the gods are real, active, and omnipresent, yet rar","b":"The two great faiths of Artisans: the Faith of the Hidden Ones, dominant in Athura, and the Order of the Ancients. The Faith of the Hidden Ones is the dominant Athuran religion centered on the conviction that the gods are real, active, and omnipresent, yet rarely, if ever, perceivable in direct form. The pantheon is collectively called “The Hidden Ones” or “Those Who Remain Unseen,” a phrase meant literally. Temples and roadside shrines are civic necessities as much as spiritual ones. They are places to offer thanks, avert harm, and acknowledge the forces that govern life. Religion in the Faith of the Hidden Ones is treated as a relationship of distance and consequence rather than intimacy and constant reassurance. Mortals do not assume the gods are obligated to comfort them; they assume the gods will act according to domain and temperament. Prayer is therefore context-driven—asked when needed, offered when spared, repeated when fear demands a name—and it is culturally acceptable to address certain gods with reverent anger or frank distrust, especially Oiyas, because Death is seen as necessary but not tender. The faithful aim less to “understand” the gods than to live in ways that do not invite their worst expressions: to honor Adena without indulging excess, to respect Oiyas without pretending to love him, to appease Phera when the sea turns sharp, and to keep shrines tended so the unseen never feels ignored. A common opening invocation frames this posture of reverence without presumption: “Hidden Ones, who move without footstep and speak without tongue—see us, though we cannot see you.” Adena is revered as the 'Goddess of Life,' governing the living balance of ecosystems; prayers to her often sound like gratitude paired with restraint. “Oh, Adena. The beauty of your forests from which all life began. You are that which all strive to be, though we cannot.” Oiyas is the 'God of Death' and keeper of the Da’et, feared not as evil but as finality, necessity, and impartial ending. “Before us is the God who dared sully Adena, his lover, beautiful and perfect. Oiyas, protector of the sunken realm, the Da’et… I serve you, yet I do not trust you… Hear me, oh Great Oiyas.” Rhodite, the 'God of Skirmish,' governs righteous conflict, honorable defense, and guardianship—invoked by soldiers and city-guards who want victory without becoming monsters. “Rhodite, set my feet true and my hand steady; let me strike only what I must.” Phera, the 'Goddess of Weather,' governs her domain with the volatile temperament of sea and sky; sailors speak to her less like a beloved and more like a sovereign to be appeased. “Phera, spare this hull; take our offering and let your wrath pass over open water.” Artisia, 'Goddess of Land and Planets,' governs Artisans’ stability, geological shift, tides, and the slow order of celestial movement; farmers and builders ask for steadiness rather than miracles. “Artisia, hold the ground beneath us—let the world keep its shape.” Cezolis, 'Goddess of the Sun,' governs day and night, seasons, warmth, and the destructive edge of heat. “Cezolis, burn bright but not bitter; give warmth to seed and skin.” Idu, 'God of Time and Innovation,' governs the cyclical nature of life, destiny, learning, and the push toward invention; scholars and artisans ask for progress that doesn’t become hubris. “Idu, let my hands make what my mind can bear; let my work outlive my error.” Shakir, 'God of Wealth & Prosperity,' governs prosperity and good fortune, but is traditionally approached through generosity rather than hoarding. “Shakir, let abundance pass through me—full hands, open hands.” TBD","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Adena","u":"wiki/adena.html","a":["Goddess of Life","Mother of Growth"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Embodying the essence of nature and the interconnectedness of all things living, Adena stands as the most beloved and cherished of all of the deities. Being a relatively new deity in the historical context, Adena's role as mother nature isn't exactly unique to","b":"Embodying the essence of nature and the interconnectedness of all things living, Adena stands as the most beloved and cherished of all of the deities. Being a relatively new deity in the historical context, Adena's role as mother nature isn't exactly unique to her, as the goddess Artisia, who was worshipped for centuries prior, encompassed this role as well. However, Adena more directly associated with matters of life and the wellness of nature. In artistic depictions, she is most commonly surrounded by lush foliage and various animals as she's interacting with other deities or historical figures; exuding a compassionate, maternal aura. The color associated with Adena is green, symbolizing fertility, growth, and renewal. She is believed to nurture the land, and gift life to all while attempting to maintain the delicate balance of ecosystems. The modern Jotuun were probably some of the most devout followers of Adena, shifting their focus from Artisia in favor of a more benevolent deity.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Artisia","u":"wiki/artisia.html","a":["Goddess of Land & Planets","Shepherd of Worlds"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The stoic and ever-ancient Artisia assumes the role of a shepherd, guiding the movement of the land and celestial bodies over vast spans of time. Depicted as a regal and contemplative figure, she holds the weight of the world and the cosmic order upon her shou","b":"The stoic and ever-ancient Artisia assumes the role of a shepherd, guiding the movement of the land and celestial bodies over vast spans of time. Depicted as a regal and contemplative figure, she holds the weight of the world and the cosmic order upon her shoulders. Balance and equity are what she's all about, for without it, neither life or death would be possible on a dry, barren world. Artisia's actions influence the geological shifts, the ebb and flow of tides, and the positions of the celestial bodies. Her role as the protector of the planet and its interconnected systems instills a sense of reverence for the delicate balance of the natural world. Often, people tend to seek her blessings for fertile soil, favorable celestial alignments, and stable environments to thrive within. The most notable religious order dedicated to Artisia and her purpose are the Jotuun. However, the longer the jotuun were active, the more they started to slowly shift their focus from the balance of the world itself to the balance of more small-minded civil and political disputes. The colors most commonly associated with Artisia are brown, green, and grey.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Cezolis","u":"wiki/cezolis.html","a":["Goddess of the Sun","Bringer of Light"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A radiant and powerful deity representing the sun and all its attributes. Often depicted as a majestic figure, radiating intense light and warmth. The colors most often associated with Cezolis are gold or yellow. In mythology, Cezolis is believed to control th","b":"A radiant and powerful deity representing the sun and all its attributes. Often depicted as a majestic figure, radiating intense light and warmth. The colors most often associated with Cezolis are gold or yellow. In mythology, Cezolis is believed to control the seasons, as well as the cycle of day and night, providing light, energy, and sustenance to the world. She symbolizes warmth, vitality, and stoicism. People believe that whenever Cezolis has been angered, she will produce more heat than usual, spawning heatwave after heatwave. Devout worshippers of Cezolis perform rituals or ceremonies at sunrise or sunset to honor and gain favor from the goddess, in the hopes that she won't take her anger out on mortal kind.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Haethum","u":"wiki/haethum.html","a":["Haethum","Idu","God of Time & Innovation","Keeper of Progress"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A complex deity embodying the concepts of time and innovation. Originating in the northern regions of Icos, he is often portrayed as an enigmatic figure, representing the passage of time and the continuous evolution of knowledge and progress. Haethum is associ","b":"A complex deity embodying the concepts of time and innovation. Originating in the northern regions of Icos, he is often portrayed as an enigmatic figure, representing the passage of time and the continuous evolution of knowledge and progress. Haethum is associated with the colors silver and tan. As the god of time, he governs the cyclical nature of existence and guides the destiny of individuals and civilizations. Additionally, as the god of innovation, he inspires inventors, thinkers, and artisans to push the boundaries of knowledge and creativity. Temples dedicated to Haethum have served as centers of learning and intellectual exchange, although in recent eras, Haethum's influence has diminished. And while he has a place in both Icosian and Athuran pantheons, he isn't as popular with the Athurans as he is with the Icosians.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Lucan","u":"wiki/lucan.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Founder of Azkassar, believed to have ruled it jointly with Millera. Venerated by the Order of the Ancients for introducing mortals to a more ordered and prosperous form of governance. See The Fall of Azkassar.","b":"Founder of Azkassar, believed to have ruled it jointly with Millera. Venerated by the Order of the Ancients for introducing mortals to a more ordered and prosperous form of governance. See The Fall of Azkassar.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Millera","u":"wiki/millera.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Empress of Azkassar, believed to have ruled it jointly with Lucan. Venerated by the Order of the Ancients for introducing mortals to a more ordered and prosperous form of governance. See The Fall of Azkassar.","b":"Empress of Azkassar, believed to have ruled it jointly with Lucan. Venerated by the Order of the Ancients for introducing mortals to a more ordered and prosperous form of governance. See The Fall of Azkassar.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Oies","u":"wiki/oies.html","a":["Oiyas","God of Death","Keeper of the End"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Shrouded in mystery and fear. Due to the inherently unknown nature of death, Oies has never been visually defined or depicted in art throughout the realms of Icos and Athura. Yet, his existence looms over mortals, instilling a sense of unease and wariness. Unl","b":"Shrouded in mystery and fear. Due to the inherently unknown nature of death, Oies has never been visually defined or depicted in art throughout the realms of Icos and Athura. Yet, his existence looms over mortals, instilling a sense of unease and wariness. Unlike common conceptions of death deities, Oies is not portrayed as malevolent or evil in scripture. Instead, he is often depicted as a tragic figure, being unable to touch or get close to anyone who isn't immortal without killing them outright. The fear associated with Oies stems from the uncertainty and finality of death itself, compelling people to live with reverence for life and to cherish their time in the mortal realm. The color most commonly associated with Oies is white.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Phera","u":"wiki/phera.html","a":["Goddess of Weather","Mistress of Storms"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Phera is a force to be reckoned with, wielding the power of storms and natural calamities. She is typically depicted as a formidable and vengeful deity, with storm clouds encircling her and lightning crackling in her hands. Phera's infamous jealousy and vengef","b":"Phera is a force to be reckoned with, wielding the power of storms and natural calamities. She is typically depicted as a formidable and vengeful deity, with storm clouds encircling her and lightning crackling in her hands. Phera's infamous jealousy and vengefulness stem from the stories that depict her husband, Rhodite, interacting with other deities and mortals, leading her to challenge them in fierce battles to win back his attention and affection. However, winning her husband back isn't enough to quell her fury, as afterward, innocent mortals often bear the brunt of her rage through violent storms, hurricanes, and floods. Only when her anger subsides does the weather follow. Sailors and fishermen hold great reverence for Phera, offering prayers and rituals to appease her and seek safe voyages. The colors most commonly associated with Phera are yellow, purple, and cyan.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Rhodite","u":"wiki/rhodite.html","a":["God of Skirmish","Guardian of the Righteous"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Revered as a powerful warrior and guardian, the formidable 'God of Skirmish' is typically depicted as a tall and physically fit humanoid in bruthil armor, always wearing a helmet that obscures his face. Rhodite exudes an aura of strength and dominance. A colos","b":"Revered as a powerful warrior and guardian, the formidable 'God of Skirmish' is typically depicted as a tall and physically fit humanoid in bruthil armor, always wearing a helmet that obscures his face. Rhodite exudes an aura of strength and dominance. A colossal bronze statue of Rhodite erected in the bay of Sukara, overlooking an island fort between the city and Sulala, stands as a testament to his might and serves as a symbol of protection. Being the 'God of Skirmish' and not a 'God of War', Rhodite naturally favors righteousness and honor instead of dominative power and resolve whenever conflict presents itself. Legends recount tales of his divine intervention in battles to ensure victory for the righteous. Inspiring warriors to fight honorably. Many warriors and guardians seek his blessings before proceeding into combat, each hoping to harness his skill and courage. The colors most commonly associated with him are red and bronze, further accentuating his intimidating yet grounded demeanor.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Shakir","u":"wiki/shakir.html","a":["God of Wealth & Prosperity","Keeper of Abundance"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A deity embodying abundance, prosperity, and wealth. He is often depicted as a benevolent figure, juxtaposing his massive and beastly appearance, most often adorned with lavish attire and surrounded by symbols of affluence. The colors associated with Shakir ar","b":"A deity embodying abundance, prosperity, and wealth. He is often depicted as a benevolent figure, juxtaposing his massive and beastly appearance, most often adorned with lavish attire and surrounded by symbols of affluence. The colors associated with Shakir are often gold, amber, or teal. In mythology, Shakir is believed to bless individuals with material wealth, success, and good fortune. Followers of Shakir gift offerings to seek his favor, such as donating to charitable causes or engaging in acts of generosity.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Korkophilopolis","u":"wiki/korkophilopolis.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The city-temple of Oies in the Realm of the Scorned, upon which the condemned labor forever. See Order of the Ancients.","b":"The city-temple of Oies in the Realm of the Scorned, upon which the condemned labor forever. See Order of the Ancients.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Abec","u":"wiki/abec.html","a":["God of Guidance","Shepherd of the Lost"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Lesser Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Abec is worshipped primarily within the Abu, Anklan'A, and Bali regions on the continent of Athura. This benevolent god is admired for assisting lost mortals, offering them direction and solace during challenging times. Not only can Abec help you find your way","b":"Abec is worshipped primarily within the Abu, Anklan'A, and Bali regions on the continent of Athura. This benevolent god is admired for assisting lost mortals, offering them direction and solace during challenging times. Not only can Abec help you find your way physically, but he is also believed to help individuals navigate through difficult decisions, find their path in life, and overcome mental struggles. Additionally, Abec is responsible for guiding lost souls to the afterlife, ensuring their safe transition and spiritual journey beyond the mortal realm.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Appateth","u":"wiki/appateth.html","a":["Goddess of Hunting & Foraging","The Huntress"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Lesser Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Appateth holds great significance to the South Athuran Abunali. She guides mortals to success in hunting and foraging through positive reinforcement. The abunali believe if they conduct damaging practices she provides nothing, and if they conduct sustainable a","b":"Appateth holds great significance to the South Athuran Abunali. She guides mortals to success in hunting and foraging through positive reinforcement. The abunali believe if they conduct damaging practices she provides nothing, and if they conduct sustainable and resourceful practices she grows generous. Appateth is celebrated for providing food and resources to her followers, ensuring their survival in the wilds. Long ago, Appateth was a real person, a huntress capable of impossible feats who brought prosperity to the diminishing abunali. It is unknown of her fate, but her legacy had been cemented and immortalized. Now, people only know of Appateth as a deity, hearing her stories being passed down through the generations.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Desu","u":"wiki/desu.html","a":["Goddess of Purity","The Healer of Souls"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Lesser Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Desu hails from the Matau region on the continent of Athura. As the offspring of Oies and Adena, Desu takes after her mother, and embodies the harmonious connection between life and purity. Desu is worshipped as a divine figure, promoting purity of mind, body,","b":"Desu hails from the Matau region on the continent of Athura. As the offspring of Oies and Adena, Desu takes after her mother, and embodies the harmonious connection between life and purity. Desu is worshipped as a divine figure, promoting purity of mind, body, and spirit. Followers of Desu engage in rituals and practices related to purification, healing, and spiritual cleansing that heal the soul and maintain moral integrity. Desu's presence brings a sense of serenity, offering solace and the promise of inner purity to her devotees.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Desumet","u":"wiki/desumet.html","a":["God of Impurity","The Veiled Brother"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Lesser Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Desumet is the brother and counterpart of Desu, being yet another offspring of Oies and Adena. Originating from the Matau region on the continent of Athura, this deity represents the concept of impurity and acts as a reminder of the inherent flaws and imperfec","b":"Desumet is the brother and counterpart of Desu, being yet another offspring of Oies and Adena. Originating from the Matau region on the continent of Athura, this deity represents the concept of impurity and acts as a reminder of the inherent flaws and imperfections within the mortal realm. Desumet's influence manifests in the acknowledgment of the human condition, embracing the complexities and contradictions of existence. The followers of Desumet engage in rituals that recognize and accept the multifaceted nature of life, seeking wisdom in the acknowledgment of both light and darkness.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Monoro","u":"wiki/monoro.html","a":["Goddess of Fate & Destiny"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Lesser Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Spawn of Oies and Adena","b":"Spawn of Oies and Adena","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Ptalamun","u":"wiki/ptalamun.html","a":["God of Protection","The Guardian"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Lesser Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Ptalamun is worshipped in the Abu, Anklan'A, and Bali regions on the continent of Athura. He is known for safeguarding his followers from harm when absolutely necessary. The tribes residing in the northern islands of Tabla Pise perform a dance ritual every sea","b":"Ptalamun is worshipped in the Abu, Anklan'A, and Bali regions on the continent of Athura. He is known for safeguarding his followers from harm when absolutely necessary. The tribes residing in the northern islands of Tabla Pise perform a dance ritual every season to receive Ptalamun's blessing, seeking his divine protection for the entire village. Ptalamun's influence is believed to shield communities from calamities, both natural and supernatural, and to bestow courage and resilience upon his followers. Shrines and sacred sites dedicated to Ptalamun are places of communal gathering, where the faithful seek his protection and express their gratitude.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Roan","u":"wiki/roan.html","a":["Goddess of the Six Senses"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Lesser Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"TBD","b":"TBD","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Timroquet","u":"wiki/timroquet.html","a":["God of Beasts & Dra'iq","The Primordial Dread"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion/Lesser Gods","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Timroquet originates from the Abunali culture, but his legend spreads as far as Upror on Icos. Revered as the most fearsome and elusive Dra'iq, Timroquet holds dominion over all dra'iq and rare beasts. Mythology describes Timroquet as a primal force, ensuring ","b":"Timroquet originates from the Abunali culture, but his legend spreads as far as Upror on Icos. Revered as the most fearsome and elusive Dra'iq, Timroquet holds dominion over all dra'iq and rare beasts. Mythology describes Timroquet as a primal force, ensuring that dra'iq and beasts remain fierce and powerful, serving as a check against the dominance of other species like grolls, Ringo, Atlae, and even titans. Though never seen by mortals, the existence of Timroquet is etched into the mythology of Athura. This god's influence creates a veil of mystery and a sense of respect for the untamed wilderness of the natural world. Legend tells of a time when Oies was unsatisfied with his inability to create life like his one true love, Adena. Instead, being cursed with the predisposed ability to take life. Wanting more for himself, to acquire a new dominion, Oies approached Cezolis, recognizing her nurturing and transformative powers as the sun's divine embodiment. Pitying him in his plight, Cezolis decided to assist him. Together, they birthed a divine creature, Timroquet, who was not too physically dissimilar from the wonderful creatures Adena had made. It's only flaw being that just like its father, it was predisposed to taking life. However unlike Oies, Timroquet quite enjoyed it. Blessed with the power of both Cezolis and Oies, they both could not keep Timroquet out of the mortal realm, and once it freed itself, it unleashed a new threat upon Artisans--the dra'iq.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Order of the Ancients","u":"wiki/order-of-the-ancients.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Cultures/Religion","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Order of the Ancients is the dominant religion of Icos and one of the principal faiths practiced within territories influenced by Icosi culture. Followers of the Order worship a defined pantheon of deities known collectively as the Ancients, whom they beli","b":"The Order of the Ancients is the dominant religion of Icos and one of the principal faiths practiced within territories influenced by Icosi culture. Followers of the Order worship a defined pantheon of deities known collectively as the Ancients, whom they believe once physically inhabited the mortal world and lived among the civilizations of antiquity. According to the Order, the Ancients were not mortals who achieved divinity, but beings who were divine from their beginning. They created and shaped the world in successive ages, established many of the foundations upon which mortal civilization rests, and eventually passed from the mortal realm into domains of their own. From these domains, the Ancients are believed to continue influencing the world. The faith is closely intertwined with the hierarchical society of Icos. Hereditary rule, inherited responsibilities, oaths, ancestry, and continuity between generations are regarded as parts of the natural order of mortal civilization. Royal blood in particular is traditionally viewed as spiritually nearer to the gods, even that noble birth alone does not make an individual pious. The Order is not entirely static. Its teachings develop through public sermons and the circumstances of different historical periods, with figures and events of contemporary importance influencing how the stories of the Ancients are told and understood. The gods themselves possess familial relationships, and divine lineage contributes to a hierarchy within the pantheon. This distinction between divine and mortal birth forms one of the central theological boundaries of the Order. A god may be born from gods, whether through procreation or other divine means. A mortal cannot become a god merely through accomplishment, authority, wealth, or reputation. To claim otherwise is regarded by orthodox believers as among the gravest offenses a mortal can commit. Orthodox teachings maintain an absolute distinction between the divine and the mortal. The Ancients were gods because they were ordained to be. Their accomplishments demonstrate their nature but did not create it. A mortal performing extraordinary deeds may earn renown, authority, or remembrance, but cannot thereby make themselves divine. Feigning godhood is consequently considered an extreme act of sacrilege. To compare oneself directly to an Ancient is to claim a nature one does not possess and to place oneself alongside the beings responsible for the creation and ordering of the world. Several denominations have disputed this interpretation, however. These groups teach that mortals who perform sufficiently extraordinary feats may attain a state comparable to godhood. The orthodox Order rejects such teachings as heresy. The Order teaches that the formation of the world occurred through a succession of divine acts. Artisia first created the world itself and the celestial bodies surrounding it. Cezolis then gave the world light. Adena brought life into creation, while Oies introduced death. With life and death established, Haethum gave mortals the tools necessary to create civilization. Much later, Lucan and Millera introduced mortals to a more ordered and prosperous form of governance. This progression gives the pantheon a loose chronology within religious tradition. The deeds of one Ancient establish the conditions necessary for those who follow: a world must exist before it can be illuminated, life must exist before it can die, and mortals must exist before civilization can be built. Lucan and Millera occupy a particularly important place within Icosi political theology because of their association with rulership. The two are believed to have jointly ruled the ancient civilization of Azkassar, where their subjects regarded them as gods as well as kings. Their kingdom possessed wealth considered unimaginable for its age and was remembered as both prosperous and unassailable, without the decadence that followers might otherwise associate with unchecked luxury. Their reign serves as one of the Order's principal models for mortal governance. The nine Ancients are believed to possess individual domains beyond the mortal realm. When an Ancient dies, they pass into their domain and continue to exist there eternally. From these domains, the Ancients remain capable of influencing mortal affairs. Prayer is therefore not merely commemorative. Followers may petition the Ancients for intervention while also venerating them for the deeds they performed during their existence in the mortal world. The exact nature of such intervention is understood through faith, stories, and religious interpretation rather than through any universally defined set of rules. Followers of the Order are encouraged to choose a patron Ancient during their mortal lives. A devout follower who faithfully serves their chosen deity is believed to enter that Ancient's domain after death. There they may continue serving their patron for eternity. Those who reject the gods, lack sufficient devotion, or otherwise die without such favor are instead believed to pass into the keeping of Oies. Oies rules the Realm of the Scorned, where the condemned labor as slaves upon Korkophilopolis, his city-temple. The city is said to be without completion, ensuring that the dead consigned there may toil upon it forever. The fear of Oies therefore concerns more than death itself. He represents the fate awaiting those who enter death without the protection or favor of another Ancient. Ancestor veneration is common among followers of the Order. The dead are remembered as previous custodians of the obligations, names, lands, and traditions inherited by the living. Ancestors who held royal titles receive particular reverence and may be regarded as having drawn closer to the gods after death. This reverence does not ordinarily make an ancestor an Ancient. The distinction between honored mortal and god remains fundamental to orthodox belief. Those known to have worshipped Oies during life are generally excluded from the same assumptions of divine closeness afforded to other prestigious ancestors. As a result, lineage within Icos possesses both political and spiritual importance. To dishonor one's ancestors is not merely to embarrass a family name, but to show contempt toward the generations whose responsibilities have been inherited by the living. The Order possesses no singular comprehensive code defining the behavior of a good follower. Instead, the stories of the Ancients provide examples, warnings, and cautionary tales from which followers are expected to draw lessons. Different individuals may emphasize different stories and different gods depending upon their circumstances and patronage. Certain actions nevertheless carry severe religious weight. Abandoning an inherited responsibility, breaking a sworn oath, violating guest right, dishonoring one's ancestors, and comparing oneself to the Ancients can all constitute profound moral failures. Their severity is not necessarily ranked universally. The weight placed upon each offense often depends upon the individual, their circumstances, their family, and the obligations they possess. The Order considers hierarchy the natural state of mortal civilization. The progression of divine history—from creation, to life, to civilization, and eventually to the ordered rule represented by Lucan and Millera—is interpreted as evidence that structured society is part of the proper development of the mortal world. Icosi feudalism consequently exists comfortably alongside the teachings of the Order. Families inherit lands, titles, offices, obligations, and reputations from those who preceded them. An inherited responsibility is not considered solely a privilege belonging to its current holder, but something entrusted to them by previous generations and eventually owed to those who follow. Abandoning such a responsibility may therefore possess both social and religious implications. Royalty occupies a particularly elevated position. Because rulership represents the final stage of civilization associated with Lucan and Millera, royal blood is traditionally regarded as spiritually closer to the gods than that of the lowborn. This does not make every king, queen, prince, or noble virtuous. Rank and piety are distinct. A ruler may occupy a position nearer to the divine order while still proving themselves impious, dishonorable, or otherwise unworthy of the responsibilities inherited from their ancestors. The Order maintains an organized male priesthood. Only clergymen are permitted to enter the interior of its temples. Worshippers generally gather outside, where they kneel in prayer or assemble to hear sermons delivered by priests. Priests are forbidden from ordinary labor, hunting, and personal indulgence. Their material needs are instead supported by contributions from followers, most commonly food and money. This dependence allows the clergy to devote themselves wholly to religious life while making the support of the priesthood an ordinary expression of devotion among believers. Public sermons form an important part of the Order's religious life. Priests recount stories of the Ancients, interpret their deeds, offer warnings or lessons drawn from them, and may relate these teachings to circumstances affecting the society around them. Chroniclers are commonly hired to record what is said during important sermons. Because sermons participate in the continuing development of the faith, religious interpretation can change alongside the historical circumstances in which the Order exists. The pantheon remains defined, but the prominence afforded to particular stories, Ancients, and interpretations may shift over time. Temples of the Order are enormous structures, commonly comparable in scale to castles and royal palaces. Their size reflects both the influence of the faith and the reverence afforded to the Ancients. The interior is reserved for the clergy and houses objects of religious significance, including relics attributed to the gods themselves. Ordinary worship instead takes place outside the temple, where large gatherings may assemble before its entrance for prayer and sermons. The specific architectural and visual traditions distinguishing these temples from secular Icosi buildings vary from region to region and are not universally defined. Most adherents pray privately. Particularly devout adherents may regularly travel to a local temple to kneel among other worshippers and listen to public sermons. Followers may also bring offerings of food or money to sustain the clergy. The orthodox Order does not require constant public demonstrations of devotion for a person to be considered a follower. Followers may dedicate themselves to one Ancient as their patron deity. Such patronage carries significance beyond mortal life, as faithful service is believed to determine the domain into which a believer passes after death. The choice of patron does not require a follower to deny or reject the other Ancients. Priests frequently oversee births. Their presence places the beginning of mortal life beneath the observation of a faith whose creation tradition attributes life itself to Adena. Marriage ceremonies are commonly overseen by priests. The precise rites involved may differ, but clerical participation places the union within the religious traditions of the Order. Priests oversee funeral pyres and the rites surrounding the dead. Death carries particular significance because the deceased is believed to pass either into the domain of their patron or, if found without divine favor, into the Realm of the Scorned. Religious involvement in coronations varies between families and regions, but the Order participates more often than not. A ruler maintaining visible good standing with the faith is regarded favorably by both worshippers and clergy, and religious participation reinforces the association between rulership and the divine order represented by Lucan and Millera. On particularly significant occasions, relics attributed to the Ancients may be removed from temples and incorporated into coronation rites. Birthdays may likewise involve the Order according to regional or familial preference, though religious participation is not universal. Objects believed to have belonged to or been associated with the Ancients are preserved within temples. Such relics are rarely taken outside. Exceptions may be made for events of exceptional religious or political significance, particularly major coronations and funerals. Relics associated with Lucan and Millera hold obvious significance during ceremonies involving royalty, though objects associated with other Ancients may also be used when considered appropriate. The rarity with which these objects leave their temples contributes to their importance when they are displayed publicly. Disagreement within the Order is not automatically met with persecution. Competing interpretations and theological discourse may persist so long as they remain limited enough not to challenge the broader authority of the established faith. The most significant divide concerns mortal divinity. Certain denominations believe extraordinary mortals possess the capacity to become god-like through accomplishments comparable to those attributed to the Ancients. Orthodox doctrine regards this belief as fundamentally incompatible with the divine nature of the gods. As long as such beliefs remain relatively marginal, they may exist primarily as subjects of dispute. When a heretical movement gains sufficient influence to become a rival institution or sect, the response may become political. The Order may call upon the crown to suppress the movement, with punishments ranging from imprisonment to execution. The matter therefore becomes most dangerous when theological dissent acquires enough followers, wealth, or influence to challenge established religious authority. The Order generally has little interest in the private beliefs of foreigners who live beyond its political and religious sphere. This tolerance becomes considerably narrower where authority is concerned. Those who seek titles or positions of influence within societies dominated by the Order are expected to exist within its religious framework. Foreign belief becomes more contentious when it intersects with political legitimacy. Attitudes toward competing religions also differ among sects and individuals. Adherents of the Faith of the Hidden Ones, the dominant tradition throughout much of Athura, are commonly regarded by followers of the Ancients as misguided and intellectually inferior for their beliefs. More fervent elements of the Order go further, viewing followers of the Hidden Ones as people who knowingly turn their backs upon the gods. Such sects may pursue conversion aggressively and, in extreme circumstances, resort to violence against competing faiths. These attitudes are not universal throughout the Order. The Order and the secular rulers of Icos occupy separate but closely connected positions within society. The faith provides theological legitimacy to hereditary rule by treating hierarchy as the natural condition of civilized mortals. In turn, rulers may offer protection and political support to the established religious order. A monarch benefits from being seen in good standing with the clergy, particularly during coronations and other ceremonies intended to establish legitimacy before both subjects and ancestors. The relationship is not without tension. The Order may appeal to secular rulers when religious disputes threaten its authority, while rulers must contend with a faith capable of shaping public perceptions of honor, ancestry, legitimacy, and proper governance. Neither institution can easily disregard the other. Although the Order possesses no singular moral code, several expectations recur throughout its traditions: Honor inherited responsibilities: What is received from one's predecessors should not be discarded without cause. Keep one's oaths: A sworn obligation carries moral and social weight beyond personal convenience. Respect guest right: Hospitality establishes obligations between host and guest which should not be casually violated. Honor one's ancestors: The living inherit more than property from the dead. Recognize the distinction between mortal and divine: Greatness does not make a mortal an Ancient. Respect rightful hierarchy: Ordered ranks and inherited obligations are regarded as part of civilized existence. How these principles are prioritized depends heavily upon the believer. For one follower, breaking an oath may represent the worst stain upon a person's character. For another, abandoning a family responsibility or disgracing one's ancestors may be more shameful. What unites them is the belief that a mortal is not an isolated individual, but someone situated within obligations inherited from the past and carried toward the future.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Elektrokin","u":"wiki/elektrokin.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Kinmatter","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A nascent form of kinmatter. Kiro has some grasp of it, courtesy of a deceased Jotu'un master, though he barely produces a spark.","b":"A nascent form of kinmatter. Kiro has some grasp of it, courtesy of a deceased Jotu'un master, though he barely produces a spark.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Hidrokin","u":"wiki/hidrokin.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Kinmatter","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Practiced by S'Afar and Yanna of Neru'Augma.","b":"Practiced by S'Afar and Yanna of Neru'Augma.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Jeokin","u":"wiki/jeokin.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Kinmatter","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A form of kinmatter. A disillusioned sect of the jotuun used it to raise the river that became Mandor Canyon; Mirak used it against Mephisto. Practiced by S'Afar and Yanna.","b":"A form of kinmatter. A disillusioned sect of the jotuun used it to raise the river that became Mandor Canyon; Mirak used it against Mephisto. Practiced by S'Afar and Yanna.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Mnemokin","u":"wiki/mnemokin.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Kinmatter","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Practiced by Yanna of Neru'Augma.","b":"Practiced by Yanna of Neru'Augma.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Pirokin","u":"wiki/pirokin.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Kinmatter","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Practiced by S'Afar of Neru'Augma. Terra dra'iq can shake off intense pirokin attacks.","b":"Practiced by S'Afar of Neru'Augma. Terra dra'iq can shake off intense pirokin attacks.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Quintecrakin","u":"wiki/quintecrakin.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Kinmatter","c":"#5c66f5","s":"An ancient form of kinmatter: the ability to manipulate life energy. With it, one can drain the life from oneself and redirect it to someone else. Reversed, it could drain another's life and give it to oneself. It's very dangerous, and forbidden within the jot","b":"An ancient form of kinmatter: the ability to manipulate life energy. With it, one can drain the life from oneself and redirect it to someone else. Reversed, it could drain another's life and give it to oneself. It's very dangerous, and forbidden within the jotuun order, as it goes against their principles of nonviolence and compassion. Augmen studied it from forbidden jotuun texts, and was expelled for it. It's practiced by S'Afar, leader of Neru'Augma.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Belvium","u":"wiki/belvium.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Materials","c":"#5c66f5","s":"One of the two materials, with gold, of the interior gate of Orsena.","b":"One of the two materials, with gold, of the interior gate of Orsena.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Bruthil","u":"wiki/bruthil.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Materials","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The material of The Gates of Shakir in Orsena. Rhodite is typically depicted in bruthil armor.","b":"The material of The Gates of Shakir in Orsena. Rhodite is typically depicted in bruthil armor.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Atlae","u":"wiki/atlae.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Races","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The most dominant sentient species on the planet Artisans are the atlae, who resemble modern humans to a degree. Averaging a height of 5'5\", they possess large cat-like ears that vary in shape and size depending on their race. Their tails, much like their ears","b":"The most dominant sentient species on the planet Artisans are the atlae, who resemble modern humans to a degree. Averaging a height of 5'5\", they possess large cat-like ears that vary in shape and size depending on their race. Their tails, much like their ears, also differ in shape, length, and patterning based on their race. The biodiversity among the atlae suggests that several distinct species once existed across the globe. Over time, these species evolved into the modern atlae, now primarily inhabiting two main land masses. Their average lifespan is 123 years. Within their mouths, atlae don't differ much from humans other than the fact that their tongues are 40% flatter, and that their canines develop quicker, being the first few teeth to grow during infancy, and larger, providing a significantly more wounding bite than a human could. Adorning the tips of their toes and fingers rest thick claws that vary in shape and length from individual to individual. This seems largely unaffected by ethnic differences. Their claws sheathe a bone that helps strengthen the structure, making them effective tools for climbing and prying shelled foods open with ease. An atlaes pupils are reflective and their eyeballs are larger than human eyes by 20%, allowing them to capture more light and see in low-light conditions far better. The ability to contract their pupils into cat-like slits seemingly at will is indicative of low-to-the-ground predatory hunting during their prehistory. Atlae have tails that vary in length depending on the individual, and within Icosian tradition they're typically removed at birth as it's seen as 'primitive' by many noble families. Those too old to clip their tails without permanently injuring themselves prefer hiding it to appear more fashionable. However, most cultures around the known world of Artisans embrace their tails, and some adorn them with jewelry, fashioning them beautifully. The atlae of Athura have a higher likelihood of being born with patterns on their hair and skin resembling the big cats of Earth. Conversely, the atlae of Icos are more likely to be born with more body hair and bulkier physiques. White hair is a rare genetic anomaly, and a bad omen. People believe it brings death, as white is the color most commonly associated with Oies, the God of Death. In Icos, those born with it are treated far more harshly, with many punishments seeming almost disproportionate. Opraxi atlae typically have pale skin with jet-black hair and grey, monolid eyes. Their eyebrows are round, almost circle spots instead of the long, horizontal eyebrows you might expect. They typically come in about a foot shorter than the average height for an atlae, and their tails vary a bunch. Some opraxi have long, sleek, pantherine tails. While others have wide, yet stubby cotton balls. Uprorian atlae have historically been outcasted time and time again due to their inclination to associate with barbaric cultures. Their physical appearance doesn't quite help with that reputation either. They have a taller, more muscular build, with rounded ears and more hair coverage. Needless to say, your average atlae would be intimidated just being in the presence of an uprorian. Especially paired with the exceptionally rare genetic mutation resulting in white eyes. Their hair colors range from ginger to beige, and their skin from pale to... well, pale. Given that Upror in the present day has largely been vacant, aside from the odd \"feral\" men that'll show up every now and again, it's getting increasingly harder to find a pureblood uprorian. Morgari atlae share a lineage with uprorians but embrace a passive and nomadic lifestyle. The majority of Morgar's inhabitants don't live in or around any major settlements, and choose to live off the land the old fashioned way. They have physical characteristics similar to uprorians such as the massive height and muscle mass, as well as the thicker body hair, but can be distinguished by their thick northern accent. Ashelan atlae are culturally diverse but can be identified by their fair skin, blonde or brown hair, and an accent reminiscent of New Zealanders with a mix of British. The atlae on the eastern coast of Stade commonly have an olive complexion, with wavy hair and brown eyes. These atlae are as average as they come. Not too tall, not too short, not too thin, not too bulky. Their claws are usually shaven and tails trimmed since that's what passes as 'proper' in their culture. Anklanae atlae are shorter than average, and are acclimated to the seas, hunting and gathering from coral reefs and traveling via fishing boats. These atlae are of an umber complexion with rich brown hair. Their hair's texture is wavy to curly. Traditionally, the atlae of Anklan'A need to be fair swimmers if they're to be taken seriously, so despite being smaller than average, natural selection favored atlae with a healthier set of lungs and a knack for athleticism. Matause atlae have dark skin, thick eyelashes, black hair, and brown eyes. Their ears are smaller and rounded, and some have panthera-like spots on their hair and skin. Historically, the atlae of Matau were slaves and slavers, so a mix of various ethnic groups and dead cultures make up what matause people are now. Matause atlae are taller than average, and are generally known for their bad tempers. Pair this with their knack for physicality and things can get bloody quick. Though, this isn't all bad, as physical touch is also their love language. They're inclined to be very touchy when introducing themselves to familiarize themselves with others. Balian atlae are, very similarly to matause atlae, a diverse group shaped by slave trades, immigration, and wars within Bali. They have clay skin, brown to black hair, and brown to orange eyes with pointed, tufted ears. Their noses are somewhat downward curved, and their eyes are typically monolid like the opraxi. They possess lynx-like spots on their hands and forearms, feet and shins, along their spine and on their tails, and around their necks. Abuun atlae are born with beige or orange hair, with a honey complexion and bright eye colors like green, yellow, and orange. Atlae of Abu have larger, pointed tipped ears with a rare chance of being born with tiger-like stripes on their skin and hair.","n":""},{"t":"Azkassari","u":"wiki/azkassari.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Races","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Azkassari are the atlae of fallen Azkassar. Most noble families from Linnenmoire and Endlemoire descend from Azkassari ancestors who fled Azkassar before the calamity. See The Fall of Azkassar. Their blood survives to this day in those noble families becau","b":"The Azkassari are the atlae of fallen Azkassar. Most noble families from Linnenmoire and Endlemoire descend from Azkassari ancestors who fled Azkassar before the calamity. See The Fall of Azkassar. Their blood survives to this day in those noble families because, after fleeing, they considered themselves of purer blood than the other atlae around them. They engaged in avunculate incest, and married between other Azkassari families, for many generations. The surviving families like to spread the notion that their blood is more pure because they claim heritage to Lucan and Millera. The Azkassari aren't descended from either. When Azkassar fell, some of its noble families fled to Ibya, where they lived as refugees among people they considered beneath them. Many found the Ibyans' simple governance easy to understand, and grew wealthy as advisors, commanders, and even landholders. Quietly, they gained influence and gathered soldiers. Knowing they stood no chance of challenging Ibya so soon, they went west to build a feudal realm by subjugating weaker peoples. The houses that had allied with one another founded Linnenmoire, the capital of Icos and the first city-state, and with it the beginning of feudalism on Icos. See The Azkassari in Ibya. After The Cremation of Linnenmoire, Endlemoire was built upon its ruins, and the Larrensires came to rule there, as they still do. The Barrentyne are another Azkassari noble house. The Valentyres, who are merchants, never established an estate in Linnenmoire with the rest, and their blood has been diluted. Those of Azkassari blood are easily identifiable from their pale skin, tall and elegant figures, dark hair, and red eyes. Their nose bridge is straight, and their profile is flat and linear from the forehead to the tip. Their long faces help them stand even further apart from other atlae ethnic groups.","n":""},{"t":"Groll","u":"wiki/groll.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Races","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Large, bipedal predators that resemble foxes or hyenas, Grolls are formidable creatures found throughout the world of Artisans. Standing at the imposing height of 8 feet on average, some individuals can reach up to 10 feet. Their average lifespan is 92 years. ","b":"Large, bipedal predators that resemble foxes or hyenas, Grolls are formidable creatures found throughout the world of Artisans. Standing at the imposing height of 8 feet on average, some individuals can reach up to 10 feet. Their average lifespan is 92 years. Grolls possess immense strength and agility, making them fearsome fighters, and when in groups, they're lethal threats to any and everything they encounter. Grolls lead a nomadic lifestyle, making them highly unpredictable and feared across all regions. Originating in the Northwestern hemisphere, they typically have a spotted coat of matted fur that provides insulation against the cold. Their communication consists of grunts, barks, howls, and yips that unsettlingly resemble laughter. Despite being more intelligent than most beasts, being capable of using tools, they're often underestimated by both atlae and ringo, who consider them unintelligent due to their higher wit. However, Grolls are extremely tough to outmaneuver and should never be taken lightly. Native to arctic regions, Snow Grolls have significantly more body mass compared to other Grolls and possess thicker fur coverage to withstand extreme cold. They lead a more isolated lifestyle, often found in the icy expanses of Icos. Their coloration ranges from piercing white to black, but their patterning is usually very muted, almost not even being visible. Savanna Grolls inhabit the warmer grasslands and savannas as their name suggests. But it isn't uncommon to find them roaming cooler regions as they're acclimated to many climates. They have shorter fur and are leaner compared to their northern counterparts, adapted for speed and endurance in open terrains. Their coloration can be all manner of hues, but white and black specimens are rare for Savanna Grolls. Their patterning is very prominent, boasting black spots all over their body, and some specimens have an uncommon mutation that turns those spots into thick stripes. Jungle Grolls are adapted to dense, tropical forests. They have a slightly smaller stature but are incredibly agile and adept at navigating through thick vegetation. As their name suggests, they're sparsely found in the more tropical regions of Athura, where they were able to remain unnoticed during the wastings. A jungle in central Athura was burned down in 173 BBU to cull a massive groll population, and those that slipped through the wastings simply slipped beneath Alusha's proverbial radar. Although, they actively avoid the Tan'we Jungle due to them being unable to compete with the natural predators there. Specifically Tirvaan, who seem to actively hunt them down whenever their scent is picked up. The fur of Jungle Grolls is often darker to blend in with their environment, with similar spotting as Savanna Groll, however instead of solid spots, they're rosettes. Their fur coloration ranges from brown to black, but the rare orange or yellow mutation is still possible. Cave Grolls dwell primarily in mountainous regions. Having a stockier, robust build to navigate rocky terrains and tight spaces. Their fur is coarse and commonly a variation of grey to match the stone surroundings.","n":""},{"t":"Ringo","u":"wiki/ringo.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Races","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The second most dominant sentient species on the planet Artisans. Originating from Athura, the ringo, without their tools, clothing, and other defining characteristics, would appear no different from any other wild animal on Artisans. They have an average heig","b":"The second most dominant sentient species on the planet Artisans. Originating from Athura, the ringo, without their tools, clothing, and other defining characteristics, would appear no different from any other wild animal on Artisans. They have an average height of 3'11'' and resemble large, bipedal rodents, specifically mice and rats. Their average lifespan is 192 years. Ringo are brilliant creatures, often outsmarting atlae, but due to their physical disadvantages, they haven't had an easy time reigning dominant over the atlae in recent history. There are several distinct subspecies of ringo, each with their own varying characteristics. TBD Pisewi 'Islander' Ringo are the smallest and rarest ringo ethnic group, native to Namona Pise and beyond The Southern Isles. These ringo are so isolated from the rest of the world that they still practice much of old ringo culture. Some traditions several thousands of years old, and with that much time passing by for this island-dwelling subspecies, some physical changes were sure to appear. Aside from the obvious smaller stature, the fur coat of the Pisewi Ringo is dense and shiny. Most commonly possessing muted browns and beiges with minimal patterning, their fur can also come in shades of black and blonde. The color of their eyes is scantly seen, as their iris is quite small due to the large size of their pupils, giving them a doe-eyed appearance. They're able to swim much better than their mainland cousins, and thanks to their thick, hydrophobic fur, are also able to avoid being drenched and survive more extreme weather conditions. Ringo native to lands that are hot, dry, or humid. These ringo are found all over Athura and tend to have short, thin fur, with beautiful patterning and vibrant colors. Sudr'iq ringo are believed to be the most intelligent, with many notable ringo artisans, inventors, and scholars being sudr'iq. But this is likely due to social bias, with atlae giving them more opportunities than other ethnicities. Their ears, unlike the average ringo, are pointed and usually hold tufts on the tips. Their tails are hairless, usually with another tuft on the tip. They're born blind and entirely hairless. Their eyes are brightly colored like gemstones, which inspired the tradition for ringo to carry a gemstone on their persons the same color as their eyes, as it's believed to bring good fortune. Ringo native to lands that are cold. Primarily only found on the continent of Icos; these ringo carry the worst reputation out of the few, so you're not likely to see many roaming anywhere near known civilization. These ringo have dense matted fur and are the second smallest ethnic group. Their eyes are brightly colored, but the range is mostly but not entirely limited to red, blue, turquoise, and beige. These ringo have the least impressive patterns, and you'll very seldom find a darker color on their coat. They can be seen having blonde, white, grey, and blue fur most commonly. Their negative reputation largely stems from the days of old, when atlae first started to expand their reach onto Icos. Explorers would wander into the wilderness, charting unknown regions, and stumble into sun'om ringo territory. Unknowing and afraid, the ringo acted with aggression, and the atlae did the same. Souring relations for generations to come. Ringo native to lands in the middle. Lands that are neither hot, or cold. You'll find these ringo in pretty much every city on both continents, despite carrying a negative reputation. Unlike the sun'om, whose reputation is built off of their aggression. The sutu'uns reputation is built upon trust. Or lack thereof. The list of notable sutu'un ringo is comprised primarily of outlaws and pirates most notorious for cheating and murdering their way to infamy. Able to possess both long and short fur with decent patterning and varied colors, these ringo have an easy time masking their identity as a sutu'un, where most typically try to pass as the much more trusted sudr'iq. As a result, many people today on artisans are skeptical of all ringo. Some places even go so far as to ban ringo entirely just because of the stigma. The tails of sutu'un can be either skinned or bushy, but their eyes, much unlike both sudr'iq and sun'om, are usually dull in coloration.","n":""},{"t":"Titan","u":"wiki/titan.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Races","c":"#5c66f5","s":"An especially large humanoid that comes in a whole range of sizes, shapes, and pigmentations, with an average lifespan of 814 years. They're not too intelligent and are historically easy to trick or manipulate, though there are a number of titans that are know","b":"An especially large humanoid that comes in a whole range of sizes, shapes, and pigmentations, with an average lifespan of 814 years. They're not too intelligent and are historically easy to trick or manipulate, though there are a number of titans that are known for their exceptional intelligence, a handful even going so far as to erect cities of their own. They originate in the northwestern hemisphere just like the grolls, and maintained a steady population in the north for centuries. But given the population was rather small for such large creatures, they often inbred, thus giving us the titan monsters we saw two-hundred years prior. Presently, however, there are no known titans that exist on Artisans, and they're all presumed to be extinct. The ruins of their ancient civilization still remain untouched in the harsh cold of The White Abyss. The titans withdrew north after the battles told in The Death of Archon.","n":""},{"t":"Athuran Groll Wastings","u":"wiki/athuran-groll-wastings.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The eradication of the native grolls of Abu, begun by Alusha the Vengeful, and the violence that followed their return. As of 211 ABU, few grolls are left on Athura. War is waged on the entire groll population in the region of Abu by Alusha the Vengeful after ","b":"The eradication of the native grolls of Abu, begun by Alusha the Vengeful, and the violence that followed their return. As of 211 ABU, few grolls are left on Athura. War is waged on the entire groll population in the region of Abu by Alusha the Vengeful after her children were mutilated by members of a groll tribe in the then capital city of Sundiri. It didn't take much to convince the rest of Abu to rally to her cause, as there were very very few positive relations with grolls at the time, and as such, it only took a decade to completely eradicate the native groll population. A jungle in central Athura was burned down to cull a massive groll population. A few years after the wasting, and the division of the Athuran Sultanate, the jotuun started advocating on behalf of all groll to be accepted back into Athuran society. This was successful, as the jotuun had learned how to speak the groll's native tongue, Upper Et'lesh & Lower Et'lesh, and it didn't take long for the grolls to learn how to speak pumesh as well. Alusha the Vengeful would rise once again, reminding the nations of \"what monsters the grolls truly were\" in staged events that baited several groll tribes to attack her pawns. She began by plotting for the few Athuran leaders who sided with her to be taken by her closest followers, only to instigate contempt between the rulers and the grolls by making their comments and actions taken against the grolls publicly known. She set up staged, public executions by allowing the agitated grolls to mutilate the Athuran rulers as horrified onlookers watched the primal display. And as harsh as this was, it was exceedingly effective in twisting the narrative that the grolls were monsters and nothing more. Afterward, the local jotuun were left with no choice but to defend the few groll tribes who swore to peace, leaving the others to their fates at the hands of rogue atlae militia. At the time of the Bicontinental Union, the grolls on Athura are few and far between, and the public opinion about them hasn't changed much. The few surviving tribes being safeguarded by the jotuun still remain active, though the pacifist mindset seems to be ever-quickly declining. Some grolls even become jotuun themselves, though the message spoken by Alusha and her followers remains echoing in the heads of both atlae and groll alike.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"King Baesilius Morwurth","u":"wiki/king-baesilius-morwurth.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Ten-Year War","u":"wiki/ten-year-war.html","a":["The Great Jotuun Purge","The Ten-Year War"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Also called the Great Jotuun Purge: the war of extermination waged on the jotuun by Anteo Zuul and the ex-jotuun Augmen, unparalleled in history. Unable to move openly against the powerful jotuun, Anteo met with Augmen in secret, and together they devised the ","b":"Also called the Great Jotuun Purge: the war of extermination waged on the jotuun by Anteo Zuul and the ex-jotuun Augmen, unparalleled in history. Unable to move openly against the powerful jotuun, Anteo met with Augmen in secret, and together they devised the war. The campaign began a year later. Anteo kept the Temple of Isi, where Augmen's daughter, Ayala, had gone to train, for last, knowing that once Augmen had her, he'd have no use for the war. Augmen was much more determined to find her than Anteo would've liked, so he bargained with him. Isi would be spared, and Ayala with it, for as long as Augmen did as he was told. Augmen held to it until he couldn't. Augmen aided the Zuul Union in dispatching multiple jotuun temples, and destroying any who supported them. Revered for its loyalty to the jotuun, Ancrage hid monks in a temple beneath the city. In retaliation, Anteo and Augmen launched a brutal siege. Augmen shattered the battlements by hurling massive stone spires from the surrounding mountains, breaching the walls and devastating the city's infrastructure. The city lay in ruins. The purge raged for a decade, devastating Athura. Countless temples fell, and by its end, an estimated 98% of the jotuun populace was destroyed. It ended with the siege of the Temple of Isi. By the end, there was nothing left to burn. The survivors had fled to the one place Anteo wouldn't touch, and Augmen had driven the whole order into his daughter's house. Anteo told him so before giving the word. Augmen went in first, to be the one to reach her, and found Ayala among the refugees. Believing he could 'rescue' her, he slaughtered the defenders without mercy, and killed Prior Ad Regalis, once his master. Ayala pleaded with her father to stop. He tried to talk to her, and told her of the bargain, that she'd been safe for ten years because of him. She understood then who had paid for it. Realizing he was lost, she waited for her chance. When other jotuun distracted him, Augmen faltered, and Ayala launched a stone spire into his back, killing him instantly. Anteo, though deprived of his enforcer, reveled in a near-total victory as the order collapsed. Ayala survived the final culling and disappeared into obscurity with seven others (see Qis), one of whom she later took as her secret apprentice: Mirak, a ringo youth who'd been in the order since before the war, at the Temple of Khada. Though ruined in the purge, Ancrage was later rebuilt as a symbol of resilience. The city flourished, incorporating the very spires once used to destroy it into its design. The largest now stands as a monument to the families lost in the war.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Battle of Suran","u":"wiki/the-battle-of-suran.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Cremation of Linnenmoire","u":"wiki/the-cremation-of-linnenmoire.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A long time ago, around the beginning of the Old Kingdom. Linnenmoire, the first major city on the continent of Icos, housed a stolen Dra'iq egg underneath the keep. Taken from a lair deep within the Kintari Mountain range during a hunt commissioned by the lat","b":"A long time ago, around the beginning of the Old Kingdom. Linnenmoire, the first major city on the continent of Icos, housed a stolen Dra'iq egg underneath the keep. Taken from a lair deep within the Kintari Mountain range during a hunt commissioned by the late King Baesilius Morwurth, who intended to keep the hatchling as a pet. Baesilius boasted, touted, and even held ceremony. Souring relations with the titans, fully believing he would not fail. He stated that soon the Atlae will no longer need the protection of the titans as they now held the power of a dra'iq. After hatching, the infant dra'iq demanded much from the king. Food, space, someone to play with--all of which they struggled to provide effectively. It didn't take long before the infant grew so unsatisfied that it began to wail every night, loud enough that the city grew restless. So much so that the populace began fearing for their safety, prompting them to find someplace else to stay. One night, the last night for Linnenmoire, the infant cried the loudest it ever has. Driven to madness, King Baesilius finally demanded that the infant be slain, but such a deed would never come to pass as a dra'iq had been sighted flying toward the city--its mother. Within minutes, the illustrious Linnenmoire would be razed to the ground, and its citizens with it. Stones melted over the now glassed streets. Innocents perished as they prayed, wondering if the death their king invited would allow their souls to pass into the afterlife. Many families that had left in the months prior wrought their own noble houses in the years past. A few lasted for many generations, though the rest fell victim to the same fate. The scorned mother's wrath.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Crowning of Onfroi","u":"wiki/the-crowning-of-onfroi.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"The judgment in Karlaine by which Aethel Zuul refused to recognize Cedric Talvo as king, and made a king of his fifteen-year-old son, Onfroi. It took place the same year Anteo Zuul died. What began as a small hamlet on the shores of Athura had become something","b":"The judgment in Karlaine by which Aethel Zuul refused to recognize Cedric Talvo as king, and made a king of his fifteen-year-old son, Onfroi. It took place the same year Anteo Zuul died. What began as a small hamlet on the shores of Athura had become something considerably harder to dismiss (see The Founding of Trion). Trion had developed into a political center. Novon had grown around its edges, made up of refugees from Oprax living under the protection and lordship of Lord Hiro Kuroshi, and at the cliff's peak stood Greystone, its limestone walls built for generations to live within. The region had cropland, livestock, mines, a growing harbor, and a people willing to recognize Talvo authority. What it didn't have was legitimacy. To the rulers of the Bicontinental Union, Cedric was the descendant of a disgraced house, and the old accusations of treason had never entirely disappeared (see Talvo Royal Family). A kingdom that existed only because its neighbors hadn't yet disputed it was vulnerable to any ruler powerful enough to do so. Cedric returned to Icos with Hiro, Onfroi, and a small delegation, with the appearance of a diplomatic mission and too few men to resemble a challenge. Rumor placed the High King not at High Sonham, but in Karlaine, at the court of Mandel Kalmere. Winter had settled over the continent. Roads disappeared beneath the snow, and the frigid air sent Onfroi into coughing fits, which he attempted to conceal whenever his father looked toward him. He'd known of his family's history, and of Wietmoire, from stories told in the safety of Trion. Now he passed banners of houses that had outlived his own family's ruin, and settlements whose rulers thought the Talvos exiles, pretenders, or curiosities from overseas. When Cedric entered the royal hall, whispers followed him. \"The Prince Overseas.\" \"They dare call him a king?\" The stadian nobles held the oldest resentments, while the Morgari and Uprorian courtiers were less openly contemptuous. He ignored them all. Then he saw the throne, and upon it sat Aethel. Mandel stood among his own courtiers, quick to smile when Aethel spoke, and yielding space without being asked. Cedric recognized sycophancy when he saw it. Aethel appeared to recognize it as well, and at moments his expression suggested irritation. Yet he went on sitting upon Mandel's throne. He might've disliked being flattered, but he didn't dislike being obeyed. Beside him stood his younger son, Alfor, watching. In the center of the hall, Cedric drew his sword. A guard's steel slid partway from its scabbard. Cedric paid him little attention, placed the sword upon the floor before him, and knelt. The two men had known one another years before, when neither held the position he now did, and Aethel didn't indulge it. \"State your purpose.\" Cedric explained what had been built in southern Athura. He asked that the High Crown recognize Talvar as a kingdom within the Union, and then asked to be acknowledged as its king. Aethel's eyes settled upon Onfroi, who stood beside Hiro, doing his best to remain composed. Then he looked back to Cedric. \"And what will you do for me, brother?\" \"Forgive me, but is being ruler of eight kingdoms not enough?\" Several nobles gasped. The Union had six. Cedric had counted Oprax, which refused it, and Talvar, which Aethel hadn't yet granted. Aethel's expression hardened only slightly. \"I meant no offense by it. Our families' histories have been negatively connected for generations. I intend to mend that.\" Aethel asked the boy his name, considered the answer, and offered him his daughter, Aeleina. It would've bound the ruling house of the Union to the Talvos, and made Talvar kin to the High Sovereignty. For a moment, it was everything Cedric had crossed the sea to obtain. But he'd already given his word. Onfroi was betrothed to Hiro's granddaughter, Kota. \"You married your son off to a lowborn? And an Opraxi at that.\" To many in the hall, the decision was incomprehensible. Hiro said nothing. He'd helped to preserve the Talvos when they had little left, and helped bring the refugees from whom Talvar had grown. Cedric had given his word before there were walls grand enough for foreign rulers to notice, and wouldn't break it now that keeping it was inconvenient. He affirmed the arrangement. The hostility he expected never came. Aethel faintly smiled. \"I respect a man of honor more than a man of desperation.\" Cedric wouldn't be recognized as King of Talvar. For an instant, the journey appeared to have failed. Then Aethel turned to Onfroi, and recognized him in his father's place. The decision astonished the court. It avoided elevating a man who carried the full weight of the Talvos' grievances, while acknowledging the realm he'd built. Onfroi had been a child when his family was killed, and had taken no part in the old dispute with the Kalmeres. His throne would also depend upon Cedric's administration for years. Cedric bowed his head and thanked Aethel. It wasn't the victory he'd requested, but Talvar had what it needed. Afterward, Aethel spoke with Cedric alone. He told him that Gideon hadn't knelt, and that he himself had stood there (see The Massacre at Wietmoire). He was the only Zuul who'd ever told Cedric the truth. The delegation left Karlaine with Cedric carrying no crown. His son did. For the first time since the fall of their ancestral city, the Talvos possessed a kingdom acknowledged by the highest authority in the Union. Cedric then took his son to see his mother, Lady Haileen Ermsburry.","n":""},{"t":"The Death of Archon","u":"wiki/the-death-of-archon.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The battles against the titans that cost Anteo Zuul his firstborn son, Archon Zuul. Archon rushed into battle to aid his friends in Stade, in their fight to push the forces of Morgar back from their homeland. In the cold north, he and his friend Sabba discover","b":"The battles against the titans that cost Anteo Zuul his firstborn son, Archon Zuul. Archon rushed into battle to aid his friends in Stade, in their fight to push the forces of Morgar back from their homeland. In the cold north, he and his friend Sabba discovered a regiment of Doli forces holding out in a frozen cave with titans. Thinking the titans had sided with his enemy, Sabba ambushed the encampment, using plastite to collapse the cave. He killed the atlae, but the titans rose from the debris and turned on the two of them. Before they could flee to safety, Sabba was caught by a titan and eaten alive. Archon escaped by the skin of his teeth, but not before witnessing his friend's demise. He told his allies in Stade that the titans were allied with the enemy, which spurred them into intense hatred, as the titans had given their word that they'd never get involved in atlae affairs. After recuperating, Archon and a few allies sought vengeance on the titan that killed Sabba. They killed one, but it wasn't the one they were looking for. This provoked the remaining titans into marching south and invading Morgar, killing a great many civilians. Seeing the carnage unfold upon his 'enemy' struck Archon with an unusual, intense feeling. Joras, another of his friends, opted to prevent the titans from reaching Stade. Archon agreed, only to realize they were far outmatched. Throughout his life, his arrogance and pride had led him from one battle to another, winning most, fearing little. It wasn't until he stood before the raging giants that he understood the feeling from earlier was fear. He knew he had no chance of stopping them without help from his father, the High King. He attempted to flee, but a titan grabbed him off of his mount, slammed him to the ground, and stepped on him, killing him instantly. A day later, Anteo and Endelyn caught wind of their son's actions. The informant conveyed it pedantically and chronologically. Furious and impatient, Anteo barked at him to get to the point, and only then learned of his son's demise. Wasting no time, he prepared for war. Fearing her husband would meet a similar fate, Endelyn traveled to a jotuun monastery to plead for assistance. Already aware of the battle, the jotuun were planning to intervene. Prior Ad Regalis told her she could accompany him and his apprentice, Augmen. Anteo and his infantry battled the titans in the forests between Upror and Morgar, where the titans were slowly but eventually overpowered. Anteo demanded answers, but they remained silent. Before he could give the order to slay them, the jotuun intervened and saved individuals on both sides. Unwilling to stand down, Anteo threatened them to stay out of it or there'd be consequences. The jotuun, equally as stubborn, refused. Suddenly an army of titans revealed itself, rising over a northern ridge. Intimidated, Anteo begrudgingly retreated, while the jotuun stayed to clean up the mess.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Fall of Azkassar","u":"wiki/the-fall-of-azkassar.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Kingdom of Azkassar was always real, even if some don't believe in it. The story of its founders, however, is a fable that has existed from time immemorial, and neither of them is proven to have existed. The Kingdom is said to have been ruled by a line of ","b":"The Kingdom of Azkassar was always real, even if some don't believe in it. The story of its founders, however, is a fable that has existed from time immemorial, and neither of them is proven to have existed. The Kingdom is said to have been ruled by a line of god-kings, of which all descend from Azkassar's founder, Lucan and his queen, Millera. Both held immense power in their own right, and are said to have ruled together in perfect harmony. Under their reign, the Azkassari were revered, and the realm prospered. Unprecedented levels of peace prevailed throughout. In spite of the generations of Azkassari rulers after them, however, neither are confirmed to have produced an heir to inherit their vast realm; further, none knew of their next-to-kin. After their deaths, the problem plagued the kingdom through many of its rulers, who frequently claimed legitimacy through blood, even that no one could prove such claims false. After more than eight hundred years of supremacy, the kingdom would eventually succumb to infighting between claimant factions, with the wars now collectively referred to as The God Wars. In its final years, priests and jotuun would warn of a great disaster that will befall the Azkassari Kingdom, and many of whom were punished. Tongues, throats, eyes, and ears were removed. Some were even executed; such an act was seen as a slight against the very gods they worshipped and claimed exalted blood from. At the tail end of these waning years, the land would crack violently and through the fissures, magma rose; from the sky, fire fell; and from the hills, lava spilled. The capital, Azkaria, that had once glittered so vibrantly was no more. The land that once seated the heart of the realm became lost to time, engulfed by the rising waters of Abunala Sea.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Fall of Malu","u":"wiki/the-fall-of-malu.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The dra'iq attack of 196 ABU that killed King Arabah, and made a king of the smuggler captain who drove it off, Alexium Ariadne. Malu was rebuilt, and renamed Phylomina. For some time, the city of Malu was ruled by a dignified and honest man, King Arabah. One ","b":"The dra'iq attack of 196 ABU that killed King Arabah, and made a king of the smuggler captain who drove it off, Alexium Ariadne. Malu was rebuilt, and renamed Phylomina. For some time, the city of Malu was ruled by a dignified and honest man, King Arabah. One evening, the peace was disturbed. A rumble, followed by a crash emitted from the southern city wall. From the dust and debris flew the biggest creature the city’s populace had ever seen. The Dra'iq, known only from fable, had revealed itself to the city of Malu. The people watched in horror as it began to lay waste to the city. Too late to save the southern districts, the army began to utilize their defenses. Many trebuchets and arrows were fired at the dra’iq, but few hit it, and for every shot that was missed, civilian casualties grew. The dra’iq, growing more agitated, swept the city walls, burning each and every soldier firing upon it. After three devastating sweeps, it soon began flying toward Malu Palace in the center of the bay. The city could only watch as the end drew near. The King did the only thing he knew how to in the moment. He knelt before his very throne and began praying. The towers around the palace toppled as wings crashed into them, falling onto the palace, fissuring the roof. Debris and rubble fell on many noblemen inside. A stray stone slammed the king in the chest, fracturing his chest. As he lay against a wall, attempting to catch his breath, he began to accept his fate. As things go quiet, he hears the ripple of flames barreling past the palace. It doesn't sound like dra'iq flames, no—he watches as a flaming projectile scorches across the sky and narrowly misses the dra'iq as it soared overhead. In unison, the people of Malu felt hope reclaim their hearts. On the horizon, smuggler vessels sailed into the bay and fired upon the beast with everything they had. Having avoided the vast majority of projectiles, the dra'iq dives upon the small fleet, and with a final wave of projectiles, the dra’iq pulled away from its attack—it had been shot in the eye. With the dra’iq retreating, flying west into the mountains, King Arabah demanded he know the names of each and every one of his saviors, to which the remaining servants and noblemen began to exit the palace and retrieve them. Once the leading ships had docked at the palace, the smuggling crew rushed into the palace to aide the king, only to be met with the sad image of his defeat. The smuggler captain had approached, and the king, not expecting to see his long-time friend, Alexium Ariadne, began to smile. Unable to laugh without coughing up blood, King Arabah attempted to make small talk and reminisce. But Alexium, along with everyone else in the great hall, knew the end was nigh for the king. As he struggles to keep his eyes open, he mutters his final plea, that Alexium takes his place as ruler of Malu. Alexium's heart skips. He rose and looked upon the city as smoke billows from the many ruins. Shaking his head, he asks the king how he's expected to manage, but returning his gaze, he finds that the king is now dead, and those around him stand at the ready, awaiting Alexium's orders. The next day, Alexium’s crewmates reported a strange howl coming from the caverns beneath the palace. Curious, they investigate. It didn’t take long for them to discover the reason the beast attacked the city. An infant dra’iq lay emaciated on the jagged stone shore at the mouth of the cavern, its weak howls being amplified by the cave. Unsure of what to do, he thought: If the dra’iq returns for its baby, and discovers it, there may be little damage. But if the dra’iq returns and the baby is dead, it will finish what it started. With that, Alexium decided the best course of action would be to nurture the infant until it was capable of surviving on its own and then release it back into the wild. This, however, didn’t take long. A week passed, and the infant was as spry as could be. Though still dangerous, King Alexium had it relocated safely and quietly back into the matause jungles. A year later, Alexium had finally found it safe to bring his family to Malu. A decent portion of the city had been rebuilt, but the scars from the year before are still obvious. Despite this, Alexium decreed that the city shall go by a new name, representative of its restoration. Malu henceforth shall be known as Phylomina. Alexium’s wife, Aiore, made a hobby out of foraging from the local jungles and would often take their very young daughter, Vaenera. On one of these days, Aiore reaches into a bush and grabs something warm. Instantly reeling back from the odd sensation, she takes a closer look. It’s a young dra’iq, the same one from before only slightly bigger. Though, she wouldn’t know that. Vaenera, being no older than seven, asks to keep it. Aiore, having lived in Tabla Pise for most of her life was ignorant of the dra’iq and so she agrees. They stow the infant into the fruit basket while it slept, and carry it back to Malu Palace. Eventually, Alexium had found out, and was furious. Everything he had worked toward—his enterprise, the city, his family—with the infant in their possession, all of it was now forfeit. For a month they had kept it a secret, locked in a room, feeding it fish. Aiore apologizes profusely, trembling in fear for what she has done. Vaenera, hearing all of this for the first time, gazes at the dra'iq, intrigued. It’s just an animal, surely it wouldn’t misbehave if it didn’t have reason to. Alexium asks Aiore where they found it and she states it was just outside of the wall in the berry grove. Alexium, not liking the sound of a dra’iq making its home so close to the city, ponders. Most dra’iq don’t abandon their territories once its been marked, so even if released, it will just grow right outside of the city. 'By killing it right here, we can prevent future suffering...but should its mother were to return—I dare not ponder the thought.' Seeing no rational alternative, Alexium opts to safeguard it until further notice, sending word to High King Alfor Zuul—not about the young dra'iq, that would remain a secret, but for the armaments required to defend against such a threat.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Fall of Talvar","u":"wiki/the-fall-of-talvar.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The destruction of Talvar by a dra'iq, and the ruin of the Talvo Royal Family that followed, before the Bicontinental Union was formed. Talvar towered over the ocean, upon cliffs just beyond the steppe. By the time Oswulf Talvo founded it, the Talvos and the C","b":"The destruction of Talvar by a dra'iq, and the ruin of the Talvo Royal Family that followed, before the Bicontinental Union was formed. Talvar towered over the ocean, upon cliffs just beyond the steppe. By the time Oswulf Talvo founded it, the Talvos and the Crannochs had made peace with the hilltribes of the western frontier, hence why the hilltribes allowed the city to be built quietly, with no conflict. The Talvos had forged alliances with them, and were their kings (see Talvo Royal Family). With the city's defenses nearly unassailable and their influence growing, they were undeniably a threat to the ruling house of Stade, the Kalmeres. Diplomacy became their shield, as they offered trade, tribute, and goodwill to a crown that fretted over them. They'd also raised a second bastion, High Sonham, hidden deep in the mountains. It was a refuge the hilltribes asked for, and asked that nobody be told of. The Talvos kept the promise, even from Stade. The Talvos were no one's vassals. A king of Stade who couldn't hold the west, when the Talvos already did, had released them from fealty by charter in return for a quiet border. The Zuuls, who wanted the west, found High Sonham under Aevor Zuul, and told the Kalmeres that only a rebel hides a fortress. The two houses entered into conspiracy with the rest of the realm, and waited for the moment Talvar might become vulnerable. A colossal dra'iq descended upon Talvar with devastating force, burning the city and crashing into the ground as though it were a living meteorite. Half the city collapsed into the sea. Before the Talvos could recover from the chaos, Tarris Kalmere, then a young king, declared them guilty of treason, for raising High Sonham in hiding. It was the one true thing in the charge against them. A king can't betray a crown he doesn't serve, so to call it treason, the Kalmeres had to say the charter never existed, and Tarris burned their copy of it. Stade's claim to the west rests on that lie. Stadian forces swept across the steppe to capture the surviving family. The Zuuls, their rivals, moved as swiftly, laying claim to High Sonham and capturing any Talvos who slipped past the crown's men. Cornered, starving, and outnumbered, the last king in the west, Conrad Talvo, bowed to the crown and swore fealty to spare his bloodline from execution, despite the Zuuls' demands for their heads. He didn't ask the tribes first. His crown was annulled, and by that the west became the king of Stade's by law. The Talvos returned to Stade humbled and stripped of their legacy, and later built Wietmoire Manor. The Zuuls were made Dukes of Tuvar. When the realms formed the Bicontinental Union, to put an end to the opportunism that followed every disaster of the dra'iq, the Talvos were excluded entirely, as their name still carried the stain of treason. The fall of Talvar, and the knee, gave the Kalmeres and Zuuls reason enough to war with the hilltribes, in an attempt to keep them weak. The Talvos' peace had been strengthening them. Had things never been discovered, the hilltribes could feasibly have become lords of a Talvo-led kingdom. The hilltribes remember the knee as clearly as they remember the granaries, and not all of them would welcome a Talvo back. The Talvos had a copy of the charter as well, and it was in High Sonham when the Zuuls took it. It's lost: the Zuuls don't know of it, and nobody has seen it since.","n":""},{"t":"The Founding of Trion","u":"wiki/the-founding-of-trion.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"How Cedric Talvo and Lord Hiro Kuroshi raised a new Talvar in southern Athura, in the years between The Massacre at Wietmoire and The Crowning of Onfroi. Several weeks after they arrived, Hiro took Cedric to a cave at the base of the cliff that overlooks Black","b":"How Cedric Talvo and Lord Hiro Kuroshi raised a new Talvar in southern Athura, in the years between The Massacre at Wietmoire and The Crowning of Onfroi. Several weeks after they arrived, Hiro took Cedric to a cave at the base of the cliff that overlooks Blackrock Bay. Nestled within was the accumulated fortune of the Talvos, or a hefty portion of it. Gideon had foreseen the challenges that lay ahead and hidden it there with Hiro, while Cedric saw to the refugees. Cedric hadn't known of it. Talvar was built with it. Cedric felt that he was forever indebted to Hiro, who wanted no part of the fortune for himself. Hiro asked only one thing of him: to marry Onfroi to his granddaughter, Kota. Recognizing the genuine connection between Onfroi and Hiro's grandchildren, Cedric found the proposition to be in the best interest of both families, and gave his word. With the succession of his family secured, Cedric's next goal was a stronghold for them to live within for the foreseeable future. For months, Hiro brought Opraxi refugees across the sea (see Internal Struggle) to live in the region and aid in the formation of Novon and Greystone, a keep constructed from the limestone deposits of the region that could last centuries. Even after Greystone's construction, the surplus resources facilitated a small town around the keep, and eventually a formidable wall, stretching from the southernmost to the westernmost cliff. This second, more noble settlement was named Trion, after the tallest keep in Talvar, his bloodline's fallen city of old. With Trion and Novon showing promise, Cedric's vision of a new kingdom began to take shape. A harbor was underway, the crops and livestock were ever-growing, and there was a busy mining operation along the shores of Blackrock Bay. In time, whispers of Talvar's existence reached Icosian shores, and the ears of those loyal to the High Crown. Cedric realized that political legitimacy was paramount if he wanted to secure a future for his family and their new home. See The Crowning of Onfroi.","n":""},{"t":"The God Wars","u":"wiki/the-god-wars.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The wars between claimant factions of Azkassar. See The Fall of Azkassar.","b":"The wars between claimant factions of Azkassar. See The Fall of Azkassar.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Massacre at Wietmoire","u":"wiki/the-massacre-at-wietmoire.html","a":["The Wietmoire Massacre"],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The killing of much of the Talvo Royal Family at Wietmoire Manor, their family home. It's an open secret that it was Anteo Zuul's doing, by then High King. When Talvar fell, the Zuuls had needed a king's law, and the Kalmeres' soldiers, to bring the Talvos dow","b":"The killing of much of the Talvo Royal Family at Wietmoire Manor, their family home. It's an open secret that it was Anteo Zuul's doing, by then High King. When Talvar fell, the Zuuls had needed a king's law, and the Kalmeres' soldiers, to bring the Talvos down. This time they were the law, and needed nobody. The High King sought not only dominion over Icos, but the eradication of any threat to his power. King Gideon Talvo, his son Cedric, and Lord Hiro Kuroshi weren't there when it happened. They were in Athura, bringing Opraxi refugees across the sea (see Internal Struggle). Assassins had come in the night, and left no mercy in their wake. Gideon, Cedric, and Hiro came home to the aftermath. As they moved through the bloodstained halls, Gideon's and Cedric's hearts shattered with each familiar face that stared lifelessly back at them. Women, children, pets, and mere servants all lay dead. Needing to escape it, they hurried out through one of the back doors and into the gardens. There, Cedric spotted a dying servant. With his last breath, the servant told him, \"Your mother, they t-took her... Zuul sent his regards.\" Cedric's mother, Queen Kaelyn Skyfall, would be killed by Anteo. Gideon, known for his level-headedness, succumbed to the overwhelming rage that consumed him. Cedric stood and asked to fight by his father's side, before a small child approached from the back of the gardens. It was his son, Onfroi. The servants had hidden him from what was unfolding within the manor. Cedric rushed over to embrace him. Gideon saw the love Cedric had for his son, and understood the feeling. He ordered Hiro to flee with the two of them to Athura. Cedric was torn between staying to fight and preserving his family's line, and pleaded with his father to face Anteo together. Gideon refused. It was a burden he believed must be faced alone. He stayed to face Anteo. Gideon walked into High Sonham unannounced. Anteo had Kaelyn brought in, and offered what the Talvos had taken once before: kneel, and she'd live (see The Fall of Talvar). Gideon had apologized to the hilltribes for that knee, and sworn that no Talvo would kneel again unless they said so. He began to go down, and Kaelyn cried out, telling him to refuse. He stood back up. Bending the knee to Anteo would have meant undoing everything he'd worked so hard to rebuild. Nobody in that room would walk out understanding that, but he held true to an oath, and it cost him. His wife made sure he remembered, even at the cost of her life. Anteo was visibly upset to hear her, and to see Gideon listen. He shouted, doubling down, wanting to revel in his victory. Gideon simply shook his head, silent, his face as still as stone. Anteo reiterated everything Gideon had just lost, and asked what more it would take for him to kneel. \"...Watching your wife's head roll?\" Gideon said one sentence, in his gruff voice. \"You are no king.\" Anteo drew his blade, huffing with anger. Gideon knew that if it came to a duel, he'd win. Anteo knew it as well, and ordered his men to force him onto his knees. Gideon was tall and strong, and they had to kick the bend of his knees in order to drop him. Kaelyn kicked and screamed the entire time, restrained by two men. Anteo approached, blade in hand, and Gideon simply lowered his head, already knowing the outcome. The sword rose, and it dropped. Kaelyn's blood-curdling scream rang throughout the walls of High Sonham, followed by the thud of Gideon's body dropping to the cold stones. Afterward, Anteo took his anger out on Kaelyn. He struck her with the pommel of the sword before tossing it aside to beat her with his bare hands, and strangled her to death in the very same room. He had them thrown from the walls into the ravine, and nothing of them was ever found. He wasn't the honorable type to bury his dead enemies. Aethel was in the room, and did nothing. His mother, Endelyn, heard it from her garden, where she was kneeling for the Talvos' dead. That night there were two more to pray for. The two of them never spoke of it. Anteo seized the manor, and the Zuuls have held it since. Dendrilla Ariadne lived there as Archon Zuul's wife. Lady Haileen Ermsburry, Onfroi's mother, was with her family, the Ermsburrys, in Endletonn that night. Anteo's order reached them before Cedric could, and she was kept there. The survivors raised a new Talvar in Athura, where Onfroi would be king. See The Founding of Trion.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Peoples of Old Icos","u":"wiki/the-peoples-of-old-icos.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"All of Icos at one point in time was tribal. Long ago, when Azkassar was the largest realm in all of Artisans, the people of Icos knew only a few native realms, and none of them practiced feudalism. Neither did Athura. Azkassar was the first civilization in hi","b":"All of Icos at one point in time was tribal. Long ago, when Azkassar was the largest realm in all of Artisans, the people of Icos knew only a few native realms, and none of them practiced feudalism. Neither did Athura. Azkassar was the first civilization in history to establish feudalism, and \"king\" was a title heard only there. Ibya was the first major power on Icos. It spanned what are now Pysos, Pavilia, Masseos, Erseos and Thena in the Ibyan Isles, along with most of the eastern coast and the minor islands around them, and it later founded a settlement on the mainland that became Acrenon. Modern icosi descend from them. The Ibyans knew nothing of kings, and they had no single ruler. Minor landholders, the strategoi, each governed a region, a nome, and the people who lived off its land. The strategoi were all of one culture, and they saw themselves as one realm: they traded with one another, dealt with one another, and needed one another. Community was paramount to them, so it was no surprise when they formed a senate of the governors, who listened to the people they served and made changes that would appeal to them, strengthening that sense of shared community. Some settlements grew into cities, and some cities grew over the domains of other landholders. When that happened, the city's governor would usually take the other governor in as a vassal, and in time became the holder of a city-state. The title didn't change, only the power behind it. In time, to compete with the other realms, Ibya had to reform its governance, centuries before 211 ABU. It took up the feudal ranks of its neighbours, and the greatest city-state holder, at Pavilia, was made Prince over the rest. The senate didn't survive it. The reform is what let Ibya join the Commonwealth of Stade as a principality rather than be swallowed. Not a formal realm, but a collection of tribes in central Icos called the Moirish, a name that roughly means \"people of the moorlands\". Their land was Moirach. They were a shorter atlae, who could make their home upon crags and moors alike. The term 'moire', as in Linnenmoire and Endlemoire, comes from these moors, which the Crannochs still hold. Not a formal realm, but a collection of tribes in the north called the Morgari. Their land was Morgai, now Morgar. Not a formal realm, but a collection of tribes in central Icos called the Oinu, the proto-Opraxi. Their land was Oinui. They lived as far north as where modern Endlemoire sits. They, alongside the Moirish, constantly fought the Ibyans, who sought to expand their reach further inland. Acrenon rose and fell several times during this era. Not a formal realm, but a collection of tribes in the south called the Gurtai. Their land was Guraxa. Not a formal realm, but a collection of tribes in the west called the Ror. Their land was Upror. They were shaggy atlae who grew about as tall as a groll. When Azkassar fell, some of its noble families fled to Ibya, where they lived as refugees among people they considered beneath them. Many found the Ibyans' simple governance easy to understand, and grew wealthy as advisors, commanders, and even landholders themselves. Quietly, they gained influence and gathered soldiers. They knew they stood no chance of challenging Ibya so soon. So they went west instead, to build a feudal realm by subjugating weaker peoples. The Azkassari houses that had allied with one another founded a settlement west of Ibya: Linnenmoire. Its name is a subtle nod that not all of it was won by bloody conquest. They mingled with the local tribes, and likely married into some of them. It was Icos' first major city, and the beginning of feudalism on Icos. In the north, a new seat was raised in imitation of the rulers down south, and was named Morgar. It took a long while to complete: a walled city atop a hill near the open plains, some miles south of where Ancrage sits today. Some of the noble families who'd been granted holdings away from Linnenmoire began to expect its downfall before it came. Karlaine was among them, though its dynasty and nobles weren't of Azkassari descent, but a mixture of Ibyan and Moirish. After The Cremation of Linnenmoire, over the course of a century, the surviving rulers across Icos established their realms as petty kingdoms and princedoms, each claiming the primacy of their forebear, Linnenmoire. Karlaine grew into the largest city on Icos, as people migrated there and traffic increased. The rest were keeps that towns later grew around, or villages that ballooned in size. The seats along the major trade routes grew the most. Linnenmoire's ruins were a mixture of blackened glass, ash and stone. An Azkassari house rebuilt the once-great city under a new name, Endlemoire, and the blackened glass of Linnenmoire still decorates its grand structures today. Endlemoire never reached the prosperity Linnenmoire once had. Faced with the threat of Morgar to the north, the Three Great Princes banded together and formed the Commonwealth of Stade, named for the high hill where they negotiated its creation. See Kingdom of Stade. In the southern fertile basin, the proto-Opraxi warred with the Gurtai. They pressed south from where Ōnari now sits to Sekitō, then west past Nōka and south past Toyohara. Gurtai tribes burned between Nōka and Kumoori, between Zousen and Yanebori, and near Kohan Castle. Others fled west, past Mukaikumo Castle, Yamamura, Isomura and Biwako, and out through the western pass that Sekijō Castle now watches. This took a couple of centuries. Over the generations thereafter, Oprax shaped into a feudal government, with several Ōkimi crowned at any given time, until they eventually crowned a Tennō to rule over the once-kings of the land.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Vaenera & Azra's Origin","u":"wiki/vaenera-azras-origin.html","a":[],"f":"Lore/Miscellaneous/Tales","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Once an infamous man, to both civilians and pirates alike, Kade Asher was the captain of a band of vagrants turned pirates. He fell out of a life of piracy when he and his crew made landfall on Tabla Pise, enabling him to meet a young Aiore; a gentle yet very ","b":"Once an infamous man, to both civilians and pirates alike, Kade Asher was the captain of a band of vagrants turned pirates. He fell out of a life of piracy when he and his crew made landfall on Tabla Pise, enabling him to meet a young Aiore; a gentle yet very headstrong woman. Here, within the Anklan Isles, she was royalty of sorts, held with high esteem among the Abunali Islanders. She was desired by many men, including the likes of Alexium Ariadne, who wanted her hand in marriage. Kade soon put the foolhardy lifestyle behind him in his quest for love. But it would be too late, as by the time he had fully left his crew, Aiore had already accepted Alexium's proposal and had a child with him. Kade was distraught, seeing as he had lost his crew and his woman by the likes of a much more successful man, and he chose to remain on Tabla Pise as Aiore and Alexium went off to pursue a life together. A couple years later, Aiore would return back to her hometown of Morowai, where she would spend time with her family and old friends. To her surprise, she saw that Kade had been working hard to take care of her elderly mother and support the sprouting villages within the community. This seemed to be an entirely different man to the one she once knew, much to her delight. Where Alexium wouldn't bother with getting to know her family, Kade, even through his hardships, went out of his way to stick with them through the thick of it. As much as she loved Alexium, she found herself falling in love with Kade all over again. After having an affair, she became pregnant with a child that Alexium would go on to raise, assuming it was his. For five years, Azra would grow up with her sister Vaenera in Morowai before moving to Phylomina, living a sound life until her guilt-ridden mother eventually came clean to Alexium about the true nature of their situation. Disgusted and full of contempt, Alexium gave Aiore an ultimatum. To either give him the whereabouts of Kade Asher, or to leave Phylomina with Azra entirely. Much to his surprise, Aiore chose to leave. But not expecting her to actually consider this outcome, he refused to let her walk out of their lives, so instead, he cast Azra out of the capital, back to the island from which she was born. After being informed by Aiore that Alexium would be watching their relations closely, Kade knew he couldn't stay in Morowai with Azra and her grandmother. So he made the difficult choice to leave them alone, not being heard from since. Not long after being shipped away from her family in Phylomina, Azra found it rather easy to make new friends in Morowai. And given that she lived with her grandmother, she had a loving family there too. Now being ten years of age, Azra had been allowed to visit her family in Phylomina for the past two years, growing closer and closer to her sister Vaenera. However despite enjoying each other's company, here in Phylomina, Azra had always felt like a guest rather than a part of the family. That very year after Azra had returned home from visitation, Morowai was raided by a band of pirates in an unforeseeable tragedy. Azra's grandmother spied the flags that could be seen from shore several miles out, a familiar sigil, these pirates were associated with Kade Asher. What nobody expected however were the flaming arrows that rained down upon the settlement. As people were slaughtered and buildings burned, Azra was led by her grandmother to hide beneath the floor of their hut, though only she could fit. Azra begged and pleaded for her grandmother to try and squeeze her way into safety too, but Azra was closed in, and told to remain silent. Hours of almost nothing but screams and chaos go by until Azra, in a panic, leaps out of the hideaway. Unable to keep herself composed, she screams for her grandmother, running out into the open while doing so. Her eyes dart erratically as she watches the settlement around her crumble into ashes, the jungle around it catching ablaze and charred corpses lie in the roads. It seems as though an entire culture is being stomped out, but for what reason? The pirates who made landfall are ransacking huts as they go, looking for someone, and killing who they didn't need. A couple of pirates grab Azra and alert the others that they found her. Could they really mean...? One of them pulls out a knife, about to plunge it into her chest before an arrow pierces his head. The other pirate pulls out a sword and is about to charge after the attacker, but he's soon shot dead too. Azra, who now is trembling uncontrollably due to fear stands helplessly as Tibs, holding a crossbow, rushes over to pull her behind a hut. He assures her that he will get her someplace safe, and leads her to a small dinghy before proceeding to set course toward mainland Athura. Kade, having seen the sails of his old crew, had returned to Morowai. He made his stand against Joss Harrow, his old first mate and the captain who'd replaced him, and lost. He was left for the fire, and the ships of the house of Polys, who came late, found him just barely alive. He believed that everyone there was dead, Azra included. In the proceeding months after the raid, Vaenera and the rest of her family assume Azra to be dead, alongside the rest of Morowai's residents. This fueled Vaenera to resent any and all pirates and scoundrels alike, which persists to this day. Presently, Azra still struggles with the events that unfolded on that day, keeping herself busy and refusing to take a moments respite as those memories will only resurface with complacency. From time to time, Vaenera hears rumors that fuel her suspicions that Azra may still be alive after the burning of Morowai. Tell of a young woman with certain distinguishing characteristics being sighted in parts of the continent where such traits are most uncommon. Though, Vaenera can get very emotional whenever an informant brings up the subject of her lost sister, almost to the point of blind rage. She always insists they check if there's a mole on this mystery woman's chin, but as of now, there are always conflicting stories. Azra harbors some resentment toward Vaenera as she truly believed that Vaenera had forgotten about her in favor of living her privileged life of a princess turned queen. And although Azra is aware that everyone carries their own plights and tries to empathize, she struggles with doing so. As a result, she opts to act as if she never even had a sister. Although, after she met Artemis and vaguely told him about Vaenera, he made it a point to remind her during their time in Tibs' Gang. When young, Azra felt unworthy to be around the Phylominan royal family after Alexium learned of the truth. Even Azra was unaware that she was not his child. This feeling of worthlessness has only amplified over the years as she's been living as a nobody for ten years. She feels as if she's too far gone from what her family knew of her all those years ago to show her face again, and fears that they have changed as well.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Abu","u":"wiki/abu.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu","c":"#c9671b","s":"Placeholder Description. Places of Interest: Tan'we Jungle Abuun Wastes Ashla Sanctuary Kashif Trial Encampment Mesu-Nebet River Mandor Canyon Temple of Atta Abunala Firth Settlements: Sukara Sulala Kilan Shur'iq Medjet Djetbri Unico O'iq Wapiti Yren Ruat Nant","b":"Placeholder Description. Places of Interest: Tan'we Jungle Abuun Wastes Ashla Sanctuary Kashif Trial Encampment Mesu-Nebet River Mandor Canyon Temple of Atta Abunala Firth Settlements: Sukara Sulala Kilan Shur'iq Medjet Djetbri Unico O'iq Wapiti Yren Ruat Nantaka Khamu Dunis Bahaad Nepta Pa'iq Jhaqarta (Lost) Qis (Lost) Sundiri (Lost)","n":""},{"t":"Abunala Firth","u":"wiki/abunala-firth.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Places of Interest","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Abuun Wastes","u":"wiki/abuun-wastes.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Places of Interest","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Ashla Sanctuary","u":"wiki/ashla-sanctuary.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Places of Interest","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kashif Trial Encampment","u":"wiki/kashif-trial-encampment.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Places of Interest","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Mesu-Nebet River","u":"wiki/mesu-nebet-river.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Places of Interest","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Nimjabi Palace","u":"wiki/nimjabi-palace.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Places of Interest","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Tan'we Jungle","u":"wiki/tanwe-jungle.html","a":["Tan'we","Enchanted Forest"],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Places of Interest","c":"#c9671b","s":"The Tan'we Jungle, also known as the Enchanted Forest, is a massive tropical forest covering the majority of the southeastern fertile belt of Athura. Its range spans across the Starry Strait and into Ashla Sanctuary. Long ago, the Tan'we ranged as far north as","b":"The Tan'we Jungle, also known as the Enchanted Forest, is a massive tropical forest covering the majority of the southeastern fertile belt of Athura. Its range spans across the Starry Strait and into Ashla Sanctuary. Long ago, the Tan'we ranged as far north as the middle course of Mandor Canyon, which at the time was a great river called the Mes-Mandor. The jungle is the ancient ancestral home of the Abunali, and every atlae descends from the heart of it. The animals which reside in the jungle are greatly diverse, with many species having no other populace outside of the jungle; remaining untouched by the outside world due to the stalwart protection from the traditions of the Abunali and their Kashif. The Abunali has historically been present in the Tan'we for centuries, and presently in 211 ABU, they still inhabit their ancestral lands. Here are a list of their settlements: Kama (Pre-Talvarian Acquisition) Nantaka Bahaad Khamu Netor (Pre-Talvarian Acquisition) Ruat Dunis Here's a (non-comprehensive) list of animals that can be found within the Tan'we. Most of which can be found nowhere else: Dew Vex Tulkai Zarbuck Bush Vex Yris Trampa Garugala Pipi Ytara Labua Tirvaan Khovis Brightworm Here's a (non-comprehensive) list of foliage that can be found within the Tan'we. Most of which can be found nowhere else: Ytara Tree Hapjuk Tree Ucheeberry Vines Marriot Yawa Tree Isp Nectar Pods","n":""},{"t":"Temple of Atta","u":"wiki/temple-of-atta.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Places of Interest","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Bahaad","u":"wiki/bahaad.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Djetbri","u":"wiki/djetbri.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Dunis","u":"wiki/dunis.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"Dunis is a small village nearby the southeastern land bridge that connects Ashla Sanctuary to the mainland of Athura. Being largely known for its quiet and welcoming atmosphere, Dunis often sees its fair share of sojourners looking for a place to rest while ei","b":"Dunis is a small village nearby the southeastern land bridge that connects Ashla Sanctuary to the mainland of Athura. Being largely known for its quiet and welcoming atmosphere, Dunis often sees its fair share of sojourners looking for a place to rest while either on their way to Ashla Sanctuary, or to simply enjoy the hospitality here. Vines are strung with brightworm nests, creating a glow that slowly moves as the worms wriggle within their nests, illuminating the cobblestone paths. On the outskirts, homes are built into the trunks of ytara trees, identical to those seen in Khamu, while more conventional huts populate the village center. Unlike most of the villages in the Tan'we, Dunis wasn't led by a tribunal of elders, but rather a single elder who oversaw all elements of the tribe. The matriarch, Elder A'Te, maintained her position within the village for many decades before eventually dying peacefully in her sleep at the start of 211 ABU. Her closest friend, Shaman Ankti, was given temporary eldership until a successor could be anointed, and this is where we stand presently.","n":""},{"t":"Jhaqarta","u":"wiki/jhaqarta.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Khamu","u":"wiki/khamu.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"The largest village of the Southern Abunali Tribes, situated in the heart of the Tan'we Jungle. Khamu's location has given the Abunali a deep spiritual connection to the forest and its inhabitants. Within their culture, it is believed that the forest is its ow","b":"The largest village of the Southern Abunali Tribes, situated in the heart of the Tan'we Jungle. Khamu's location has given the Abunali a deep spiritual connection to the forest and its inhabitants. Within their culture, it is believed that the forest is its own separate realm that bridges the tangible and other-worldly, where spirits of ancestors and beyond reside. Khamu for many generations has been the stage for the sacred tradition of the Kashif Trials; a rite of passage where the young become brave warriors and protectors of the natural world. The Abunali in Khamu often refuse to abandon their way of life. This nonconformity has helped preserve their ancient traditions and customs, as well as the unique flora and fauna of the Tan'we Jungle. Most of which cannot be found anywhere else. However, this has also made them vulnerable in recent decades, where many of Khamu's largest neighbors had been assimilated into the foreign realm of Talvar. Though, the Abunali aren't known for violence, they have put up decent resistance thanks to the many kashif they raised.","n":""},{"t":"Kilan","u":"wiki/kilan.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"An aspiring city, Kilan is a town located at the fall line of the lower Atlan Mountains, sitting just near the border of Abu. The town is known for its usage of slave labor on the many plantations and buildings in town, due to their extravagant designs and lar","b":"An aspiring city, Kilan is a town located at the fall line of the lower Atlan Mountains, sitting just near the border of Abu. The town is known for its usage of slave labor on the many plantations and buildings in town, due to their extravagant designs and large sizes. This reflects the aspirations of the founding parties, as they strive to become just as prosperous as the neighboring cities of Sulala and Sukara. The town stretches down to the shore of the estuary, where many farms and streams splinter away from the town and become land owned by individual families of the region. The town's location provides a unique opportunity for agriculture and trade, as it allows for the cultivation of crops that thrive in both desert and tropical climates.","n":""},{"t":"Medjet","u":"wiki/medjet.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Nantaka","u":"wiki/nantaka.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Nepta","u":"wiki/nepta.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"O'iq","u":"wiki/oiq.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Qis","u":"wiki/qis.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"An ancient ruined city, terribly overgrown, and a nightmare to traverse. Its overgrowth is what made it an effective hiding place. Ayala and seven other jotuun, Mirak among them, held out here after the Ten-Year War, until Aethel Zuul found them. Years later, ","b":"An ancient ruined city, terribly overgrown, and a nightmare to traverse. Its overgrowth is what made it an effective hiding place. Ayala and seven other jotuun, Mirak among them, held out here after the Ten-Year War, until Aethel Zuul found them. Years later, Catalina Valentyre told Zuul's Hand of it, and the holdouts died. Ayala and Mirak survived.","n":""},{"t":"Ralat","u":"wiki/ralat.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Ruat","u":"wiki/ruat.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Sanala","u":"wiki/sanala.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Shur'iq","u":"wiki/shuriq.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Sukara","u":"wiki/sukara.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"Sukara is a lush and vibrant city, and similar to its sister city, Sulala, both cities are circular in shape and are divided into rings based on social class and economic value. Sukara however, is divided into three rings instead of four. The first and outer r","b":"Sukara is a lush and vibrant city, and similar to its sister city, Sulala, both cities are circular in shape and are divided into rings based on social class and economic value. Sukara however, is divided into three rings instead of four. The first and outer ring is broken up into five islands, two of which are located in the firth between Sukara and Sulala. These two islands are heavily fortified and decorated, having two imposing statues standing guard in front of them. One of the god of skirmish, Rhodite, and the other of the goddess of weather, Phera, prompting both forts to be named after them respectively. The middle ring is for the city's civilians and is the widest of the three rings. This is where most of the residents live and it's known for its beauty and hospitality. The city's inhabitants enjoy a high standard of living and take pride in their city and its culture. Sukaras leaders are seen as approachable and relatable by the people they serve because, unlike Sulala, the Sukaran monarch lives on the same level as the wealthy. The center island, the palace, is open to the civilians with many bridges sprouting from it, connecting it to the middle ring. The palace, while being the home of the monarch, also serves as a monastery for people to conduct prayer and other appropriate activities. The city's location, between the lush jungle and the sparkling waters, provides a unique and breathtaking landscape made even more impressive by the statues posing in the Abunala Firth.","n":""},{"t":"Sulala","u":"wiki/sulala.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"As the capital of Abu, Sulala stands as one of the wealthiest trade nexuses on Athura. The city is defined by its four rings, each a social stratum within itself. Within the outer ring, the largest and most densely populated district, homes double as storefron","b":"As the capital of Abu, Sulala stands as one of the wealthiest trade nexuses on Athura. The city is defined by its four rings, each a social stratum within itself. Within the outer ring, the largest and most densely populated district, homes double as storefronts; storefronts spill into the streets. Clay ovens burn from dawn until deep night. Spiced stews, roasted grains, skewered meats, and sugared fruits perfume the air. Pottery and hammered metalwork are displayed openly, as much an advertisement as a declaration of pride. Prosperity and desperation coexist here. With trade comes opportunity; with opportunity comes theft. Crime is common. Sulaladji guards patrol heavily, especially near the city gates. Enforcement is visible, deliberate, and occasionally brutal. Children run unsupervised through the tight alleys. Ringo porters weave between atlae merchants with loaded carts. The noise never truly stops. Within the second ring, the chaos thins into organization. Inns, clinics, apothecaries, jewelers, and large trade houses operate in stone buildings with proper façades and signage. Contracts are written here. Debts are negotiated. Alliances are forged over tea rather than shouted over bargaining tables. Travelers who can afford comfort rarely sleep in the outer ring. Information brokers and merchant consortia quietly shape Abu’s economy from this layer. This is also where Sulalas influence becomes subtle rather than loud. The third ring contains wide streets, controlled greenery, and a wealthy citizenry. They reside here behind sculpted walls and ornamental gates. Prestigious academies educate the children of merchant dynasties and political elites. Gardens are carefully maintained despite the encroaching desert. Water management systems are hidden beneath marble and tile — a quiet flex of engineering dominance over the Wastes. Crime is nearly nonexistent. Not because morality improves inward, but because protection does. At the center lie the palatial grounds: Yidji Palace, administrative halls, and ceremonial plazas. Access is restricted. Guards do not merely patrol; they perform visibility. Captains and lieutenants conduct marches and training exercises in open courtyards. Discipline is meant to be seen. This ring is less a district and more a statement: order radiates outward from here. Sulala’s design makes it stable, but fragile in a specific way. Revolt would not begin at Yidji Palace. It would begin in the outer ring, where density, economic pressure, and resentment overlap. Should instability ignite, it would move inward like a tightening vice. So as a countermeasure, the gates of each ring gate are engineered to withstand siege. Collapsible bridges span defensive moats. Oiled stones are stockpiled above gate-bays, ready to be ignited and cast downward. Arrow slits line interior corridors to allow defenders to fire into the gate passage itself.","n":""},{"t":"Sundiri","u":"wiki/sundiri.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Unico","u":"wiki/unico.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Wapiti","u":"wiki/wapiti.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Yren","u":"wiki/yren.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Abu/Settlements","c":"#c9671b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Anklan Isles","u":"wiki/anklan-isles.html","a":["Kingdom of the Anklan Isles","Anklan"],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles","c":"#2f8a55","s":"Culturally still Anklan'A, as the Abunali of the isles still refer to it, but their Icosi overlords simplified it into Anklan. Places of Interest: Tabla Pise Bank of Pearls Tsavin's Fortress Kendo's Estate Hot Springs Enclave of the Tongueless Atlan Mountains ","b":"Culturally still Anklan'A, as the Abunali of the isles still refer to it, but their Icosi overlords simplified it into Anklan. Places of Interest: Tabla Pise Bank of Pearls Tsavin's Fortress Kendo's Estate Hot Springs Enclave of the Tongueless Atlan Mountains Temple of Yolotl Temple of Rokiri Settlements: Orsena Demeia Lorence Milena Tuni Mapusu Swe'ata Bota Nawai Kuana Sooloo Morowai (Lost) Jokuri Nedjor Talgata Broto Natika","n":""},{"t":"Anklan'A","u":"wiki/anklana.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles","c":"#2f8a55","s":"The name the Abunali of the isles still use for the Kingdom of the Anklan Isles.","b":"The name the Abunali of the isles still use for the Kingdom of the Anklan Isles.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Bank of Pearls","u":"wiki/bank-of-pearls.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"The Palatial Bank of Orsena, or as it's colloquially known, The Bank of Pearls, is","b":"The Palatial Bank of Orsena, or as it's colloquially known, The Bank of Pearls, is","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Enclave of the Tongueless","u":"wiki/enclave-of-the-tongueless.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Hot Springs","u":"wiki/hot-springs.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Hypatina Palace","u":"wiki/hypatina-palace.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"Placeholder Description.","b":"Placeholder Description.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Kendo's Estate","u":"wiki/kendos-estate.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"Kendo Burinkan's family estate.","b":"Kendo Burinkan's family estate.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Tabla Pise","u":"wiki/tabla-pise.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Temple of Rokiri","u":"wiki/temple-of-rokiri.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Temple of Yolotl","u":"wiki/temple-of-yolotl.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"The Gates of Shakir","u":"wiki/the-gates-of-shakir.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"Embedded into the two exterior walls of Orsena. Composed of bruthil and embellished with specks of jade.","b":"Embedded into the two exterior walls of Orsena. Composed of bruthil and embellished with specks of jade.","n":""},{"t":"Tsavin Castle","u":"wiki/tsavin-castle.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"The castle of the late General Tsavin.","b":"The castle of the late General Tsavin.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Tsavin's Fortress","u":"wiki/tsavins-fortress.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Places of Interest","c":"#2f8a55","s":"The castle of the late General Tsavin.","b":"The castle of the late General Tsavin.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Bota","u":"wiki/bota.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Broto","u":"wiki/broto.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Demeia","u":"wiki/demeia.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Jokuri","u":"wiki/jokuri.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kuana","u":"wiki/kuana.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kuwana","u":"wiki/kuwana.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Lorence","u":"wiki/lorence.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"A settlement of the Anklan Isles, and the home of the house of Polys.","b":"A settlement of the Anklan Isles, and the home of the house of Polys.","n":""},{"t":"Mapusu","u":"wiki/mapusu.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Milena","u":"wiki/milena.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Morowai","u":"wiki/morowai.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"Morowai, a long while ago, was once a bustling and influential town, capital of the nation of Matau with a chieftain known for their status as 'leader of the water'. It was the birthplace of the city of Phylomina (initially named Malu for its secure and safe l","b":"Morowai, a long while ago, was once a bustling and influential town, capital of the nation of Matau with a chieftain known for their status as 'leader of the water'. It was the birthplace of the city of Phylomina (initially named Malu for its secure and safe location), as well as the many villages and towns in Northern Athura, even if most of its previous territory had been absorbed into the Anklan Isles. Unlike most settlements within the Abunali tribes, each of the northern island settlements is governed by a chieftain who holds a higher position of power than the elders due to their isolated location. Despite its significance, Morowai has recently fallen victim to a raid by a large band of pirates, leading to its eventual downfall. However, the town's influence remains strong, and can still be felt in the settlements of Northern Athura.","n":""},{"t":"Natika","u":"wiki/natika.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Nawai","u":"wiki/nawai.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Nedjor","u":"wiki/nedjor.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Orsena","u":"wiki/orsena.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"An extravagant city populated by wealthy merchants and noblemen. The city is jointly ruled by the Bank of Pearls and the Orsena sovereignty, which are both in a perpetual struggle against each other for power. The Civic Guard - which report to the Orsena sover","b":"An extravagant city populated by wealthy merchants and noblemen. The city is jointly ruled by the Bank of Pearls and the Orsena sovereignty, which are both in a perpetual struggle against each other for power. The Civic Guard - which report to the Orsena sovereignty - is responsible for maintaining order within the city and likewise protecting it from external threats. Conversely, the Bank of Pearls holds sway over trade and finance. In any case, Orsena is widely known for its grandeur and peerless wealth. Besides its evident splendor, however, the city is a hub of talented artisans; innumerous people flock to the city annually in search of employment. Resident merchants embraced the opportunity wholly, employing many of them personally, but have also capitalized on it by forming institutions to organize tradesmen in order to present them to visiting merchants as well. The city is organized into two distinctive areas: the Berra District and Elias District. Whilst the Elias District does not compare to its upper class counterpart, it is relatively flawless in comparison to the likes of Suran and other Athuran cities. Its streets are generally well kept and order is maintained by the Civic Guard. The area is notable for its accessibility to the trading port via an elevator hugging the cliff's surface. The Berra District, meanwhile, is responsible for Orsena's reputation, and home to both the Bank of Pearls and Hypatina Palace; in addition, a myriad of wealthy merchants and noblemen reside there inside lavish estates. The Berra District is elevated slightly above the Elias District, and is decorated with a dozen plazas. Foremost amongst these plazas is the Grand Plaza, which stands in front of the Hypatina Palace and is well known for its statues of the first members of the royal family - Orsena's founders. Orsena boasts three primary walls: two on the exterior and one on the interior separating the districts. Constructed by the city's resident artisans, the walls are sturdy and feature elaborate murals. The Gates of Shakir are embedded into the two exterior walls and dominate the city's façade. The second exterior wall is deliberately and noticeably taller than that of the first, and this is paralleled in the Gates of Shakir as well. The Gates of Shakir are composed of bruthil and embellished with specks of jade, reminiscent of their namesake's war-hammer. The gates are considered an engineering marvel, crafted by the many talents of Orsena, due to their incorporation of bruthil. The interior gate, on the other hand, is far less impressive in terms of ingenuity but compensates in sheer expenditure being composed of both gold and belvium. Adjacent to the Golden Gate is the Central Barracks which boasts the office of the Lord Commander of the Civic Guard.","n":""},{"t":"Sooloo","u":"wiki/sooloo.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Swe'ata","u":"wiki/sweata.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Talgata","u":"wiki/talgata.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Tuni","u":"wiki/tuni.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Anklan Isles/Settlements","c":"#2f8a55","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Arkhan","u":"wiki/arkhan.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The heart of its own tribe.","b":"The heart of its own tribe.","n":""},{"t":"Athura","u":"wiki/athura.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A continent of Artisans. Realms: Abu Anklan Isles Bali Matau Talvar Outside the Realms: Jeriicho Arkhan Mehtala Temple of Akken","b":"A continent of Artisans. Realms: Abu Anklan Isles Bali Matau Talvar Outside the Realms: Jeriicho Arkhan Mehtala Temple of Akken","n":""},{"t":"Bali","u":"wiki/bali.html","a":["Bali Desert"],"f":"World/Athura/Bali","c":"#a8860b","s":"The region of Bali is known for its arid climate and sprawling sea of sand, and seems almost desolate at first glance. But within the realm of Athura, there's much interest to be found within the white sands. Balians are atlae that are native to Bali, but othe","b":"The region of Bali is known for its arid climate and sprawling sea of sand, and seems almost desolate at first glance. But within the realm of Athura, there's much interest to be found within the white sands. Balians are atlae that are native to Bali, but other races of folk are native here too such as the Sudr'iq and Sutu'un ringo, as well as grolls like Savanna Grolls and Cave Grolls. Places of Interest: Temple of Khada Mesu Monastery Ruins Mirak's Abode World/Athura/Bali/Places of Interest/Hunter's Lodge Nirvaad's Farm Mandor Canyon Mes-Naila River White Sea Settlements: Suran Una'iq Chairaad Jhansa Khadesh Neferopolis","n":""},{"t":"Banta Gorge","u":"wiki/banta-gorge.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Places of Interest","c":"#a8860b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Hunter's Lodge","u":"wiki/hunters-lodge.html","a":["House of Appateth"],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Places of Interest","c":"#a8860b","s":"Situated just outside the walls of Suran, the House of Appateth rests atop a steep cliff-side with a singular road that ascends the hill and passes by the right side of the lodge. The building's foundation is limestone, with six thick timber support beams on t","b":"Situated just outside the walls of Suran, the House of Appateth rests atop a steep cliff-side with a singular road that ascends the hill and passes by the right side of the lodge. The building's foundation is limestone, with six thick timber support beams on the left and right sides of the building, and four, slimmer timber support beams on the front and back to protect against the tremors and high velocity winds common throughout the area. Whenever damaged, however infrequent due to the original architects thinking long-term, the building undergoes revisions in that specific area so that such damages are unlikely to occur again. Upon entry, you're greeted with the mess hall, where hunters and guild workers commune. Just opposite the entrance, a bar with priceless ales and foods from the most exotic regions of Artisans. On the far sides of the mess hall, you'll find plenty of retired hunters who only show up to get tipsy and cause trouble. Two hallways on either side of the bar lead further back into the building, with four rooms down each hall, primarily for storage and production of food and beverage. Since its construction and into the present, cowardly hunters flee to the lodge when their hunts become too much to handle, hoping that other hunters will aid them in slaying whatever beast they may have angered. In these events, the lodge goes on lockdown as its patrons watch from the inside. As bleak as it may be, it's the code of conduct amongst hunters. Interfering with another's hunt is always frowned upon even if it means saving one's life, as ultimately, it's natural selection if you get picked off.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Mandor Canyon","u":"wiki/mandor-canyon.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Places of Interest","c":"#a8860b","s":"Mandor Canyon is a massive fissure-like canyon that stretches from the western coast to nearly the eastern coast of Athura. The canyon used to be a massive river by the name of Mes-Mandor, which quite simply translates 'Mandor River' in ringlesh. During the ti","b":"Mandor Canyon is a massive fissure-like canyon that stretches from the western coast to nearly the eastern coast of Athura. The canyon used to be a massive river by the name of Mes-Mandor, which quite simply translates 'Mandor River' in ringlesh. During the time of this river hundreds of thousands of years prior, the land was far greener and saw countless civilizations to rise and fall at its shores. The river rather abruptly turned canyon when a disillusioned sect of the jotuun began terraforming using jeokin to raise the river from sea level. Since then, the land grew arid and civilizations started to spring up in other places. However, that isn't to say the canyon is entirely uninhabitable. A great many species of flora and fauna call the canyon their home. Mirak's Abode can be found in the bend of the canyon near Pa'iq, dubbed 'Banta Gorge' by the locals who also found that it provides a variety of ore deposits ripe for mining.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Mes-Naila River","u":"wiki/mes-naila-river.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Places of Interest","c":"#a8860b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Mesu Monastery Ruins","u":"wiki/mesu-monastery-ruins.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Places of Interest","c":"#a8860b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Mirak's Abode","u":"wiki/miraks-abode.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Places of Interest","c":"#a8860b","s":"Mirak's home.","b":"Mirak's home.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Nirvaad's Farm","u":"wiki/nirvaads-farm.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Places of Interest","c":"#a8860b","s":"A small, quaint farm on the bank of the Mes-Naila River. The fields of crop are situated all around the house and even go quite a distance beyond the river, usually taking two days to harvest everything with the two people that live there. Nirvaad, and his lov","b":"A small, quaint farm on the bank of the Mes-Naila River. The fields of crop are situated all around the house and even go quite a distance beyond the river, usually taking two days to harvest everything with the two people that live there. Nirvaad, and his loving wife.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Temple of Khada","u":"wiki/temple-of-khada.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Places of Interest","c":"#a8860b","s":"Temple of Khada | 3 BBU | - Built about a dozen years ago, this temple stands to be one of the largest in all of Athura, although from the surface you wouldn't get this impression. All around the temple you'll find wild yris behave in a domesticated manner as ","b":"Temple of Khada | 3 BBU | - Built about a dozen years ago, this temple stands to be one of the largest in all of Athura, although from the surface you wouldn't get this impression. All around the temple you'll find wild yris behave in a domesticated manner as they've grown comfortable around the jotuun who worship and feed them. There are five subterranean levels beneath the four that stand above ground, the bottom-most three are dedicated tombs for any fallen jotuun. Coming in through the western gateway, you'll see a large garden with a sun dial in the middle and shallow pools of water on both north and southern sides. This area is where you'll most likely see neophytes training. Coming closer to the temple there's a large flight of steps leading into the main hall, where you'll see elaborate murals painted by the local ringo populous, depicting the gods giving the jotuun their power to help protect those who can't protect themselves. (...) Temple of Khada | 173 ABU | - Swallowed by the sands of time, the temple is only accessible through a collapsed ceiling that leads to the top-most floor. Feeling very much like a catacombs, these floors house no dead, or at least they weren't intended to. Littered around the halls and rooms you'll be able to see what became of the jotuun who thought it'd be best to stay and protect their home. The yris population surprisingly decided to stay despite the area having no real prey for them to catch, may their reasoning be spiritual? The west gate is largely flooded with sand covering the pools, sun dial and lush gardens, the steps are only halfway covered with the entrance leading into the main hall being sealed shut by the tall granite door. It seems whatever had killed these jotuun had a strong ire, and they locked themselves in with it. Was that intentional? (...) The temple fell during the Ten-Year War. Mirak, who belonged to it, was away at the time, and came back to the sealed door.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"White Sea","u":"wiki/white-sea.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Places of Interest","c":"#a8860b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Chairaad","u":"wiki/chairaad.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Settlements","c":"#a8860b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Jhansa","u":"wiki/jhansa.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Settlements","c":"#a8860b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Khadesh","u":"wiki/khadesh.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Settlements","c":"#a8860b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Neferopolis","u":"wiki/neferopolis.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Settlements","c":"#a8860b","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Pa'iq","u":"wiki/paiq.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Settlements","c":"#a8860b","s":"A town located in the heart of the Bali Desert. Initially, it was a sprawling city that was constructed when the desert was still lush and green, but over time, as the environment changed, the city wasted away along with it. Pa'iq now stands as one of the last","b":"A town located in the heart of the Bali Desert. Initially, it was a sprawling city that was constructed when the desert was still lush and green, but over time, as the environment changed, the city wasted away along with it. Pa'iq now stands as one of the last major towns remaining in the sea of sand. Despite the harsh conditions, Pa'iq has managed to survive due to the presence of aquifers that surround the town. To make use of them, tall towers were erected to siphon water from the aquifers up to the surface into large tubs for the townsfolk to collect and use as they please. This access to water has allowed the town to sustain a small but resilient population. Currently, the mayor is focused on taking advantage of the trade route between the cities of Suran, Sulala, and recently Trion. The town's location in the heart of the desert makes it an ideal trading post, and the mayor is working to establish Pa'iq as a hub for trade and commerce.","n":""},{"t":"Suran","u":"wiki/suran.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Settlements","c":"#a8860b","s":"A heavily fortified city located on the western coast of Athura. The city is known for its immense walls which are lined with armaments meant to defend against the large and hostile fauna of the Bali Desert, as well as to protect the city from sieges. Within t","b":"A heavily fortified city located on the western coast of Athura. The city is known for its immense walls which are lined with armaments meant to defend against the large and hostile fauna of the Bali Desert, as well as to protect the city from sieges. Within the city, the population is primarily made up of aristocrats and middle-class individuals. With grand buildings, lush foliage lining the elegant boulevards and squares, and fountains dotted around the city. Beneath the streets lie a system of large canals and tunnels connecting the city's moat to the sea, built to protect the city from floods and also used to cache goods and equipment. Just outside the city walls is a shanty town, albeit not officially recognized by the kingdom. This shanty town is home to many of Suran's original families, who have been forced out of the city due to the ever increasing high cost-of-living and the many businesses favoring the wealthy. Presently, as of 211 ABU, Suran has fallen under the control of the Keiran Clan, a highly elite criminal organization based in Bali. The clan came to power after a brutal battle took place all over Suran between the Keiran's and the Dueval's, another criminal organization. During this battle, King Arvaan was killed by the Keiran's while trying to free his city from the warfare. Unfortunately, this enabled the Keiran's to take control of the city, capturing Queen Ai and Prince Amersi in the process. After the Knights of Suran fell, the Keiran's had all the power necessary to achieve victory over the Dueval Clan. Their reward being Suran, and the entire Bali region.","n":""},{"t":"Una'iq","u":"wiki/unaiq.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Bali/Settlements","c":"#a8860b","s":"A town located in the Bali Desert, situated next to an oasis that provides ample water for the local population. The town also rests near an incredibly large cavern, which has been a source of ore deposits for many years, providing a key industry for the town.","b":"A town located in the Bali Desert, situated next to an oasis that provides ample water for the local population. The town also rests near an incredibly large cavern, which has been a source of ore deposits for many years, providing a key industry for the town. However, in recent years, the ore deposits have been running dry, and the town is facing an economic downturn as a result. Despite this, Una'iq is still an important stop for travelers passing through the desert, as it offers cheap accommodations and services. The town is the nearest to the city of Suran and serves as a convenient rest stop for those on the trade route between Suran and other nearby cities. The oasis is also a popular spot for travelers, providing them a chance to take a break from the harsh desert environment. The town is also known for its artisans who craft goods using the natural materials found in the desert. The people of Una'iq make the most of what they have and find ways to thrive in such an unforgiving environment.","n":""},{"t":"Jeriicho","u":"wiki/jeriicho.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The heart of its own tribe.","b":"The heart of its own tribe.","n":""},{"t":"Matau","u":"wiki/matau.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Matau","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"Placeholder Description. Places of Interest: Atlan Mountains Settlements: Phylomina Kampanópolis Khom Sumere Qar (Lost)","b":"Placeholder Description. Places of Interest: Atlan Mountains Settlements: Phylomina Kampanópolis Khom Sumere Qar (Lost)","n":""},{"t":"Atlan Mountains","u":"wiki/atlan-mountains.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Matau/Places of Interest","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Malu Palace","u":"wiki/malu-palace.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Matau/Places of Interest","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"Rests atop the island in the center of the bay of Phylomina. See The Fall of Malu.","b":"Rests atop the island in the center of the bay of Phylomina. See The Fall of Malu.","n":""},{"t":"Kampanópolis","u":"wiki/kampanopolis.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Matau/Settlements","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Khom","u":"wiki/khom.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Matau/Settlements","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Phylomina","u":"wiki/phylomina.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Matau/Settlements","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"The third-largest city in Athura, spanning across the entirety of a bay, with Malu Palace resting atop the island in the center. There are four primary shipyards, enabling the construction and maintenance of fleets, which happens to be what the city is known f","b":"The third-largest city in Athura, spanning across the entirety of a bay, with Malu Palace resting atop the island in the center. There are four primary shipyards, enabling the construction and maintenance of fleets, which happens to be what the city is known for; widely considered to be the most formidable naval military on the continent. Phylomina is home to some of the most ferocious fighters in all of Athura, thanks to the three large amphitheaters that host bloody tournaments where slaves and enlisted volunteers are pit against one another, as well as any beasts the public pay to see. Another distinct landmark within the city is the large citadel. It is the city's primary defensive structure, sitting atop the massive cliff overlooking the city, providing a strategic vantage point for monitoring the sea and the coast, keeping the city safe from any potential invasions. The tall bell towers are scattered throughout the city, chiming as the hours pass so the general populous knows the time at all times. They're also used as watchtowers by the city's soldiers since they provide an excellent view of the surrounding area. The garrison, located just beside the citadel, houses majority of the infantry, and it's situated between the two main entrances from the land so that in case of a land-locked siege, they can respond immediately. The many ports on the bayside are hotspots for the general population, since people can enjoy the warm waters as if they were on a beach due to the sloped ramps that lead into the ocean. The city's economy is based on selling ships to various cities, selling gemstones and ore, selling fruits and other farmed resources, and selling fish. Upper Class: The upper class is comprised of wealthy merchants, shipowners, and members of the nobility. They own the majority of the city's businesses and control a large portion of the city's wealth. They live in luxurious homes and have access to all the finest amenities that the city has to offer. With wealth, comes opportunity to travel, and with this, comes political opinion. Many upper class individuals know their Queen is still quite young, and as such, assume she's gullible. A few individuals with the wealth to pull it off try to influence the Queen's judgement via bribes and other shady practices with varying success depending on how convincing they are. Middle Class: The middle class is made up of skilled artisans, shopkeepers, and merchants. They are relatively well-off and own their own businesses, but they do not have as much wealth or influence as the upper class. They rarely get to see the world outside of the city due to travel on foot being quite tedious and travel via boat being quite expensive. As such, many of them are apolitical; harbor no personal opinions on any matter. Lower Class: The lower class is made up of manual laborers, fishermen, and unskilled workers. They have the least wealth and the least access to the city's amenities. They live in cramped conditions, and many of them struggle to make ends meet. Despite this, they are an important part of the city's economy, and their hard work and dedication are vital to the city's prosperity. Many lower class individuals try and help themselves or their families out of poverty by participating in the fighting pits, where rewards for champions are exceptionally high. Slaves & Servants: The city has a relatively small population of slaves, mostly from neighboring regions, who are used mostly in the city's shipyards and in the construction of the city's infrastructure. The city's laws have a provision for freeing slaves if they have been living in the city for a certain period of time and have not committed any crimes.","n":""},{"t":"Qar","u":"wiki/qar.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Matau/Settlements","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Sumere","u":"wiki/sumere.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Matau/Settlements","c":"#8d2f8a","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Mehtala","u":"wiki/mehtala.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The heart of its own tribe.","b":"The heart of its own tribe.","n":""},{"t":"Blackmaw Mountains","u":"wiki/blackmaw-mountains.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Places of Interest","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"Just west of them lies the seaway of The Blackmaw.","b":"Just west of them lies the seaway of The Blackmaw.","n":""},{"t":"Blackrock Bay","u":"wiki/blackrock-bay.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Places of Interest","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"Connected to the Abunala Sea, where Nirva resides and prevents access to and from the bay. Trion once prospered through trade along it.","b":"Connected to the Abunala Sea, where Nirva resides and prevents access to and from the bay. Trion once prospered through trade along it.","n":""},{"t":"Bo Lao Academy","u":"wiki/bo-lao-academy.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Places of Interest","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"\"The main building of Bo Lao Academy is expansive, with several pillars lining the front entrance and a secondary structure, the library, connected to it via two outdoor halls. The library is as tall as the statue in the center of the campus courtyard, towerin","b":"\"The main building of Bo Lao Academy is expansive, with several pillars lining the front entrance and a secondary structure, the library, connected to it via two outdoor halls. The library is as tall as the statue in the center of the campus courtyard, towering over the trees surrounding it.\" ~ Episode 2 'Of Kings & Fools' - Pg.42","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Buyu Swamp","u":"wiki/buyu-swamp.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Places of Interest","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Greystone","u":"wiki/greystone.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Places of Interest","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"A limestone keep at the peak of the cliff by Trion, built for the Talvos to live within. See The Founding of Trion. King Cedric Talvo and Lady Haileen Ermsburry are entombed next to each other in the crypt beneath it.","b":"A limestone keep at the peak of the cliff by Trion, built for the Talvos to live within. See The Founding of Trion. King Cedric Talvo and Lady Haileen Ermsburry are entombed next to each other in the crypt beneath it.","n":""},{"t":"Shrine of Ka'at","u":"wiki/shrine-of-kaat.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Places of Interest","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"\"Aesthetically, the shrine doesn’t fit in with the rest of the architecture around the city. It resembles the temple Artemis and Azlan stumbled upon hours ago. The artistic designs on the temple's foundation showcase atlae and groll engaged in some sort of cer","b":"\"Aesthetically, the shrine doesn’t fit in with the rest of the architecture around the city. It resembles the temple Artemis and Azlan stumbled upon hours ago. The artistic designs on the temple's foundation showcase atlae and groll engaged in some sort of ceremonial dance. Something that now would seem like pure fiction.\" ~ Episode 2 'Of Kings & Fools' - Pg.42","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Steps of Arrival","u":"wiki/the-steps-of-arrival.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Places of Interest","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"A point of interest in Trion.","b":"A point of interest in Trion.","n":""},{"t":"Trion Dungeon","u":"wiki/trion-dungeon.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Places of Interest","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"A point of interest in Trion.","b":"A point of interest in Trion.","n":""},{"t":"Trion Palace","u":"wiki/trion-palace.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Places of Interest","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Trion Port","u":"wiki/trion-port.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Places of Interest","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"A point of interest in Trion. Left silent by the attacks of Nirva.","b":"A point of interest in Trion. Left silent by the attacks of Nirva.","n":""},{"t":"Gebdjay","u":"wiki/gebdjay.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Settlements","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kama","u":"wiki/kama.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Settlements","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"A small town located just outside of the Tan'we Jungle in Southern Athura. Despite being in such close proximity to the Southern Abunali Tribes, by 211 ABU, the town itself no longer participates in much of their historical traditions after annexation into Tal","b":"A small town located just outside of the Tan'we Jungle in Southern Athura. Despite being in such close proximity to the Southern Abunali Tribes, by 211 ABU, the town itself no longer participates in much of their historical traditions after annexation into Talvar. Surrounded by thick forest, Kama is known for its picturesque lake to its south, surrounded by homesteads and fishing businesses. The lake isn't only a staple of the town, but of the region, being quite popular among the abunali as it's the stage for the Naluwai. An annual event in which the tribes come together to celebrate their relationship with the natural world, more specifically, its aquatic side. This event is believed to bring bountiful fish and drinkable water for the future generations to subsist upon. Talvar's newfound claim to Kama has had a significant impact on the native families that lived there. Who have either assimilated to conform to the customs and traditions of Talvar, or moved to neighboring settlements. Lending to a blend of cultures in the town, but also a loss of heritage for some of the original inhabitants.","n":""},{"t":"Netor","u":"wiki/netor.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Settlements","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Novon","u":"wiki/novon.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Settlements","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"A small town located just outside the walls of Trion, formed by Opraxi refugees under the lordship of Lord Hiro Kuroshi (see The Founding of Trion). Despite being so close to one of the wealthiest and most prosperous cities in Athura, Novon is plagued by pover","b":"A small town located just outside the walls of Trion, formed by Opraxi refugees under the lordship of Lord Hiro Kuroshi (see The Founding of Trion). Despite being so close to one of the wealthiest and most prosperous cities in Athura, Novon is plagued by poverty and social unrest. The town is made up of mostly working-class and lower-income families who have been left behind by Trion's success. Many of Novon's residents feel that Trion's prosperity is built on their backs, and that the wealth of the city is not being shared with the rest of the region. They blame the Talvo Royal Family and the city's elite for their economic struggles and lack of opportunities. As a result, Novon has seen frequent protests and riots as residents demand greater economic equality and a fairer distribution of resources. The year the taxes rose, they came to Trion's gate and called for Queen Kota Talvo by her family's name. She didn't come out, and they've never called for her since. Nobody there knows that she pays for the winter grain. The town is also known for its poor living conditions, with many residents living in overcrowded and dilapidated housing. Despite the challenging circumstances, the people of Novon are a resilient and determined community, and they continue to fight for a better future for themselves and their families.","n":""},{"t":"Trion","u":"wiki/trion.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Settlements","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"Trion serves as the capital of the Kingdom of Talvar, one of the youngest Atlae-founded realms on Athura. The city was established by King Cedric Talvo, an Ashelan-born noble exiled from Icos for treason. Derided by his contemporaries as 'The Illegitimate King","b":"Trion serves as the capital of the Kingdom of Talvar, one of the youngest Atlae-founded realms on Athura. The city was established by King Cedric Talvo, an Ashelan-born noble exiled from Icos for treason. Derided by his contemporaries as 'The Illegitimate King', Cedric’s reign nonetheless cemented Trion as a haven for immigrants from Oprax and Ashela seeking refuge from the ongoing war between their nations. It was named after the tallest keep in the Talvos' fallen city of old (see The Founding of Trion). Trion’s architecture and customs bear the imprint of two kingdoms. With Opraxi terraces rising above the coastal cliffs and Ashelan courtyards that open toward mist-laden horizons. The city’s population is almost entirely Atlae, and while the Ashelan and Opraxi descendants still hold some contempt for the other, many families have made peace, which can be felt by the merging of their dialects, traditions, and bloodlines. Icosians view the people of Trion as peculiar, if not downright abhorrent. With their ancestry mixed, foreign customs, and questionable loyalties. Trion is known for blending Opraxi spices with Ashelan dishes — savory flavors combined with fermented grains and sweet fruit marinades. Song and poetry are big cultural exports, and many are performed throughout Athura. Trion once prospered through trade along Blackrock Bay, exporting silks, oils, and steel. However, this prosperity has waned dramatically in recent years. For nearly four decades, trade and travel across Blackrock Bay have been crippled by Nirva, a dra’iq of serpentine ilk. She preyed upon merchant vessels and naval patrols, severing Talvar’s connection to trade partners and leaving Trion Port silent ever since. The city’s economy is slowly collapsing; more and more merchants flee inland every year, fishing guilds have all but dissolved. This has forced Talvar to rely on its mainland settlements, putting its weight onto many businesses and families that can hardly bare the taxation and culture shock. Nirva has become a living curse upon the kingdom. King Onfroi Talvo has spent years appealing to the High King, Alfor Zuul for aid. Though his pleas are dismissed, Alfor viewing Talvar’s suffering as the consequence of its founder’s defiance. Resentment festers among the populace. The people of Trion whisper that the Zuul Union intends to starve Talvar into submission, forcing the once-proud kingdom back under Icosian control. Trion Palace Trion Port The Steps of Arrival Shrine of Ka'at Trion Dungeon Bo Lao Academy Hunter's Guild Outpost","n":""},{"t":"Yamu","u":"wiki/yamu.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar/Settlements","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Talvar","u":"wiki/talvar.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura/Talvar","c":"#2d6fc4","s":"Placeholder Description. Places of Interest: Blackmaw Mountains Buyu Swamp Settlements: Trion Novon Netor Kama Yamu Gebdjay","b":"Placeholder Description. Places of Interest: Blackmaw Mountains Buyu Swamp Settlements: Trion Novon Netor Kama Yamu Gebdjay","n":""},{"t":"Temple of Akken","u":"wiki/temple-of-akken.html","a":[],"f":"World/Athura","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Azkaria","u":"wiki/azkaria.html","a":[],"f":"World","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The capital of Azkassar. See The Fall of Azkassar.","b":"The capital of Azkassar. See The Fall of Azkassar.","n":""},{"t":"Azkassar","u":"wiki/azkassar.html","a":[],"f":"World","c":"#5c66f5","s":"An ancient kingdom, ended by infighting and disaster. It was always real, even if some don't believe in it; the fable is specifically about Lucan and Millera, who aren't proven to have existed. See The Fall of Azkassar, Azkassari and Order of the Ancients.","b":"An ancient kingdom, ended by infighting and disaster. It was always real, even if some don't believe in it; the fable is specifically about Lucan and Millera, who aren't proven to have existed. See The Fall of Azkassar, Azkassari and Order of the Ancients.","n":""},{"t":"Icos","u":"wiki/icos.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos","c":"#5c66f5","s":"A continent of Artisans. Realms: Kingdom of Stade Morgar Oprax Settlements of the Gurtai: Khag Ugyar Zam Gurtuz Tenbet Orlag","b":"A continent of Artisans. Realms: Kingdom of Stade Morgar Oprax Settlements of the Gurtai: Khag Ugyar Zam Gurtuz Tenbet Orlag","n":""},{"t":"Khag Ugyar","u":"wiki/khag-ugyar.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Neutral ground among the nomadic Gurtai tribes of southwestern Icos, belonging to none of them. The Gurtai Horde is sworn here, and it becomes the Horde's seat.","b":"Neutral ground among the nomadic Gurtai tribes of southwestern Icos, belonging to none of them. The Gurtai Horde is sworn here, and it becomes the Horde's seat.","n":""},{"t":"Linnenmoire","u":"wiki/linnenmoire.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The first city-state on the continent of Icos and its capital, the seat of the Kingdom of Linnenmoire, erected by Azkassari survivors who had come west from Ibya. It was Icos' first major city, and the beginning of feudalism there. Its name is a subtle nod tha","b":"The first city-state on the continent of Icos and its capital, the seat of the Kingdom of Linnenmoire, erected by Azkassari survivors who had come west from Ibya. It was Icos' first major city, and the beginning of feudalism there. Its name is a subtle nod that not all of it was won by bloody conquest: the founders mingled with the local tribes, and likely married into some of them. See The Founding of Linnenmoire. It was ruled by King Baesilius Morwurth when it was razed by a dra'iq. Endlemoire was built upon its ruins. See The Cremation of Linnenmoire.","n":""},{"t":"Morgar","u":"wiki/morgar.html","a":["Kingdom of Morgar"],"f":"World/Icos/Morgar","c":"#85603c","s":"The realm above Stade, in the land the Morgari called Morgai. The realm takes its name from its first seat, Morgar, raised in imitation of the rulers down south in Linnenmoire. It took a long while to complete: a walled city atop a hill near the open plains, s","b":"The realm above Stade, in the land the Morgari called Morgai. The realm takes its name from its first seat, Morgar, raised in imitation of the rulers down south in Linnenmoire. It took a long while to complete: a walled city atop a hill near the open plains, some miles south of where Ancrage sits today (see The Seat in the North). The threat of Morgar is what drove the Three Great Princes to form the Commonwealth of Stade. As of 211 ABU: Grand Prince Duke Lord Places of Interest: Tainted Forest Ancrage Port Temple of Manda Settlements: Ancrage Crage Mor","n":""},{"t":"Ancrage Port","u":"wiki/ancrage-port.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Morgar/Places of Interest","c":"#85603c","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Temple of Manda","u":"wiki/temple-of-manda.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Morgar/Places of Interest","c":"#85603c","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"The Vessel","u":"wiki/the-vessel.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Morgar/Places of Interest","c":"#85603c","s":"A spaceship that crashed in the far north. It isn't of this world, and nobody on Artisans can explain it, or what's inside it. The device that Kiro carries came from it. Its pilots were androids, completely shattered in the crash. Some bits of them can't be to","b":"A spaceship that crashed in the far north. It isn't of this world, and nobody on Artisans can explain it, or what's inside it. The device that Kiro carries came from it. Its pilots were androids, completely shattered in the crash. Some bits of them can't be told apart from other ruined parts of the ship, and Alfor assumed that the pieces that are there were either armor or statues. It crashed early in the reign of Anteo Zuul, but wasn't found until late in Aethel Zuul's. Aethel died before he could see it himself. Alfor Zuul was escorted there by an entourage of his courtiers and the Highguard, led by two local hunters of Morgar. He had the hunters killed, not wanting it to be discovered by anyone else. Alfor intends to discover all of its secrets. He wants it relocated to the base of High Sonham, so that his Research Commission can reveal them.","n":""},{"t":"Ancrage","u":"wiki/ancrage.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Morgar/Settlements","c":"#85603c","s":"A city located at the base of the Eastern Ventra cliff-face, surrounded by towering, metal-reinforced walls that were erected after a devastating battle during the Ten-Year War. The city rests in a large crater and is known for the stone spires that stretch ov","b":"A city located at the base of the Eastern Ventra cliff-face, surrounded by towering, metal-reinforced walls that were erected after a devastating battle during the Ten-Year War. The city rests in a large crater and is known for the stone spires that stretch over its infrastructure, remnants of the brutal siege it endured during the war. Ancrage had deep-rooted relations for the jotuun, and their ruler at the time refused to submit to the Zuul Union. During the war, they attempted to hide the jotuun from Zuul's forces, which ultimately led to the city being sieged by Anteo Zuul, and ex-jotuun Augmen. They first breached the city's defenses when Augmen launched stone spires from the surrounding cliff-faces to destroy the battlements lining the tops of the walls. He then swiftly moved in to hunt down the jotuun in hiding while Zuul's forces subjugated those attempting to flee or retaliate. The ensuing battle between Augmen and the jotuun unleashed devastation on the city's infrastructure, leaving it in ruins. Despite the carnage caused by the battle, Ancrage has since rebuilt and has become a symbol of resilience. The city has erected a larger wall than the previous one, with interior mounted ballistas in a grid-like organization across the entire wall. The spires that once aided in the siege against Ancrage have been incorporated into the city's design, with paths and interiors carved into them, with the largest spire being turned into a monument that honors the many families lost in the war. Ancrage now attracts merchants and travelers from all over Artisans. Its location, blacksmithing, and strong military and naval presence make it an important city for trade and civil protection.","n":""},{"t":"Crage","u":"wiki/crage.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Morgar/Settlements","c":"#85603c","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Mor","u":"wiki/mor.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Morgar/Settlements","c":"#85603c","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Oprax","u":"wiki/oprax.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax","c":"#b3332e","s":"Oprax sits in the fertile basin of southern Icos, once home to the Gurtai (see The Peoples of Old Icos). It shaped into a feudal government over generations, with several Ōkimi crowned at any given time, until they eventually crowned a Tennō to rule over the o","b":"Oprax sits in the fertile basin of southern Icos, once home to the Gurtai (see The Peoples of Old Icos). It shaped into a feudal government over generations, with several Ōkimi crowned at any given time, until they eventually crowned a Tennō to rule over the once-kings of the land. Oprax is now an empire, though its people don't directly call it such, as they don't use imperial terminology. Its empress is known as 'the Blood Empress' all across Artisans, but within Oprax, such a name results in death to those who speak it. Some Opraxi carry Gurtai blood. The names of Opraxi settlements speak to their origins or purposes. Opraxi culture is extremely honor bound, so much so that lords will commit seppuku to preserve or restore honor, avoid capture, atone for offenses, or as a formal capital punishment. A northern lord wouldn't be let past the wall, at least not without being thrown in chains. Present-day Oprax is a direct result of a centuries-long internal struggle. Its government changed shape in several ways, chief among them becoming less bureaucratic and more war-like, shifting toward hereditary clan leadership and the rule of warriors. It was a victory for the rebel clans beneath the Tennō, who wanted a less bureaucratic state. Many noble lineages were targeted by rival clans, pulled from their homes, and executed because of long-standing feuds that could never be settled diplomatically. The Tennō himself was merely a puppet to his advisor, who in effect held more power than the Tennō ever could. Unfortunately, the Tennō paid the price of being the figurehead. The martial clans assumed he was the leader of the opposition, and when he lost, he was executed by the rebel daimyo. The advisor attempted to play the daimyo's side, and for a while he did, ratting out old loyalists and bending the knee. But the daimyo was no fool. If the advisor had his strings in nearly every castle, he was a threat that needed to go. At his coronation, the new Tennō turned to the advisor and sentenced him to death for his crimes against the old Tennō. He cited treason, disloyalty to his liege, and dishonor to his clan, among other crimes. The advisor attempted to plead for forgiveness, but no man surrounding them faltered. The begging was an insult they had to bear. A man from a lesser clan, the Honda, stepped forward and handed the new Tennō his sword, with which he cut down the advisor and held the bloodied blade high. It was the start of a new age for Oprax, one where dishonor is met with steel and blood. The new Tennō raised a new castle further south on an island, Shinuhokori Fortress. The empress is his granddaughter. Many civilians, clans, and bureaucrats fled the chaos by several desperate methods. They built makeshift ladders to scale the walls, and even risked the harsher climates of the Kintari Mountains. They meticulously carved thin escape passages through the walls, covering them with thatch and leaves to avoid detection by nearby patrols. Lord Hiro Kuroshi was among the refugees of this era. The advisor had given his name to clan Honda, a rival with an old feud against his house, knowing it would come for him. Hiro succeeded in saving his family, and refused ever to return. The refugees weren't safe anywhere in Icos. Opraxi were targeted by stadians, many of whom had lost family in Anteo Zuul's fruitless war with Oprax, and they couldn't go anywhere near Oprax itself. The west was still open to them, but the hilltribes held prejudices of their own, and many refugees were looked at with hostility. Gideon and Cedric Talvo, with Hiro, brought refugees across the sea to Athura, so that they might live peacefully in new lands. Oprax is exceptionally easy to defend. A defensive wall spans around the entire basin, and the border of Oprax lines up with its perimeter. Both the western and northern mountain passes are quite narrow to squeeze armies and siege engines through, and the northern passes' paths run along steep drops into the rapids below. There's very little an offensive force can do to effectively penetrate these defenses, unless it uses naval warfare to attack from the south, which means sailing through The Blackmaw or the Abunala Sea. Both are particularly dangerous for a navy. Oprax refused to join the Bicontinental Union, and Anteo Zuul tried to force it. By the time he rose to the High Sovereignty, the wall was complete, and Oprax simply waited him out. The defeat was exceptionally embarrassing for the High King, and agitated him to no end. Worse still, his predecessor had a victory over Oprax that he did not. Ōnari and Kabejō Garrison were once Opraxi, but Oprax was unable to strategically supply the garrison for either settlement, and they were lost to Stade when Tarris Kalmere was still its ruler, before rising to High Sovereignty. That alone was enough to enrage Anteo. Oprax has since grown confident, increasing its raids on stadian settlements, and even training captains and navigators to specifically maneuver through the Blackmaw. It doesn't have an entire navy by 211 ABU, but it has a decently capable naval fighting force. Places of Interest: Kintari Mountains Shi-Se Mountains Ito Lake Kabejō Castle Kohan Castle Mukaikumo Castle Sekijō Castle Takaoka Castle Settlements: Shinuhokori Fortress Biwako Eikyō Isomura Kumoori Nōka Sekitō Torinokori Toyohara Yamamura Yanebori Zousen","n":""},{"t":"Ito Lake","u":"wiki/ito-lake.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Places of Interest","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kabejō Castle","u":"wiki/kabejo-castle.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Places of Interest","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kintari Mountains","u":"wiki/kintari-mountains.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Places of Interest","c":"#b3332e","s":"On their other side lies the west, home of the Gurtai.","b":"On their other side lies the west, home of the Gurtai.","n":""},{"t":"Kohan Castle","u":"wiki/kohan-castle.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Places of Interest","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Mukaikumo Castle","u":"wiki/mukaikumo-castle.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Places of Interest","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Sekijō Castle","u":"wiki/sekijo-castle.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Places of Interest","c":"#b3332e","s":"A wall watching the western pass, beyond which live the Gurtai.","b":"A wall watching the western pass, beyond which live the Gurtai.","n":""},{"t":"Shi-Se Mountains","u":"wiki/shi-se-mountains.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Places of Interest","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Takaoka Castle","u":"wiki/takaoka-castle.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Places of Interest","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Banjook","u":"wiki/banjook.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Biwako","u":"wiki/biwako.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Eikyō","u":"wiki/eikyo.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Isomura","u":"wiki/isomura.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kenshu","u":"wiki/kenshu.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Konshi","u":"wiki/konshi.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kumoori","u":"wiki/kumoori.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Norudai","u":"wiki/norudai.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Nōka","u":"wiki/noka.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Olira","u":"wiki/olira.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Raiwoo","u":"wiki/raiwoo.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Sekitō","u":"wiki/sekito.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Shinuhokori Fortress","u":"wiki/shinuhokori-fortress.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"The castle raised on an island in the south of Oprax by the rebel daimyo who became Tennō (see Internal Struggle). It was built as tall as it is elaborate, sitting upon a sea rock several hundred feet above the crashing waves below. The fortress has natural de","b":"The castle raised on an island in the south of Oprax by the rebel daimyo who became Tennō (see Internal Struggle). It was built as tall as it is elaborate, sitting upon a sea rock several hundred feet above the crashing waves below. The fortress has natural defenses, as well as a long bridge that exposes any rival clan's army that dares to march on it, if they'd even get past the armies on the mainland.","n":""},{"t":"Shinuya","u":"wiki/shinuya.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Talai","u":"wiki/talai.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Torinokori","u":"wiki/torinokori.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Toyohara","u":"wiki/toyohara.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Valai","u":"wiki/valai.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Yamamura","u":"wiki/yamamura.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Yanebori","u":"wiki/yanebori.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Zousen","u":"wiki/zousen.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Oprax/Settlements","c":"#b3332e","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Orlag","u":"wiki/orlag.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The heart of The Shume, a nomadic Gurtai tribe of southwestern Icos.","b":"The heart of The Shume, a nomadic Gurtai tribe of southwestern Icos.","n":""},{"t":"Ashela","u":"wiki/ashela.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"What is now the Kingdom of Stade.","b":"What is now the Kingdom of Stade.","n":""},{"t":"Ibya","u":"wiki/ibya.html","a":["Ibyan Realm"],"f":"World/Icos/Stade","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The first major power on Icos, and the homeland of the Ibyans, from whom modern icosi descend. It spanned the Ibyan Isles (Pysos, Pavilia, Masseos, Erseos and Thena), most of the eastern coast and the minor islands around them, and later founded a settlement o","b":"The first major power on Icos, and the homeland of the Ibyans, from whom modern icosi descend. It spanned the Ibyan Isles (Pysos, Pavilia, Masseos, Erseos and Thena), most of the eastern coast and the minor islands around them, and later founded a settlement on the mainland that became Acrenon. Ibya had no king and no single ruler. Its landholders, the strategoi, each governed a nome, and together they sat in a senate. See The Ibyans. To compete with the other realms, Ibya had to reform its governance centuries before 211 ABU. It took up the feudal ranks of its neighbours, and the greatest city-state holder, at Pavilia, was made Prince over the rest; the senate didn't survive it. The reform is what let Ibya join the Commonwealth of Stade as a principality rather than be swallowed. As of 211 ABU, Ibya is one of the three principalities of Stade, held by Prince Theron Damaris of Pavilia. When Azkassar fell, some of its noble families took refuge in Ibya, before going west to found Linnenmoire.","n":""},{"t":"Kingdom of Stade","u":"wiki/kingdom-of-stade.html","a":["Commonwealth of Stade","Stadian Commonwealth"],"f":"World/Icos/Stade","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The realm above Oprax, encompassing what used to be Ashela, and the region of Upror in the west, where the Kingdom of Tuvar once stood. Its current ruler is King Lanis Kalmere, of the Kalmere Royal Family. See Houses of Stade. The people of Western Icos, withi","b":"The realm above Oprax, encompassing what used to be Ashela, and the region of Upror in the west, where the Kingdom of Tuvar once stood. Its current ruler is King Lanis Kalmere, of the Kalmere Royal Family. See Houses of Stade. The people of Western Icos, within the region that used to be Upror, are comprised of primitive hilltribes and wildmen compared to the more developed east. They technically aren't part of the kingdom, but their lands are technically owned by the king of Stade by stadian law. After The Cremation of Linnenmoire, the surviving rulers of Icos made petty kingdoms and princedoms of their realms, each claiming the primacy of Linnenmoire (see The Petty Realms). Eventually, faced with the threat of Morgar to the north, the Three Great Princes banded together and formed the Commonwealth of Stade, named for the high hill where they negotiated its creation. Vowing to prevent the past from repeating itself, they enshrined in law that supreme authority would never be vested in a single bloodline or seat of power. So the station of Grand Prince was born, elected by the newly made Lord-Electors, a title held and inherited by the rulers of the founding states. Over time, Stade grew to encompass much of the territory once ruled by the Kingdom of Linnenmoire, by diplomatically integrating its neighbouring realms. As of 211 ABU: King. Owing direct allegiance to the king, many of them with sworn vassals of their own:Archduke of Tuvar, held by the Zuuls. Prince, one for each principality:Moirach, held by the Larrensires, the region around Endlemoire, encompassing Endletonn, Lortonn, Norvania, Bogwatch, Moirlander's Respite and Wietmoire. Karlem, held by the Kalmeres, the region around Karlaine, encompassing every settlement not within the others. Ibya, held by the Damaris of Pavilia; Prince Theron Damaris as of 211 ABU. Duke. Only one exists, holding Ōnari and Kabejō Garrison. A duke is sworn only to the king, never to the archduke or a prince. Count, a modest number, about five or six to a principality. Lord-Elector is a separate office, which may be held by any of the above. Had King Lanis Kalmere been born in the electoral era and been elected, he'd have been styled Grand Prince of the Commonwealth, Lord-Elector of the Realm, and Prince of Karlem. Places of Interest: High Sonham Wietmoire Manor Eryas Citadel Mory Raust Dremmel Stade Norvania Lortonn Bogwatch Stillwater Watch Kabejō Garrison Temple of Nolvere Temple of Isi Ibyan Isles Beruun Mountains Ventra Mountains Bolwark Gentani Bog Tainted Forest Settlements: Karlaine Arlene Endlemoire Acrenon Pavilia Wietmoire Kalmor Potterside Endletonn Moirlander's Respite Stillside Pysos Thena Masseos Erseos Ōnari Talvar (Lost)","n":""},{"t":"Beruun Mountains","u":"wiki/beruun-mountains.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Bogwatch","u":"wiki/bogwatch.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The seat of the Crannoch, upon the moors. Along with Moirlander's Respite, it's a key holding to keeping the western tribes away from the more civilized kingdoms in the east, and the same goes vice versa. Armies that walk the moors have to answer to those who ","b":"The seat of the Crannoch, upon the moors. Along with Moirlander's Respite, it's a key holding to keeping the western tribes away from the more civilized kingdoms in the east, and the same goes vice versa. Armies that walk the moors have to answer to those who hold and know them. It's easy to get lost in the fogged forests, and to disappear altogether in the murky waters. The beasts that reside here don't make survival in the moors any easier.","n":""},{"t":"Dremmel","u":"wiki/dremmel.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The seat of the Oristene.","b":"The seat of the Oristene.","n":""},{"t":"Eryas Citadel","u":"wiki/eryas-citadel.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Gentani Bog","u":"wiki/gentani-bog.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Ibyan Isles","u":"wiki/ibyan-isles.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The homeland of the Ibyans, from whom modern icosi descend, and the heart of Ibya, the first major power on Icos. Acrenon Pavilia Pysos Masseos Thena Erseos","b":"The homeland of the Ibyans, from whom modern icosi descend, and the heart of Ibya, the first major power on Icos. Acrenon Pavilia Pysos Masseos Thena Erseos","n":""},{"t":"Kabejō Garrison","u":"wiki/kabejo-garrison.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"Once an Opraxi settlement, now held by Stade. Oprax was unable to strategically supply the garrison, and it was lost alongside Ōnari when Tarris Kalmere was still the ruler of Stade, before rising to High Sovereignty.","b":"Once an Opraxi settlement, now held by Stade. Oprax was unable to strategically supply the garrison, and it was lost alongside Ōnari when Tarris Kalmere was still the ruler of Stade, before rising to High Sovereignty.","n":""},{"t":"Lortonn","u":"wiki/lortonn.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The seat of the Pellard.","b":"The seat of the Pellard.","n":""},{"t":"Mory","u":"wiki/mory.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The seat of the Hallorn.","b":"The seat of the Hallorn.","n":""},{"t":"Norvania","u":"wiki/norvania.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The seat of the Barrentyne.","b":"The seat of the Barrentyne.","n":""},{"t":"Raust","u":"wiki/raust.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The seat of the Vasker.","b":"The seat of the Vasker.","n":""},{"t":"Stade","u":"wiki/stade.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The castle that shares the kingdom's name, on the crossroads below Karlaine. The old seat of the Kalmeres, from before Eryas Citadel was built, kept by a cadet line. The line began with Oriane Kalmere, the elder sister of King Lanis Kalmere, whom Anteo Zuul on","b":"The castle that shares the kingdom's name, on the crossroads below Karlaine. The old seat of the Kalmeres, from before Eryas Citadel was built, kept by a cadet line. The line began with Oriane Kalmere, the elder sister of King Lanis Kalmere, whom Anteo Zuul once wanted for his son, Archon.","n":""},{"t":"Stillwater Watch","u":"wiki/stillwater-watch.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The seat of the Merrowick.","b":"The seat of the Merrowick.","n":""},{"t":"Tainted Forest","u":"wiki/tainted-forest.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Temple of Isi","u":"wiki/temple-of-isi.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A jotuun temple of high prestige, where Ayala trained. The Ten-Year War ended with its siege.","b":"A jotuun temple of high prestige, where Ayala trained. The Ten-Year War ended with its siege.","n":""},{"t":"Temple of Nolvere","u":"wiki/temple-of-nolvere.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Wietmoire Manor","u":"wiki/wietmoire-manor.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"Located in the township of Wietmoire, and held by the High Crown: the Zuuls. See Alfor Zuul. His sister, Aeleina Zuul, keeps it as the Lady of Wietmoire. It was built by the Talvos and was their family home, with a winery. The resources for its construction an","b":"Located in the township of Wietmoire, and held by the High Crown: the Zuuls. See Alfor Zuul. His sister, Aeleina Zuul, keeps it as the Lady of Wietmoire. It was built by the Talvos and was their family home, with a winery. The resources for its construction and for the winery were supplied by lords around the region. The family was later massacred here (see The Massacre at Wietmoire), and Anteo Zuul seized the manor, along with the township the Talvos had been granted when they built it. The Zuuls hold both by right of conquest. It's an open secret that the massacre was his doing. The locals of Wietmoire had watched the Talvos build their home, and some had known them, as had the lords who supplied them. See King Onfroi Talvo.","n":""},{"t":"Acrenon","u":"wiki/acrenon.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A city on the mainland, founded as a settlement of Ibya, that rose and fell several times during the age of The Peoples of Old Icos.","b":"A city on the mainland, founded as a settlement of Ibya, that rose and fell several times during the age of The Peoples of Old Icos.","n":""},{"t":"Arlene","u":"wiki/arlene.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Endlemoir","u":"wiki/endlemoir.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Endlemoire","u":"wiki/endlemoire.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"Built upon the ruins of Linnenmoire by an Azkassari house, after The Cremation of Linnenmoire. The blackened glass of Linnenmoire still decorates its grand structures, though Endlemoire never reached the prosperity Linnenmoire once had. It's the heart of the P","b":"Built upon the ruins of Linnenmoire by an Azkassari house, after The Cremation of Linnenmoire. The blackened glass of Linnenmoire still decorates its grand structures, though Endlemoire never reached the prosperity Linnenmoire once had. It's the heart of the Principality of Moirach (see Titles and Ranks). Most of its noble families are of Azkassari descent, among them the Larrensires, who still rule here as of 211 ABU.","n":""},{"t":"Endletonn","u":"wiki/endletonn.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"Held by the Ermsburrys, under the Larrensires of Endlemoire.","b":"Held by the Ermsburrys, under the Larrensires of Endlemoire.","n":""},{"t":"Erseos","u":"wiki/erseos.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kalmor","u":"wiki/kalmor.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kalmore","u":"wiki/kalmore.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Karlaine","u":"wiki/karlaine.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"Originally a humbly sized town, Karlaine rose to prominence following its acquisition of the Jaraq remains, killed just outside its borders. Within these creatures existed an oil secreted from their piro glands that could burn flesh within a mere instant, and ","b":"Originally a humbly sized town, Karlaine rose to prominence following its acquisition of the Jaraq remains, killed just outside its borders. Within these creatures existed an oil secreted from their piro glands that could burn flesh within a mere instant, and so too melt even the hardest of metals. Demand for its oil soared and Karlaine was the sole proprietor. The booming success was in large part due to the Merchant Guild, who aided in transport of this resource, particularly so when Karlaine had no vessels of its own. Karlaine's sovereignty grew fat and wealthy and declared that his modest residence in the center of the now city was no longer suitable for a king of his stature, and decreed that a grand castle be built in his name. Thus the Eryas Citadel was born. Karlaine was one of the holdings granted away from Linnenmoire before its fall, and its rulers expected that fall before it came. Its dynasty and nobles weren't of Azkassari descent, but a mixture of Ibyan and Moirish. After The Cremation of Linnenmoire, it grew into the largest city on Icos, as people migrated there and traffic increased. It's the heart of the Principality of Karlem (see Titles and Ranks).","n":""},{"t":"Masseos","u":"wiki/masseos.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Moirlander's Respite","u":"wiki/moirlanders-respite.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A settlement of the moors, raised by the Crannochs of Bogwatch for anyone passing through who could use a hearth and a meal before continuing on. Its people and theirs are the same. It's where the Crannochs receive any guests who wish to speak with them. Along","b":"A settlement of the moors, raised by the Crannochs of Bogwatch for anyone passing through who could use a hearth and a meal before continuing on. Its people and theirs are the same. It's where the Crannochs receive any guests who wish to speak with them. Along with Bogwatch, it's a key holding to keeping the western tribes and the eastern kingdoms away from each other. It was here that the hilltribes brought Gideon Talvo their feuds in secret. See Crannoch.","n":""},{"t":"Pavilia","u":"wiki/pavilia.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A city of the Ibyan Isles, once part of Ibya. It's the seat of the Damaris, the Princes of Ibya; Prince Theron Damaris holds it as of 211 ABU.","b":"A city of the Ibyan Isles, once part of Ibya. It's the seat of the Damaris, the Princes of Ibya; Prince Theron Damaris holds it as of 211 ABU.","n":""},{"t":"Potterside","u":"wiki/potterside.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Pysos","u":"wiki/pysos.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Stillside","u":"wiki/stillside.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Thena","u":"wiki/thena.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Wietmoire","u":"wiki/wietmoire.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"The township where Wietmoire Manor stands. The monarchs of the Bicontinental Union gather here each year, before the Highguard escort them to the Royal Conclave. It lies within the Principality of Moirach (see Titles and Ranks). Wietmoire was granted to the Ta","b":"The township where Wietmoire Manor stands. The monarchs of the Bicontinental Union gather here each year, before the Highguard escort them to the Royal Conclave. It lies within the Principality of Moirach (see Titles and Ranks). Wietmoire was granted to the Talvos when they built the manor. After The Massacre at Wietmoire, Anteo Zuul took the township along with the manor, and the Zuuls have kept it lawfully since, by right of conquest. As of 211 ABU it's held by Aeleina Zuul, the Lady of Wietmoire. Because it lies within Moirach, the Zuuls owe the Prince of Moirach, Severin Larrensire, fealty for Wietmoire. The house of the High King and the Archdukes of Tuvar, answering to a prince for a township, makes it a sore point between the Zuuls and the Larrensires.","n":""},{"t":"Ōnari","u":"wiki/onari.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Stade/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A small town located near the southern borders of Stade, near the neighboring regions of Upror and Oprax. It was once an Opraxi settlement, lost to Stade alongside Kabejō Garrison when Tarris Kalmere was still the ruler of Stade. Ōnari was built upon a marsh t","b":"A small town located near the southern borders of Stade, near the neighboring regions of Upror and Oprax. It was once an Opraxi settlement, lost to Stade alongside Kabejō Garrison when Tarris Kalmere was still the ruler of Stade. Ōnari was built upon a marsh that, as the years went on, grew larger and larger. Due to its proximity to Oprax, the town has been fortified with a wall surrounding it, and watchtowers, outposts, and patrols are scattered around the perimeter. The town's architecture is heavily influenced by its marshy environment, and due to the plentiful rainfall year-round, most of the buildings are constructed on mud foundations, strengthened by wooden planks, logs, and stones. The town's pathways and roads are designed to act as moats to channel the rainwater away from the buildings. Wooden bridges are placed over the main roads during floods to allow people to traverse the town safely. Ōnari has a strong agricultural and lumber economy, with plenty of farms and mills located outside the town's walls, growing crops that thrive in the wet marshy environment and chopping trees for neighboring settlements looking to purchase planks. The town also has a steady coal mining operation, but agriculture and lumber remains the primary source of income. Additionally, Ōnari facilitates the breeding of yippa, which are used as mounts to pull buoyant wagons that can navigate flooded roads. These animals also make for great clothing that provides excellent insulation.","n":""},{"t":"Tenbet","u":"wiki/tenbet.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The heart of The Hunto, a nomadic Gurtai tribe of southwestern Icos.","b":"The heart of The Hunto, a nomadic Gurtai tribe of southwestern Icos.","n":""},{"t":"Bolwark","u":"wiki/bolwark.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Upror/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"High Sonham","u":"wiki/high-sonham.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Upror/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A place only heard of, as no one in the realms knows its location. It was built in the mountains by the Talvos, and taken by the Zuuls when Talvar fell (see The Fall of Talvar). Home of the Highguard, who escort the monarchs of the Bicontinental Union here for","b":"A place only heard of, as no one in the realms knows its location. It was built in the mountains by the Talvos, and taken by the Zuuls when Talvar fell (see The Fall of Talvar). Home of the Highguard, who escort the monarchs of the Bicontinental Union here for the yearly Royal Conclave. The garden of Endelyn Larrensire lies within a large room that originally meant something sacred to the Talvos. Anteo Zuul allowed it to be used as a garden space. It has painted glass panes and a skylight, where the blinding white light from the Sonham Mountains reflects off of the colored glass, illuminating the room in a beautiful manner. Statues of mourning priestesses line the sides of the room. At the center is a Varrow's Tree, still young, but no less beautiful. The floor of the room is unfinished, with tile stopping just shy of the entrance. This allows the plants to take root indoors, and the room is kept warmer by the subtle heat rising through the ground. Paired with the sunlight and the plants, it has a very faint greenhouse effect. Greenish-blue grass has grown in the sun-kissed portions of the room. It was Endelyn's tradition to kneel before the tree and pray quietly, from the time the garden was granted to her last day. She's entombed underneath it, where she remains untouched. After her death, Anteo visited the garden frequently. Aethel, in his grief, refused to enter the room, and ordered servants to keep it maintained for the memory of his mother. Alfor visits seldomly, to quiet his mind, though he doesn't tend its plants. The room is where the dead lay. When High Sonham was a refuge (see The Fall of Talvar), the Talvos buried their own there beside the hilltribes' dead, and the floor was never finished because it was never meant to be walked on. Endelyn's tree grows out of them, and she was laid among them. Anteo didn't know. He figured the Talvos were never able to finish flooring it, and thought the statues peculiar decor. Endelyn was mortified to find the dead beneath the dirt when she first planted the tree. Instead of screaming and running to tell Anteo, she knelt. It's them she prayed for. The servants who dug her tomb found the dead, and understood what she'd been kneeling to. They buried her among them and kept quiet, as she had.","n":""},{"t":"Ventra Mountains","u":"wiki/ventra-mountains.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Upror/Places of Interest","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Almari","u":"wiki/almari.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Upror/Settlements","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Bolvar","u":"wiki/bolvar.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Upror/Settlements","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kinmore","u":"wiki/kinmore.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Upror/Settlements","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Kuvar","u":"wiki/kuvar.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Upror/Settlements","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":""},{"t":"Talvar","u":"wiki/talvar-world-icos-upror-settlements.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Upror/Settlements","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A city-state on the sea cliffs beyond the steppe, and the seat of the Talvos when they were kings in the west, founded by Oswulf Talvo. Its tallest keep gave Trion its name. It was destroyed by a dra'iq, and half of it fell into the sea. See The Fall of Talvar","b":"A city-state on the sea cliffs beyond the steppe, and the seat of the Talvos when they were kings in the west, founded by Oswulf Talvo. Its tallest keep gave Trion its name. It was destroyed by a dra'iq, and half of it fell into the sea. See The Fall of Talvar.","n":""},{"t":"Tuvar","u":"wiki/tuvar.html","a":["Kingdom of Tuvar"],"f":"World/Icos/Upror","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The kingdom of the Talvos in Upror, the land of the Ror in western Icos. Its seat was the city-state of Talvar. A Talvo was only king of Tuvar while the hilltribes brought him their feuds; see The Crown in the West. The crown was annulled after The Fall of Tal","b":"The kingdom of the Talvos in Upror, the land of the Ror in western Icos. Its seat was the city-state of Talvar. A Talvo was only king of Tuvar while the hilltribes brought him their feuds; see The Crown in the West. The crown was annulled after The Fall of Talvar, and its lands are now owned by the king of Stade by stadian law.","n":""},{"t":"Upror","u":"wiki/upror.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos/Upror","c":"#7a4fc0","s":"A region of Western Icos now encompassed by the Kingdom of Stade. Its people are comprised of primitive hilltribes and wildmen compared to the more developed east. They technically aren't part of the kingdom, but their lands, which used to be the lands of the ","b":"A region of Western Icos now encompassed by the Kingdom of Stade. Its people are comprised of primitive hilltribes and wildmen compared to the more developed east. They technically aren't part of the kingdom, but their lands, which used to be the lands of the Talvar City-State, are technically owned by the king of Stade by stadian law. The Zuuls, made Dukes of Tuvar after the kingdom the Talvos held here, are its Archdukes as of 211 ABU. The hilltribes have no word for king, but several of the Talvos once proved worthy of being called one. After The Fall of Talvar, the Kalmeres and Zuuls warred with the hilltribes to keep them weak. Places of Interest: High Sonham Ventra Mountains Bolwark Gentani Bog Tainted Forest Settlements: Talvar Kuvar Bolvar Kinmore Almari","n":""},{"t":"Zam Gurtuz","u":"wiki/zam-gurtuz.html","a":[],"f":"World/Icos","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The heart of The Tughai, a nomadic Gurtai tribe of southwestern Icos.","b":"The heart of The Tughai, a nomadic Gurtai tribe of southwestern Icos.","n":""},{"t":"Namona Pise","u":"wiki/namona-pise.html","a":[],"f":"World","c":"#5c66f5","s":"TBD TBD","b":"TBD TBD","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Rone Pise","u":"wiki/rone-pise.html","a":[],"f":"World","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Sommerset Isles","u":"wiki/sommerset-isles.html","a":[],"f":"World","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Inhabited by the dra'iq Scylla.","b":"Inhabited by the dra'iq Scylla.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"Starry Strait","u":"wiki/starry-strait.html","a":[],"f":"World","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The Tan'we Jungle spans across it and into Ashla Sanctuary.","b":"The Tan'we Jungle spans across it and into Ashla Sanctuary.","n":""},{"t":"Temple of Tii","u":"wiki/temple-of-tii.html","a":[],"f":"World","c":"#5c66f5","s":"","b":"","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Blackmaw","u":"wiki/the-blackmaw.html","a":["Blackmaw"],"f":"World","c":"#5c66f5","s":"The narrow, rocky seaway just west of the Blackmaw Mountains, separate from the mountains themselves. It can be sailed through, but it requires experienced captains and navigators. Oprax trains its own to maneuver through it.","b":"The narrow, rocky seaway just west of the Blackmaw Mountains, separate from the mountains themselves. It can be sailed through, but it requires experienced captains and navigators. Oprax trains its own to maneuver through it.","n":"(Uncategorized)"},{"t":"The Seas","u":"wiki/the-seas.html","a":[],"f":"World","c":"#5c66f5","s":"Placeholder Description. In present day, it's impossible to sail across without alerting Nirva, who wasn't living there when Anteo Zuul tried to force Oprax into the Bicontinental Union. TBD TBD TBD TBD TBD","b":"Placeholder Description. In present day, it's impossible to sail across without alerting Nirva, who wasn't living there when Anteo Zuul tried to force Oprax into the Bicontinental Union. TBD TBD TBD TBD TBD","n":"(Uncategorized)"}];